/*
 * Pearson Edexcel A-Level Biology A (Salters–Nuffield, 9BN0) — Exam Practice Question Bank
 * Shape: { topicId: { name, questions: [{ q, marks, command, markScheme, diagram? }] } }
 * Topic ids unchanged so topics-config.js still matches.
 *
 * WHY THIS SHAPE. Every item is written as one part of a real structured
 * question, not as a revision flashcard: a stem (an investigation with a results
 * table, an unfamiliar organism or disease, a clinical or ecological scenario)
 * and then the demand, because real papers test application to unfamiliar
 * contexts. About a fifth of items use maths skills (percentage change, rates,
 * statistics, magnification, Hardy–Weinberg, diversity indices), and every topic
 * carries practical items. Multiple-choice items are not in this bank. Items are
 * set in each topic's SNAB storyline, and core practicals are examined in
 * context.
 *
 * TARIFFS. 9BN0 parts are worth 1 to 6 marks with 6-mark extended items. Mix per
 * topic: 15% 1, 28% 2, 26% 3, 15% 4, 8% 5, 8% 6.
 *
 * MARK SCHEMES. Edexcel layout: one point per line with (1), Accept / Do not
 * accept / TE; 6-mark items give indicative content and the split of up to 4
 * marks for content and 2 for linked reasoning.
 *
 * Data marked "written for this practice question" is invented but realistic and
 * every calculation has been checked.
 * Drawing canvas flag (diagram: true) enables the sketch area in practice.html.
 */

const BIOLOGY_EDEXCEL_A_PRACTICE = {
  'S1': {
    name: 'Lifestyle, Health and Risk',
    questions: [
      { q: `The table shows the pressures in the left side of the heart of a healthy adult during one cardiac cycle (data written for this practice question).\n\nTime / s | Left atrium / kPa | Left ventricle / kPa | Aorta / kPa\n0.00 | 1.2 | 0.8 | 10.6\n0.05 | 1.5 | 1.0 | 10.4\n0.10 | 0.9 | 2.5 | 10.2\n0.15 | 0.7 | 11.5 | 11.0\n0.25 | 0.8 | 16.0 | 15.2\n0.35 | 1.0 | 12.0 | 13.5\n0.45 | 1.1 | 1.0 | 12.0\n\nName the valve that closes between 0.05 s and 0.10 s.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• (left) atrioventricular valve / bicuspid valve / mitral valve (1)\nDo not accept tricuspid valve\nDo not accept semilunar valve` },
      { q: `People with lactose intolerance produce too little of the enzyme lactase in their small intestine. Lactase catalyses the hydrolysis of the disaccharide lactose.\n\nName the two monosaccharides produced by this hydrolysis.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• glucose and galactose (1)\nBoth needed for the mark\nAccept α-glucose / β-glucose for glucose\nDo not accept fructose` },
      { q: `Blood plasma is about 90% water. Sodium ions and chloride ions are carried dissolved in the plasma.\n\nGive the property of water molecules that allows these ions to dissolve.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• water molecules are polar / are dipoles / have a slightly negative oxygen and slightly positive hydrogens (1)\nAccept water forms hydrogen bonds / is attracted to charged particles\nIgnore water is a universal solvent` },
      { q: `A student plans to investigate the effect of caffeine on heart rate using the water flea Daphnia, rather than using a vertebrate such as a small fish.\n\nGive one reason why using Daphnia is considered more ethically acceptable.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• Daphnia is an invertebrate with a less developed / simpler nervous system so is less likely to feel pain / be aware of discomfort (1)\nAccept Daphnia are very abundant / can be returned to the pond unharmed afterwards\nIgnore Daphnia are small` },
      { q: `Haemophilia is an inherited condition in which a protein needed in the blood clotting cascade is missing, so the final stage of clot formation is very slow. In this final stage, the enzyme thrombin acts on a soluble plasma protein.\n\nName the soluble plasma protein that is converted by thrombin.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• fibrinogen (1)\nDo not accept fibrin\nDo not accept prothrombin` },
      { q: `A study found that people who ate at least five portions of fruit and vegetables a day had lower rates of cardiovascular disease. The researchers suggested this was because these foods contain dietary antioxidants, which react with free radicals and so reduce damage to the endothelium of arteries.\n\nName one dietary antioxidant.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• vitamin C / ascorbic acid / vitamin E / beta-carotene / flavonoids / polyphenols (1)\nAccept vitamin A\nDo not accept vitamin D / vitamin K` },
      { q: `Glucose, amino acids and urea are all transported in solution in blood plasma.\n\nExplain how the dipole nature of water allows these molecules to be transported in the blood.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• water molecules are polar / dipoles, so are attracted to / form hydrogen bonds with other polar molecules or ions (1)\n• so these molecules dissolve / water acts as a solvent, so they can be carried in solution by mass flow around the body (1)\nAccept hydration shells form around the solutes\nIgnore water is a good solvent without explanation` },
      { q: `A planarian flatworm is about 0.5 mm thick and has no heart or blood vessels. An earthworm is about 5 mm thick and has a heart and a circulatory system.\n\nExplain why the earthworm needs a mass transport system but the flatworm does not.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• the earthworm has a smaller surface area to volume ratio / a greater diffusion distance to its innermost cells (1)\n• so diffusion alone would be too slow to supply oxygen / nutrients to (and remove waste from) all its cells (1)\nAccept converse arguments about the flatworm\nIgnore earthworm is bigger without reference to SA:V or distance` },
      { q: `A man has a mass of 94.5 kg and a height of 1.75 m.\n\nBMI = body mass (kg) ÷ height² (m²)\nBMI 18.5 to 24.9: healthy weight; 25.0 to 29.9: overweight; 30.0 and over: obese\n\nCalculate the man's BMI and deduce his weight category.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 94.5 ÷ 1.75² = 30.9 (1)\nAccept 30.86 / 30.857 / 31\n• obese (1)\nTE: correct category for candidate's calculated BMI` },
      { q: `A man's waist circumference is 101 cm and his hip circumference is 104 cm. A health clinic uses a waist-to-hip ratio above 0.90 in men to indicate increased risk of cardiovascular disease.\n\nCalculate the man's waist-to-hip ratio and comment on what it suggests about his risk.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 101 ÷ 104 = 0.97 (1)\nAccept 0.971 / 0.9712\n• above 0.90, so he has an increased risk of CVD / he has excess abdominal fat / is apple-shaped (1)\nTE for comment based on candidate's value` },
      { q: `An athlete's heart was monitored at rest. One complete cardiac cycle took 0.80 s and the stroke volume was 72 cm³.\n\nCardiac output = stroke volume × heart rate\n\nCalculate the athlete's resting cardiac output. Give your answer in dm³ min⁻¹.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• heart rate = 60 ÷ 0.80 = 75 beats min⁻¹ (1)\n• 72 × 75 = 5400 cm³ min⁻¹ = 5.4 dm³ min⁻¹ (1)\nCorrect answer with no working gains 2 marks\nAllow 1 mark for 5400 (wrong units)` },
      { q: `A newspaper headline read: "Coffee drinkers more likely to have heart attacks." The article was based on a study that found a positive correlation between the number of cups of coffee drunk per day and the incidence of myocardial infarction.\n\nExplain why this study does not show that drinking coffee causes heart attacks.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• a correlation shows only that two variables change together / is not evidence of a causal link (1)\n• the link could be due to another factor / confounding variable e.g. heavy coffee drinkers may be more likely to smoke / be stressed / have a poor diet / take less exercise (1)\nAccept need for a mechanism or a controlled trial to show causation` },
      { q: `In a survey, most people rated their risk of dying in a plane crash as higher than their risk of dying in a car accident, although the measured risk of dying in a car accident per kilometre travelled is far greater.\n\nSuggest two reasons why people's perceived risk differs from the actual risk.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• plane crashes get more media coverage / are more memorable / involve many deaths at once (1)\n• people feel they have control when driving but not when flying (1)\n• car travel is familiar / everyday so the risk is underestimated (1)\n• flying causes more fear / dread / is less understood (1)\n• people do not know / misunderstand the statistics (1)` },
      { q: `A margarine label states that it is "high in polyunsaturated fats" while butter is described as "high in saturated fats".\n\nDescribe the difference between the structure of a saturated fatty acid and an unsaturated fatty acid.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• saturated fatty acids have no carbon–carbon double bonds / only C–C single bonds (in the hydrocarbon chain) (1)\n• unsaturated fatty acids have one or more C=C double bonds, causing kinks / bends in the chain (1)\nAccept saturated: maximum number of hydrogen atoms\nPolyunsaturated: more than one C=C needed if candidate refers to it` },
      { q: `A patient's LDL cholesterol concentration was 4.6 mmol dm⁻³ before treatment. After six months of taking a statin, it was 2.9 mmol dm⁻³.\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in the patient's LDL cholesterol concentration.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 4.6 − 2.9 = 1.7 (1)\n• 1.7 ÷ 4.6 × 100 = 37% (1)\nAccept 36.96 / 37.0\nCorrect answer with no working gains 2 marks` },
      { q: `A student used DCPIP to compare the vitamin C content of fresh orange juice and carrot juice. She added the juice drop by drop from a burette to 1.0 cm³ of blue DCPIP until the blue colour disappeared.\n\nSuggest why it was difficult to judge the end point accurately with these juices, and how she could improve this.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• the colour of the juice masks / alters the colour change / the end point colour is not colourless but the colour of the juice (1)\n• compare against a colour standard / a tube of DCPIP with the juice added in excess / a tube of juice diluted with water against a white tile (1)\nAccept use a colorimeter\nAccept dilute the juice (and allow for dilution in calculations)` },
      { q: `Some fish have a single circulatory system. Mammals have a double circulatory system in which blood passes through the heart twice for each complete circuit of the body.\n\nExplain one advantage of a double circulatory system to a mammal.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• blood returns to the heart after the lungs so it can be pumped at high pressure to the body tissues (1)\n• so oxygen / glucose is delivered quickly / at a high rate to meet the high metabolic rate / for maintaining body temperature (1)\nAccept low pressure to lungs prevents damage to capillaries / tissue fluid build-up in lungs (1) while high pressure is kept for the body (1)` },
      { q: `The table shows the pressures in the left side of the heart of a healthy adult during one cardiac cycle (data written for this practice question).\n\nTime / s | Left atrium / kPa | Left ventricle / kPa | Aorta / kPa\n0.00 | 1.2 | 0.8 | 10.6\n0.05 | 1.5 | 1.0 | 10.4\n0.10 | 0.9 | 2.5 | 10.2\n0.15 | 0.7 | 11.5 | 11.0\n0.25 | 0.8 | 16.0 | 15.2\n0.35 | 1.0 | 12.0 | 13.5\n0.45 | 1.1 | 1.0 | 12.0\n\nUsing the data, explain why blood flows from the left ventricle into the aorta between 0.15 s and 0.25 s but not at 0.35 s.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• between 0.15 s and 0.25 s pressure in the ventricle is higher than in the aorta (e.g. 16.0 vs 15.2 kPa), due to ventricular systole / contraction (1)\n• so the semilunar / aortic valve is open (1)\n• at 0.35 s aortic pressure (13.5 kPa) is higher than ventricular pressure (12.0 kPa) so the semilunar valve closes, preventing backflow (1)\nData quoted at least once for full marks` },
      { q: `A light micrograph of a section through a large artery shows a thin, smooth inner lining of endothelium, a very thick middle layer containing many elastic fibres and smooth muscle, and an outer layer of collagen fibres.\n\nExplain how the elastic fibres and the smooth inner lining are related to the function of this artery.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• elastic fibres stretch when blood is forced in during ventricular systole / at high pressure (1)\n• and recoil between beats / during diastole, which maintains (high) pressure / smooths blood flow (1)\n• smooth endothelium reduces friction / resistance to blood flow (1)\nAccept collagen prevents the artery bursting for 1 mark in place of either point\nDo not accept elastic fibres contract` },
      { q: `Varicose veins are swollen veins in the legs. They develop when the valves in the veins become weakened and do not close properly.\n\nExplain why people with varicose veins often have pooling of blood in their lower legs.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• blood in veins is at low pressure (1)\n• valves normally prevent backflow of blood / ensure blood flows towards the heart (1)\n• blood is squeezed upwards by contraction of surrounding skeletal muscles, so if valves fail blood flows back down / under gravity and collects in the leg veins (1)\nAccept wide lumen / thin walls of veins mean they stretch as blood collects` },
      { q: `An angiogram of a 61-year-old man showed that one of his coronary arteries had its lumen narrowed by 70% due to atheroma. He feels chest pain (angina) when he walks uphill but not at rest.\n\nExplain why he feels chest pain during exercise but not at rest.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• during exercise heart muscle contracts faster / harder so needs more oxygen (for aerobic respiration) (1)\n• the narrowed coronary artery cannot supply enough (oxygenated) blood to the heart muscle (1)\n• so heart muscle respires anaerobically / is starved of oxygen, causing pain (1)\nAccept build-up of lactate causes pain\nAccept at rest the reduced blood supply is sufficient` },
      { q: `Warfarin is an anticoagulant. It reduces the synthesis of prothrombin in the liver.\n\nExplain how warfarin reduces the risk of a blood clot forming in an artery.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• less prothrombin so less thrombin is produced (1)\n• thrombin is the enzyme that converts soluble fibrinogen to insoluble fibrin (1)\n• so fewer fibrin fibres / less of the mesh forms that traps platelets / red blood cells (1)\nAccept warfarin inhibits vitamin K (needed to make clotting factors)` },
      { q: `Starch in bread is digested to maltose by amylase. The enzyme maltase in the small intestine then breaks down maltose.\n\nDescribe how two α-glucose molecules are joined to form maltose and how maltose is broken down by maltase.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• condensation reaction with removal of a molecule of water (1)\n• forming a 1,4 glycosidic bond (between carbon 1 of one glucose and carbon 4 of the other) (1)\n• maltase catalyses hydrolysis: water is added to break the glycosidic bond, releasing two α-glucose molecules (1)\nDo not accept peptide / ester bond` },
      { q: `Triglycerides make up most of the fat stored in adipose tissue.\n\nDescribe how a triglyceride is formed.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• one glycerol and three fatty acids (1)\n• condensation reactions, releasing three molecules of water (1)\n• forming ester bonds (between the –OH of glycerol and the –COOH of each fatty acid) (1)\nDo not accept glycosidic / peptide bonds` },
      { q: `A student counted the heartbeats of Daphnia for 15 s using a microscope. Three different Daphnia were used for each condition (data written for this practice question).\n\nCondition | Beats in 15 s\nPond water (no caffeine) | 62, 58, 65\nCaffeine solution | 78, 74, 80\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in mean heart rate caused by the caffeine solution. Give mean heart rates in beats per minute.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• mean pond water = 61.7 beats per 15 s × 4 = 247 beats min⁻¹ (1)\n• mean caffeine = 77.3 beats per 15 s × 4 = 309 beats min⁻¹ (1)\n• (309.3 − 246.7) ÷ 246.7 × 100 = 25% (1)\nAccept 25.4%\nTE for percentage from candidate's means` },
      { q: `A student used DCPIP to compare the vitamin C content of different brands of orange juice. She added each juice from a burette to blue DCPIP until it just turned colourless.\n\nGive three variables she should control to make the comparison valid.`, marks: 3, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• volume of DCPIP (1)\n• concentration of DCPIP (1)\n• temperature of the juices / store all juices at the same temperature (1)\n• time since opening / exposure to air or light (vitamin C oxidises) (1)\n• same end point / colour judged against same standard (1)\n• same swirling / mixing after each drop (1)\nIgnore amount of juice (dependent variable)` },
      { q: `A cohort study followed 2000 men for 10 years and found that men who ate the most saturated fat had a higher rate of coronary heart disease. All the men were volunteers aged 40–50 from one city, and diet was measured using a questionnaire at the start of the study.\n\nComment on the validity of concluding that a high saturated fat intake increases the risk of coronary heart disease in all adults.`, marks: 3, command: 'Comment on', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• large sample / long time period increases reliability (1)\n• only men / only one age group / one city so cannot be generalised to all adults e.g. women (1)\n• diet questionnaire relies on honesty / memory / diet may change over 10 years (1)\n• other risk factors e.g. smoking / exercise / genetics may not be controlled (1)\n• correlation does not show causation (1)\n• volunteers may not be representative / may be more health conscious (1)` },
      { q: `A woman's daily energy intake from food is 11 200 kJ. Her basal metabolic rate uses 7100 kJ per day and her physical activity uses 2700 kJ per day.\n\nAssume that 1 kg of body fat stores 37 000 kJ of energy.\n\nCalculate the change in her body mass after 30 days if her diet and activity stay the same.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• energy output = 7100 + 2700 = 9800 kJ per day so surplus = 11 200 − 9800 = 1400 kJ per day (1)\n• 1400 × 30 = 42 000 kJ (1)\n• 42 000 ÷ 37 000 = 1.1 kg increase (1)\nAccept 1.14 kg\nMust state or imply an increase for third mark` },
      { q: `A student used DCPIP to measure the vitamin C content of orange juice (data written for this practice question).\n\nFirst, 0.45 cm³ of a 1.0 mg cm⁻³ vitamin C standard solution was needed to decolourise 1.0 cm³ of DCPIP.\n\nThe volumes of orange juice needed to decolourise 1.0 cm³ of DCPIP in four repeats were: 1.9 cm³, 1.7 cm³, 1.8 cm³, 2.6 cm³.\n\nCalculate the mass of vitamin C in 100 cm³ of the orange juice. Show your working.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 2.6 cm³ identified as anomalous and excluded (1)\n• mean = (1.9 + 1.7 + 1.8) ÷ 3 = 1.8 cm³ (1)\n• 1.0 cm³ DCPIP is decolourised by 0.45 mg vitamin C, so concentration = 0.45 ÷ 1.8 = 0.25 mg cm⁻³ (1)\n• 0.25 × 100 = 25 mg (in 100 cm³) (1)\nTE throughout\nIf anomaly included: mean 2.0 cm³ → 22.5 mg gains 3 marks` },
      { q: `A 70-year-old woman with an irregular heartbeat (atrial fibrillation) is at risk of stroke. Her doctor is deciding whether to prescribe warfarin (an anticoagulant) or aspirin (a platelet inhibitor). She also has a history of stomach ulcers.\n\nDiscuss the benefits and risks of these two drugs for this patient.`, marks: 4, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `• both reduce the risk of blood clots / thrombosis forming and so reduce risk of stroke (1)\n• warfarin reduces clotting factors / prothrombin so reduces fibrin formation; aspirin reduces platelets sticking together / aggregating (1)\n• both increase the risk of bleeding / haemorrhage, e.g. internal bleeding or a haemorrhagic stroke (1)\n• aspirin can irritate the stomach / cause bleeding ulcers, which is a particular risk for her history (1)\n• warfarin dose needs careful monitoring / regular blood tests / interacts with other drugs and foods (1)\nMax 4` },
      { q: `A blood test shows a patient has a high concentration of low-density lipoproteins (LDLs) and a low concentration of high-density lipoproteins (HDLs).\n\nExplain why this patient has an increased risk of cardiovascular disease.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• LDLs carry cholesterol from the liver to the tissues / to the blood (1)\n• high LDL increases blood cholesterol which can be deposited in artery walls (1)\n• cholesterol is taken up by macrophages / white blood cells in the artery wall, forming atheroma / plaque (1)\n• HDLs transport cholesterol from the tissues back to the liver (to be removed), so low HDL means less cholesterol is removed (1)\n• atheroma narrows the lumen / increases risk of thrombosis (1)\nMax 4` },
      { q: `A study recorded the number of deaths from coronary heart disease (CHD) per 10 000 men per year in four groups (data written for this practice question).\n\nGroup | CHD deaths per 10 000 per year\nNon-smoker, systolic blood pressure below 140 mmHg | 12\nNon-smoker, systolic blood pressure 140 mmHg or above | 30\nSmoker, systolic blood pressure below 140 mmHg | 28\nSmoker, systolic blood pressure 140 mmHg or above | 70\n\nAnalyse the data to describe how smoking and high blood pressure affect the risk of death from CHD.`, marks: 4, command: 'Analyse', markScheme: `• high blood pressure alone increases risk 2.5 times (30 ÷ 12) (1)\n• smoking alone increases risk 2.3 times (28 ÷ 12) (1)\n• both together increase risk 5.8 times (70 ÷ 12) (1)\n• the combined effect is greater than either alone / the effects multiply / are more than additive (1)\nAccept values as percentage increases (150%, 133%, 483%)\nMax 2 if no calculated values used` },
      { q: `A cardiologist describes the cardiac cycle to a patient as three stages: atrial systole, ventricular systole and cardiac diastole.\n\nDescribe the events that take place in the heart during each of these three stages.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• atrial systole: atria contract, forcing remaining blood into the ventricles through open atrioventricular valves (1)\n• ventricular systole: ventricles contract (from the base / apex upwards), pressure rises above atria so atrioventricular valves close (1)\n• ventricular pressure exceeds that in the aorta / pulmonary artery so semilunar valves open and blood is ejected (1)\n• diastole: heart muscle relaxes, semilunar valves close (as pressure in arteries is higher than in ventricles) and blood flows into the atria / ventricles from the veins (1)\nDo not accept valves contract` },
      { q: `A baby is born with a ventricular septal defect: a hole in the wall (septum) between the left and right ventricles. The baby tires easily during feeding and has a faster heart rate than normal.\n\nExplain why the baby has these symptoms.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• oxygenated blood from the left ventricle mixes with deoxygenated blood in the right ventricle (1)\n• (left ventricle pressure is higher so) some blood that should go to the aorta is pumped back to the lungs / less blood goes to the body (1)\n• so less oxygen is delivered to the tissues for aerobic respiration (1)\n• heart rate increases to maintain cardiac output / oxygen supply; less ATP / energy available so baby tires easily (1)` },
      { q: `A student investigated the effect of caffeine concentration on the heart rate of Daphnia. She used one Daphnia for all concentrations, starting with 0 and increasing the concentration each time. Heart rate was counted three times at each concentration under a microscope with a bright lamp (data written for this practice question).\n\nCaffeine / mg cm⁻³ | Mean heart rate / beats min⁻¹ | Standard deviation\n0.0 | 240 | 18\n0.1 | 252 | 20\n0.2 | 271 | 15\n0.4 | 296 | 22\n0.8 | 301 | 35\n\nShe concluded: "Heart rate increases in direct proportion to caffeine concentration."\n\nEvaluate her conclusion and her method.`, marks: 5, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `• heart rate does increase as caffeine concentration increases, from 240 to 301 beats min⁻¹ (1)\n• not directly proportional: doubling from 0.4 to 0.8 mg cm⁻³ only increases rate by 5 beats min⁻¹ / levels off (1)\n• standard deviations overlap (e.g. 0 and 0.1, or 0.4 and 0.8) so differences may not be significant (1)\n• using the same Daphnia means caffeine may accumulate / effects may carry over / no acclimatisation between solutions (1)\n• lamp may heat the water, so temperature is not controlled and may increase heart rate (1)\n• only one Daphnia / three counts so results may not be representative; repeat with more individuals (1)\nMax 5` },
      { q: `Two studies investigated whether vitamin E supplements reduce coronary heart disease (CHD).\n\nStudy A (observational): 40 000 nurses reported their supplement use. After 8 years, those who took vitamin E supplements had about 40% fewer CHD events than those who did not.\nStudy B (randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial): 9500 people at high risk of CHD were randomly given vitamin E or a placebo. After 4.5 years there was no significant difference in CHD events between the groups.\n(Data written for this practice question.)\n\nEvaluate the evidence that vitamin E supplements reduce the risk of CHD.`, marks: 5, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `• study A suggests a reduced risk / vitamin E is an antioxidant that could reduce free radical damage to endothelium (1)\n• study A shows only a correlation; supplement users may have healthier lifestyles e.g. diet, exercise, not smoking (confounding variables) (1)\n• study A relies on self-reporting / only women / only nurses so may not be representative (1)\n• study B is randomised / placebo-controlled / double-blind so removes bias and controls other variables (1)\n• study B found no significant effect, so the evidence that vitamin E reduces CHD is weak (1)\n• study B used high-risk people / shorter time, so may not apply to the general population / effect may take longer (1)\nMax 5` },
      { q: `Potatoes store carbohydrate as starch, which is made of amylose and amylopectin. Human liver and muscle cells store carbohydrate as glycogen.\n\nCompare the structures of amylose, amylopectin and glycogen and explain how their structures make them suitable as energy stores.`, marks: 5, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `• all are polymers of α-glucose joined by glycosidic bonds (1)\n• amylose is unbranched with only 1,4 glycosidic bonds and coils into a helix, making it compact (1)\n• amylopectin and glycogen are branched with 1,6 as well as 1,4 glycosidic bonds; glycogen is more highly branched (1)\n• branches give many ends for enzymes to hydrolyse, so glucose is released rapidly (suits animals' higher metabolic rate) (1)\n• all are insoluble so do not affect water potential / cause osmosis (1)\n• all are large molecules so cannot leave the cell (1)\nMax 5` },
      { q: `*A 55-year-old man has smoked 20 cigarettes a day for 30 years and has high blood pressure. He has been told he is at high risk of a heart attack (myocardial infarction).\n\nExplain the course of events that could lead from damage to the endothelium of his coronary arteries to a myocardial infarction.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 endothelium damaged by high blood pressure / chemicals from cigarette smoke\nIC2 inflammatory response: white blood cells / macrophages move into the artery wall\nIC3 cholesterol (from LDLs) accumulates; fibrous tissue and calcium salts build up to form an atheroma / plaque\nIC4 artery wall loses elasticity / lumen narrows, raising blood pressure further (positive feedback)\nIC5 plaque ruptures / roughens the endothelium, exposing collagen; platelets stick and a clotting cascade forms a thrombus (thromboplastin, thrombin, fibrin)\nIC6 clot blocks the coronary artery, so heart muscle is starved of oxygen / cannot respire aerobically and dies\n\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 IC → 4; 5–4 IC → 3; 3–2 IC → 2; 1 IC → 1\nReasoning: 2 marks for a sustained, logically sequenced explanation linking each event to the next; 1 mark for partially linked reasoning; 0 for isolated points` },
      { q: `*A 58-year-old woman has a blood pressure of 165/100 mmHg and a total blood cholesterol concentration of 7.2 mmol dm⁻³. She has had a mild stroke caused by a blood clot. She has asthma and drinks alcohol regularly.\n\nHer doctor considers prescribing an antihypertensive, a statin and a platelet inhibitor such as aspirin.\n\nDiscuss the benefits and risks of each of these treatments for this woman.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 antihypertensives (e.g. ACE inhibitors, diuretics, calcium channel blockers, beta blockers) lower blood pressure, reducing endothelial damage and risk of atherosclerosis / further stroke\nIC2 risks of antihypertensives: dizziness / fainting / headaches / palpitations; beta blockers can trigger asthma attacks so are unsuitable for her\nIC3 statins inhibit cholesterol synthesis in the liver, lowering LDL / blood cholesterol and slowing atheroma formation\nIC4 risks of statins: muscle pain / liver damage (greater risk with regular alcohol use) / need regular monitoring\nIC5 aspirin reduces platelet aggregation, so reduces risk of another clot / thrombotic stroke\nIC6 risks of aspirin: bleeding / stomach ulcers / haemorrhagic stroke; may worsen asthma in some people; overall benefits likely outweigh risks given her high risk, with lifestyle changes as well\n\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 IC → 4; 5–4 IC → 3; 3–2 IC → 2; 1 IC → 1\nReasoning: 2 marks for benefits and risks weighed against this patient's specific details; 1 mark for partially linked reasoning; 0 for isolated points` },
      { q: `*Caffeine is a stimulant found in coffee and energy drinks.\n\nDevise an investigation to determine the effect of caffeine concentration on the heart rate of Daphnia. Include ethical considerations in your answer.`, marks: 6, command: 'Devise', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 make a range of caffeine concentrations (at least five, e.g. by serial dilution) plus a control of pond water / 0 caffeine\nIC2 place a Daphnia in a cavity slide / on cotton wool with a small, standard volume of solution and leave to acclimatise for a fixed time\nIC3 count heartbeats under a microscope for a set time (e.g. 15 s, using a tally or stopwatch) and convert to beats per minute\nIC4 control temperature (e.g. use a cold light source / short time under the lamp / thermometer), and the size / age of Daphnia\nIC5 use a different Daphnia for each concentration and repeat (at least three) to calculate a mean / standard deviation; plot a graph of heart rate against concentration\nIC6 ethics: Daphnia are invertebrates with a simple nervous system so are thought to feel less pain; minimise time on the slide / avoid overheating; return them to the pond afterwards\n\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 IC → 4; 5–4 IC → 3; 3–2 IC → 2; 1 IC → 1\nReasoning: 2 marks for a logically sequenced method with justified controls; 1 mark for partially structured method; 0 for isolated points` }
    ]
  },
  'S2': {
    name: 'Genes and Health',
    questions: [
      { q: `The most common mutation causing cystic fibrosis is called ΔF508. In this mutation three nucleotides are lost from the CFTR gene, so the CFTR protein lacks the amino acid phenylalanine at position 508.\n\nName the type of gene mutation that causes ΔF508.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `deletion (1)\nAccept: deletion of a (base) triplet / three bases\nDo not accept: substitution / frameshift / insertion` },
      { q: `A sample of double-stranded DNA was extracted from a cheek cell of a person being tested for cystic fibrosis. Analysis showed that 22% of the bases in the sample were adenine.\n\nCalculate the percentage of bases in this sample that were guanine.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `28 (%) (1)\n[thymine = 22%, so cytosine + guanine = 100 − 44 = 56%, guanine = 56 ÷ 2]\nDo not accept: 22 or 56` },
      { q: `A student investigated the effect of temperature on the permeability of beetroot cell membranes. Discs of beetroot were placed in tubes of distilled water in water baths at different temperatures. After 30 minutes the absorbance of the water around each set of discs was measured using a colorimeter.\n\nGive one variable, other than time, that the student should control in this investigation.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `One from:\nsize / surface area / mass / thickness of the beetroot discs (1)\nnumber of discs per tube (1)\nvolume of distilled water (1)\nsource / age of the beetroot (1)\ncolour of filter / wavelength on the colorimeter (1)\nAccept: washing the discs for the same time before the experiment\nDo not accept: temperature (independent variable) / amount of beetroot without qualification` },
      { q: `In the Meselson–Stahl experiment, bacteria were grown for many generations in a medium containing only heavy nitrogen (¹⁵N). They were then transferred to a medium containing only light nitrogen (¹⁴N) and allowed to divide once. DNA was extracted and centrifuged in a density gradient. A single band was seen, at a density between that of ¹⁵N DNA and ¹⁴N DNA.\n\nState which hypothesis of DNA replication is disproved by this result.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `conservative (replication) (1)\nDo not accept: semi-conservative / dispersive` },
      { q: `During translation of CFTR mRNA, a tRNA molecule carrying the anticodon UAC binds to a codon on the mRNA.\n\nGive the sequence of bases on the DNA template strand that was transcribed to produce this codon.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `TAC (1)\n[anticodon UAC pairs with codon AUG; AUG was transcribed from template TAC]\nDo not accept: UAC / AUG / ATG` },
      { q: `The CFTR protein is a single polypeptide of 1480 amino acids.\n\nCalculate the minimum number of nucleotides in the part of the mRNA that codes for the amino acids of this polypeptide.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `4440 (1)\n[1480 × 3]\nAccept: 4443 (if stop codon included)\nDo not accept: 1480 / 8880` },
      { q: `The alveoli in human lungs are the site of gas exchange. The lungs are ventilated by breathing in and out, and the alveoli are surrounded by a dense network of capillaries through which blood flows continuously.\n\nExplain how ventilation and blood flow increase the rate of diffusion of oxygen into the blood.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `ventilation brings in air with a high concentration of oxygen / removes air with low oxygen (1)\nblood flow carries oxygenated blood away / brings deoxygenated blood (1)\nso a steep concentration gradient (of oxygen) is maintained (between alveolus and blood) (1)\nMax 2\nAccept: rate of diffusion is proportional to the concentration difference (Fick's law)\nIgnore: references to surface area or thin walls` },
      { q: `(Data written for this practice question.)\nIn a lung disease, the total surface area of the alveoli of a patient decreased from 70 m² to 50 m², and the mean thickness of the gas exchange surface increased from 0.5 µm to 0.8 µm. Assume the concentration difference of oxygen is unchanged.\n\nFick's law states that:\nrate of diffusion ∝ (surface area × difference in concentration) ÷ thickness of the exchange surface\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in the rate of diffusion of oxygen in this patient.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `correct ratios: 70 ÷ 0.5 = 140 and 50 ÷ 0.8 = 62.5 (1)\n55.4 (%) (1)\n[(140 − 62.5) ÷ 140 × 100 = 55.36]\nAccept: 55 / 55.36\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2` },
      { q: `Two people, who are both carriers of cystic fibrosis, plan to have children. Cystic fibrosis is caused by a recessive allele of the CFTR gene.\n\nCalculate the probability that their first two children will both have cystic fibrosis.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `probability of one child with cystic fibrosis = 0.25 / ¼ / 25% (1)\n0.0625 / 1/16 / 6.25% (1)\n[0.25 × 0.25]\nAccept: 1 in 16\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2` },
      { q: `The uptake of potassium ions by root hair cells of a plant was measured. When a respiratory inhibitor was added to the solution around the roots, the uptake of potassium ions fell almost to zero, although the concentration of potassium ions outside the cells was lower than inside.\n\nSuggest an explanation for this result.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `potassium ions are taken up by active transport (against a concentration gradient) (1)\ninhibitor stops (aerobic) respiration so no / less ATP is produced (1)\nATP needed for carrier proteins to change shape / for the pump (1)\nMax 2\nDo not accept: energy is produced / made` },
      { q: `A sample of human red blood cells was placed in a 0.3% sodium chloride solution. When viewed with a light microscope after ten minutes, most of the cells had swollen and burst. Blood plasma has a sodium chloride concentration of about 0.9%.\n\nExplain why the red blood cells burst.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `solution has a higher water potential than the cytoplasm / is hypotonic (1)\nwater enters the cells by osmosis (through the partially permeable membrane) (1)\nred blood cells have no cell wall to resist expansion (1)\nMax 2\nAccept: water moves down a water potential gradient\nDo not accept: water concentration / salt moves out of the cell` },
      { q: `(Data written for this practice question.)\nA student investigated the effect of temperature on beetroot cell membranes. The absorbance of the water surrounding beetroot discs after 30 minutes is shown below.\n\nTemperature / °C — Absorbance\n20 — 0.08\n30 — 0.10\n40 — 0.14\n50 — 0.42\n60 — 0.86\n70 — 0.92\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in absorbance between 40 °C and 60 °C.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `0.86 − 0.14 = 0.72 (1)\n514 (%) (1)\n[0.72 ÷ 0.14 × 100 = 514.3]\nAccept: 510 / 514.3\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2` },
      { q: `An experimental treatment for cystic fibrosis involves patients inhaling mRNA that carries the code for a functional CFTR protein. The mRNA is taken up by the epithelial cells lining the airways.\n\nGive two differences between the structure of an mRNA molecule and the structure of a DNA molecule.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `mRNA is single-stranded but DNA is double-stranded (1)\nmRNA contains ribose but DNA contains deoxyribose (1)\nmRNA contains uracil but DNA contains thymine (1)\nmRNA is shorter / fewer nucleotides (1)\nmRNA not a double helix / no hydrogen bonds between strands (1)\nMax 2\nComparative statements needed for each mark\nDo not accept: uracil replaces thymine in DNA` },
      { q: `A point mutation in a gene changed the DNA triplet CTT to CTC. Analysis of the protein produced showed that its amino acid sequence was unchanged.\n\nExplain why this mutation had no effect on the amino acid sequence.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the genetic code is degenerate (1)\nboth triplets / codons code for the same amino acid (1)\nAccept: more than one triplet codes for an amino acid\nIgnore: references to non-overlapping` },
      { q: `A sweat test is used to diagnose cystic fibrosis. A chloride ion concentration in sweat of 60 mmol dm⁻³ or more indicates cystic fibrosis. In the ducts of sweat glands, the CFTR protein normally allows chloride ions to move from the sweat into the epithelial cells lining the duct.\n\nSuggest why people with cystic fibrosis have a high concentration of chloride ions in their sweat.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `CFTR (channel) protein is not functional / not present in the cell surface membrane (1)\nchloride ions are not reabsorbed from the sweat (into the duct cells) (1)\nAccept: sodium ions also not reabsorbed\nDo not accept: chloride ions pumped into sweat` },
      { q: `Goblet cells in the epithelium of the airways secrete mucin, a glycoprotein, into the mucus. Mucin is packaged into vesicles by the Golgi apparatus.\n\nDescribe how mucin is released from a goblet cell.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `(by) exocytosis (1)\nvesicles move to / fuse with the cell surface membrane (1)\ncontents released outside the cell (1)\nMax 2\nAccept: vesicles moved along microtubules\nDo not accept: endocytosis / diffusion` },
      { q: `A pregnant woman whose first child has cystic fibrosis is offered a prenatal test. She can choose chorionic villus sampling (CVS), carried out at 11–14 weeks of pregnancy, or amniocentesis, carried out at 15–20 weeks.\n\nGive two reasons why she might choose chorionic villus sampling rather than amniocentesis.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `CVS can be carried out earlier in pregnancy (1)\nresults available sooner / less waiting time / less anxiety (1)\nif termination is chosen, it can be earlier (which is safer / less distressing) (1)\nCVS sample contains many fetal cells so cells do not need to be cultured / results are faster (1)\nMax 2\nIgnore: references to miscarriage risk unless correctly argued` },
      { q: `In people without cystic fibrosis, CFTR proteins in the epithelial cells lining the airways move chloride ions out of the cells into the mucus. Sodium ions follow, and water moves into the mucus.\n\nExplain why people with cystic fibrosis have thick, sticky mucus in their airways.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `CFTR protein is not functional so chloride ions are not moved out of cells (into the mucus) (1)\nsodium ions continue to be absorbed into the epithelial cells (1)\nwater potential of the cells is lower than that of the mucus (1)\nwater moves out of the mucus into the cells by osmosis (1)\nso mucus is dehydrated / has less water (1)\nMax 3\nAccept: water does not move into the mucus by osmosis\nDo not accept: water concentration` },
      { q: `About 98% of men with cystic fibrosis are infertile, and fertility is also reduced in many women with cystic fibrosis.\n\nExplain why both men and women with cystic fibrosis may have reduced fertility.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(in men) the vas deferens / sperm duct is blocked by mucus / is absent (1)\nso sperm cannot leave the testes / fewer sperm in the semen (1)\n(in women) thick / sticky mucus at the cervix (1)\nprevents sperm reaching the egg / oviduct (1)\nMax 3\nAccept: oviduct partially blocked by mucus, so egg / sperm cannot pass\nDo not accept: no sperm produced` },
      { q: `Many people with cystic fibrosis have to take capsules of pancreatic enzymes with every meal. Without these capsules they may be underweight even when they eat a high-energy diet.\n\nExplain why people with cystic fibrosis may be underweight if they do not take the enzyme capsules.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `pancreatic duct is blocked by (thick) mucus (1)\npancreatic enzymes do not reach the duodenum / small intestine (1)\nfood is not fully digested / fewer products of digestion (1)\nso fewer nutrients absorbed (1)\nenzymes trapped in pancreas damage it / form cysts / fibrosis, so fewer enzymes made (1)\nMax 3\nAccept: named enzymes, e.g. lipase, amylase, trypsin\nAccept: mucus on intestine wall reduces absorption` },
      { q: `In the Meselson–Stahl experiment, bacteria were grown for many generations in a medium containing heavy nitrogen (¹⁵N), then transferred to a medium containing light nitrogen (¹⁴N). Samples of DNA were extracted after each generation and centrifuged in a density gradient.\n\nExplain the positions of the DNA bands expected after two generations in ¹⁴N if DNA replication is semi-conservative.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `two bands, one intermediate and one light (1)\nequal amounts in each band / half intermediate, half light (1)\neach new DNA molecule contains one original strand and one new strand (1)\nthe original ¹⁵N strands are now each paired with a ¹⁴N strand (intermediate) and the other molecules contain only ¹⁴N (light) (1)\nMax 3\nDo not accept: heavy band present` },
      { q: `(Data written for this practice question.)\nA student investigated the effect of temperature on the permeability of beetroot cell membranes. The red pigment is stored in the vacuoles of the cells. The absorbance of the water surrounding the beetroot discs after 30 minutes is shown below.\n\nTemperature / °C — Absorbance\n20 — 0.08\n30 — 0.10\n40 — 0.14\n50 — 0.42\n60 — 0.86\n70 — 0.92\n\nExplain the change in absorbance between 40 °C and 70 °C.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `absorbance increases (greatly) above 40 °C (1)\nmembrane proteins are denatured (1)\nphospholipids gain kinetic energy / membrane more fluid / gaps form in bilayer (1)\nmembranes (tonoplast and cell surface membrane) become more permeable so more pigment leaks out (1)\nMax 3\nAccept: credit any quantitative manipulation of the data as the first mark, e.g. absorbance increases by 0.78\nDo not accept: membranes / cells are denatured` },
      { q: `(Data written for this practice question.)\nIn the beetroot investigation, a student placed four tubes of beetroot discs in a water bath at 50 °C. The absorbance readings of the water from the four tubes were 0.42, 0.45, 0.39 and 0.61.\n\nDetermine the mean absorbance and the range of the results, after removing any anomalous reading.`, marks: 3, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `0.61 identified as anomalous (1)\nmean = 0.42 (1)\nrange = 0.39 to 0.45 / 0.06 (1)\nIf 0.61 not excluded: mean 0.47 and range 0.22 scores 1 mark (calculation correct, anomaly not identified)` },
      { q: `Trypsin is a protease released by the pancreas into the duodenum. People with cystic fibrosis may have less trypsin in their duodenum.\n\nExplain how trypsin increases the rate of hydrolysis of proteins.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(protein / substrate) binds to the active site (1)\nforms an enzyme–substrate complex (1)\n(enzyme) lowers the activation energy (1)\nby putting strain on the peptide bonds / bringing groups close together / induced fit (1)\nMax 3\nAccept: active site complementary to the substrate\nDo not accept: active site is the same shape as the substrate / enzyme provides energy` },
      { q: `CFTR is a globular protein that forms a channel in the cell surface membrane. Collagen is a fibrous protein found in the connective tissue of the lungs.\n\nCompare the structure of globular proteins and fibrous proteins.`, marks: 3, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `both have a primary structure / sequence of amino acids joined by peptide bonds (1)\nglobular proteins are folded into a compact / spherical shape, fibrous proteins form long chains / strands (1)\nglobular proteins have hydrophilic R groups on the outside, fibrous have mainly hydrophobic R groups (1)\nfibrous proteins have repeating sequences of amino acids, globular have irregular / varied sequences (1)\nglobular soluble in water, fibrous insoluble (1)\nfibrous often have chains wound together / cross-links between chains (1)\nMax 3\nComparative statements needed for each mark, except the first` },
      { q: `The CFTR gene is located on chromosome 7. In an epithelial cell lining the airways, the gene is transcribed in the nucleus.\n\nDescribe how the CFTR gene is transcribed.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `hydrogen bonds between DNA bases break / DNA unwinds (1)\none strand acts as the template (1)\nRNA nucleotides line up by complementary base pairing (1)\nuracil pairs with adenine (1)\nRNA polymerase joins nucleotides (by phosphodiester bonds) (1)\nmRNA leaves nucleus through nuclear pore (1)\nMax 3\nDo not accept: DNA polymerase / thymine in mRNA` },
      { q: `In a gene therapy trial for cystic fibrosis, copies of the normal CFTR allele were enclosed in liposomes. Patients inhaled the liposomes using a nebuliser once a month. Liposomes fuse with the cell surface membranes of the airway epithelial cells, or are taken in by endocytosis.\n\nExplain why this treatment must be repeated regularly.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `this is somatic gene therapy / alleles not inserted into germ line / stem cells (1)\nepithelial cells have a short life / are constantly replaced (1)\nnew cells (produced by mitosis) do not contain the normal allele (1)\nnormal allele may not be incorporated into the cell's DNA / chromosomes (1)\nonly a small proportion of cells take up the allele (1)\nMax 3\nIgnore: allele is broken down without explanation` },
      { q: `A genetic counsellor drew a pedigree for a family. Generation I: an unaffected man (1) and an unaffected woman (2). Their children, generation II: an unaffected son (3), an affected daughter (4) and an unaffected son (5).\n\nDeduce whether the condition is caused by a dominant or a recessive allele, and whether the gene is on an autosome or on the X chromosome. Give reasons for your answers.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `recessive (1)\nbecause both parents (1 and 2) are unaffected but have an affected child, so the parents are carriers / heterozygous (1)\nautosomal, because the father (1) is unaffected but his daughter (4) is affected / if X-linked recessive the father would have to be affected (1)\nAccept: correct genotypes used in reasoning, e.g. Ff × Ff` },
      { q: `A student planned to investigate the effect of trypsin concentration on the initial rate of hydrolysis of milk protein (casein). A suspension of skimmed milk powder is cloudy and becomes clearer as the protein is hydrolysed. The student was provided with a 1% trypsin solution, a milk suspension, distilled water, a colorimeter and a water bath.\n\nDescribe a method the student could use to carry out this investigation.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `dilute the trypsin solution with distilled water to make a range of (at least five) concentrations, e.g. 0.2% to 1.0% (1)\nequilibrate milk and trypsin in a water bath at a set temperature before mixing (1)\ncontrol volume / concentration of milk suspension and volume of trypsin (1)\nmeasure absorbance (with colorimeter) at regular intervals / time taken for milk to clear (1)\ncalculate initial rate from gradient of tangent at t = 0 / 1 ÷ time (1)\nrepeat at each concentration and calculate a mean (1)\ncontrol pH (using buffer) (1)\nMax 4\nAccept: use of a mark on paper behind tube to judge end point` },
      { q: `One mutation in the CFTR gene, G551D, is a substitution that changes the amino acid glycine (R group = H) at position 551 into aspartic acid, which has a large, negatively charged R group. Position 551 is in the region of the protein that binds ATP. CFTR protein with this mutation reaches the cell surface membrane but the channel rarely opens.\n\nExplain how this mutation could prevent the CFTR channel from functioning.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the change in primary structure / amino acid sequence (1)\nchanges the bonds that form between R groups, e.g. hydrogen / ionic bonds (1)\ntertiary structure / 3D shape changes (1)\n(so) ATP cannot bind / binding site no longer complementary to ATP (1)\nchannel does not change shape / open so chloride ions cannot pass through (1)\nMax 4\nAccept: disulfide bridges in context of tertiary structure\nDo not accept: active site in context of a channel unless qualified as ATP binding site` },
      { q: `The cell surface membrane of an airway epithelial cell contains phospholipids, cholesterol, glycoproteins and channel proteins such as CFTR. In freeze-fracture electron micrographs the membrane appears as a smooth surface with many particles scattered across it, and experiments with labelled proteins show that the proteins move within the membrane.\n\nExplain why the cell surface membrane is described as a fluid mosaic.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `phospholipid bilayer with hydrophilic heads facing outwards / hydrophobic tails inwards (1)\nfluid because phospholipids (and proteins) can move (laterally) within the layer (1)\nmosaic because proteins are scattered / embedded among the phospholipids (1)\nsome proteins span the bilayer (intrinsic) and some are on the surface (extrinsic) (1)\ncholesterol regulates fluidity (1)\nglycoproteins / glycolipids on outer surface (1)\nMax 4\nDo not accept: cell wall` },
      { q: `During protein synthesis in an airway epithelial cell, an mRNA molecule transcribed from the CFTR gene leaves the nucleus and attaches to a ribosome on the rough endoplasmic reticulum.\n\nDescribe how a polypeptide is produced from this mRNA molecule.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `mRNA attaches to the ribosome (1)\ntRNA molecules carry specific amino acids (1)\nanticodon on tRNA binds to complementary codon on mRNA (1)\ntwo tRNAs bind at a time / ribosome holds two codons (1)\npeptide bond forms between adjacent amino acids (1)\nribosome moves along mRNA one codon at a time (1)\nuntil a stop codon is reached (1)\nMax 4\nDo not accept: tRNA codons / mRNA anticodons` },
      { q: `(Data written for this practice question.)\nA student investigated the effect of trypsin concentration on the rate of hydrolysis of milk protein. The time taken for a milk suspension to clear at each concentration is shown below. Rate of reaction can be calculated as 1 ÷ time.\n\nTrypsin concentration / % — Time to clear / s\n0.25 — 400\n0.50 — 205\n1.00 — 108\n2.00 — 80\n\nCalculate the rate of reaction at each concentration and deduce the relationship between trypsin concentration and rate of reaction.`, marks: 4, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `rates: 0.0025, 0.0049, 0.0093, 0.0125 s⁻¹ (1)\nAccept: 2.5 × 10⁻³, 4.88 × 10⁻³, 9.26 × 10⁻³, 1.25 × 10⁻² s⁻¹ (any 2–3 s.f.)\nunits s⁻¹ (1)\nbetween 0.25% and 1.00% rate approximately doubles when concentration doubles / is (directly) proportional (1)\nfrom 1.00% to 2.00% rate increases less / not proportional (only × 1.35) (1)\n(because) substrate becomes limiting / not all active sites occupied (1)\nMax 4` },
      { q: `A couple who are both carriers of cystic fibrosis are considering pre-implantation genetic diagnosis (PGD). In PGD, embryos are produced by IVF and one cell is removed from each embryo at the 8-cell stage and tested for the CFTR alleles.\n\nExplain why this couple might choose PGD rather than prenatal testing during a natural pregnancy.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `only embryos without cystic fibrosis / homozygous dominant / carriers are implanted (1)\nso no need to consider termination of pregnancy (1)\nwhich may be against their beliefs / is distressing (1)\navoids risk of miscarriage from amniocentesis / CVS (1)\ntesting done before pregnancy so no anxious wait during pregnancy (1)\nembryos can also be chosen that are not carriers (1)\nMax 4\nIgnore: PGD is cheaper` },
      { q: `Before an epithelial cell in the airway divides by mitosis, all of its DNA, including the CFTR gene, is copied.\n\nDescribe how DNA is replicated.`, marks: 5, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `DNA (helix) unwinds (1)\nhydrogen bonds between bases break (1)\n(by) DNA helicase (1)\nboth strands act as templates (1)\nfree (DNA) nucleotides line up by complementary base pairing / A with T, C with G (1)\nDNA polymerase joins nucleotides (1)\nforming phosphodiester bonds / condensation reactions (1)\neach new molecule has one original and one new strand / semi-conservative (1)\nMax 5\nDo not accept: RNA polymerase / uracil` },
      { q: `(Data written for this practice question.)\nA student investigated the effect of ethanol on the permeability of beetroot cell membranes. Beetroot discs were placed in ethanol solutions at room temperature for 20 minutes, and the absorbance of each solution was measured. The investigation was carried out three times.\n\nEthanol concentration / % — Mean absorbance — Range\n0 — 0.05 — 0.04 to 0.07\n10 — 0.06 — 0.03 to 0.09\n20 — 0.12 — 0.10 to 0.15\n40 — 0.71 — 0.65 to 0.76\n\nThe student concluded that ethanol damages beetroot cell membranes at all concentrations above 0%.\n\nEvaluate the student's conclusion.`, marks: 5, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `(support) mean absorbance increases as ethanol concentration increases (1)\n(support) at 20% and 40% the ranges do not overlap with 0%, so these are likely to be real differences (1)\n(against) at 10% the range overlaps with 0% so there may be no real difference / damage not shown at 10% (1)\n(against) only three repeats / no statistical test carried out (1)\n(against) no concentrations between 0% and 20% other than 10% / large intervals, so threshold not known (1)\n(against) temperature not controlled / room temperature may vary (1)\n(against) absorbance only measures pigment leakage, not damage directly (1)\nethanol dissolves phospholipids / denatures membrane proteins, explaining increase (1)\nMax 5\nMust include at least one support and one against point for full marks` },
      { q: `In the CFTR mutation G542X, a single base substitution changes the codon for amino acid 542 into a stop codon. The normal CFTR protein contains 1480 amino acids.\n\nCalculate the percentage of the normal length of the polypeptide that would be produced, and explain why this mutation leads to the symptoms of cystic fibrosis.`, marks: 5, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `36.6 (%) (1)\n[541 ÷ 1480 × 100 = 36.55]\nAccept: 36.5–36.7 / 36.6 from 542 ÷ 1480 (= 36.6)\ntranslation stops at codon 542 / polypeptide is shortened / truncated (1)\nprotein lacks (many) amino acids so cannot fold into correct tertiary structure (1)\nno functional CFTR channel in cell surface membrane (1)\nchloride ions not transported out of epithelial cells (1)\nso water does not move into mucus by osmosis / mucus is thick and sticky (1)\nMax 5` },
      { q: `*People with cystic fibrosis often have difficulty breathing, become breathless during exercise and suffer from frequent lung infections. Their airways become blocked by thick, sticky mucus.\n\nExplain how thick, sticky mucus causes these symptoms.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 cilia cannot move the thick mucus / mucus not removed from the airways\nIC2 bacteria / pathogens trapped in mucus are not removed so they multiply, causing infections\nIC3 mucus blocks bronchioles so less air reaches the alveoli / fewer alveoli ventilated\nIC4 concentration gradient of oxygen between alveoli and blood is reduced\nIC5 infections / inflammation damage the alveoli, reducing surface area for gas exchange\nIC6 mucus / scarring increases the diffusion distance\nIC7 Fick's law: rate of diffusion reduced because of lower surface area, lower concentration difference or greater thickness\nIC8 less oxygen reaches cells for (aerobic) respiration, so breathless during exercise / less ATP\nIC9 narrower airways increase resistance, so more effort needed to breathe\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 IC → 4; 5–4 IC → 3; 3–2 IC → 2; 1 IC → 1\nLinkages and reasoning: sustained logical chain from mucus to symptoms → 2; partial chain → 1; none → 0` },
      { q: `*A woman who knows she is a carrier of cystic fibrosis is pregnant. Her partner has not been tested. She has been offered genetic screening, including carrier testing of her partner and prenatal testing by amniocentesis or chorionic villus sampling.\n\nDiscuss the ethical issues that the couple and society might consider in relation to this genetic screening.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 carrier testing of the partner shows whether the fetus can have cystic fibrosis (1 in 4 if both carriers), avoiding unnecessary invasive testing\nIC2 amniocentesis / CVS carries a risk of miscarriage of a fetus that may be healthy\nIC3 a positive result allows the couple to make an informed choice / prepare for the care of the child\nIC4 the couple may choose termination; some people believe the fetus has a right to life / termination is against religious beliefs\nIC5 false positives / false negatives can occur, leading to wrong decisions\nIC6 the test may reveal the fetus is a carrier, raising issues about who should be told\nIC7 the results could be used to discriminate, e.g. by insurers or employers\nIC8 the 'slippery slope' argument: selection against disabilities / designer babies; may devalue people living with cystic fibrosis\nIC9 cost to health services of screening compared with treatment; who should make the decision (autonomy, informed consent, counselling)\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 IC → 4; 5–4 IC → 3; 3–2 IC → 2; 1 IC → 1\nLinkages and reasoning: balanced discussion with arguments for and against, linked to consequences → 2; one-sided or partially linked → 1; list only → 0` },
      { q: `*Beetroot cells contain a red pigment stored in their vacuoles. The pigment can only leak out of a cell if both the tonoplast and the cell surface membrane are damaged. A student is provided with beetroot, a cork borer, a knife, a ruler, test tubes, water baths and a colorimeter.\n\nDevise an investigation to find the effect of temperature on the permeability of beetroot cell membranes.`, marks: 6, command: 'Devise', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 use a cork borer and ruler to cut discs / cylinders of the same diameter and thickness from the same beetroot\nIC2 wash the discs in (running) distilled water until no more pigment is released, to remove pigment from cut cells\nIC3 place the same number of discs in the same volume of distilled water in each tube\nIC4 use a range of at least five temperatures, e.g. 20 °C to 70 °C, using water baths, with water equilibrated before the discs are added\nIC5 leave for the same time, e.g. 30 minutes, then shake / mix and remove the discs\nIC6 use a colorimeter with a blue / green filter, zeroed with distilled water\nIC7 record absorbance (or % transmission); higher absorbance means more pigment leaked so membranes more permeable\nIC8 repeat at each temperature (at least three times) and calculate a mean; identify anomalies\nIC9 plot a graph of mean absorbance against temperature; risk: take care with cork borer / knife and hot water\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 IC → 4; 5–4 IC → 3; 3–2 IC → 2; 1 IC → 1\nLinkages and reasoning: a logically ordered method with control of variables justified → 2; partially structured → 1; unconnected points → 0` }
    ]
  },
  'S3': {
    name: 'Voice of the Genome',
    questions: [
      { q: `An electron micrograph of a cell from the pancreas shows a stack of flattened, curved, membrane-bound sacs. Small vesicles are budding from the edges of the sacs.\n\nName this organelle.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `Golgi apparatus (1)\nAccept Golgi body / Golgi complex\nDo not accept rough endoplasmic reticulum / ER` },
      { q: `Escherichia coli is a bacterium found in the human gut. Its cytoplasm contains ribosomes that are smaller than those in the cytoplasm of human cells.\n\nState the size of the ribosomes found in the cytoplasm of E. coli.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `70S (1)\nDo not accept 80S\nIgnore references to mass in daltons` },
      { q: `A student prepared a squash of a garlic root to observe mitosis. She cut off only the end 5 mm of the root and discarded the rest.\n\nGive the reason why only the root tip was used.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `the tip contains the meristem / it is the only region where cells are dividing by mitosis (1)\nAccept the tip is where most cells are in the cell cycle\nDo not accept 'where cells are growing' alone` },
      { q: `Four days after fertilisation, a human embryo is a ball of 16 to 32 cells called a morula. Each cell of the morula can give rise to any cell of the body and also to the cells of the placenta.\n\nGive the term used to describe cells with this ability.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `totipotent (1)\nDo not accept pluripotent / multipotent` },
      { q: `Maize (Zea mays) is a flowering plant with a diploid chromosome number (2n) of 20. In maize, double fertilisation produces both an embryo and an endosperm, which stores starch in the grain.\n\nState the number of chromosomes in each nucleus of the maize endosperm.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `30 (1)\nAccept 3n = 30` },
      { q: `Haemophilia A is a sex-linked disorder in which blood fails to clot normally. It is caused by a recessive allele, h, of a gene on the X chromosome. The dominant allele is H.\n\nGive the genotype of a woman who is a carrier of haemophilia A.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `XᴴXʰ (1)\nAccept X^H X^h written in any clear form\nDo not accept Hh (sex chromosomes must be shown)` },
      { q: `A student used an eyepiece graticule to measure cells in an onion root tip squash at ×400. When she calibrated the graticule, 40 eyepiece divisions lined up exactly with 10 divisions of a stage micrometer. Each stage micrometer division is 10 µm.\n\nOne cell in prophase was 18 eyepiece divisions long.\n\nCalculate the actual length of this cell.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `one eyepiece division = 100 ÷ 40 = 2.5 µm (1)\n18 × 2.5 = 45 µm (1)\nCorrect answer with units scores 2 marks\nAccept 0.045 mm` },
      { q: `An electron micrograph of a human sperm cell shows a mitochondrion in the midpiece. On the micrograph the mitochondrion is 36 mm long. The magnification of the micrograph is ×18 000.\n\nCalculate the actual length of the mitochondrion in micrometres (µm).`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `converts 36 mm to 36 000 µm (1)\n36 000 ÷ 18 000 = 2.0 µm (1)\nCorrect answer scores 2 marks\nAccept 2 µm\nIf answer given in mm (0.002 mm) award 1 mark` },
      { q: `In a practical to observe mitosis, a student placed onion root tips in 1 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid at 60 °C for 5 minutes before staining them with toluidine blue.\n\nExplain why the root tips were placed in hydrochloric acid.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `hydrolyses / breaks down the middle lamella / pectin between cells (1)\nso that the cells separate / can be spread into a single layer when squashed (1)\nAccept so that the stain can reach / penetrate the cells / chromosomes\nAccept stops mitosis / kills the cells so cells are fixed at the stage they were in\nDo not accept 'breaks down cell walls' alone` },
      { q: `In some mammals, such as the hamster, several sperm may reach the zona pellucida of an egg at the same time. After the first sperm fuses with the egg cell membrane, the cortical reaction occurs.\n\nExplain the importance of the cortical reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `cortical granules release (enzymes) causing the zona pellucida to harden / thicken / become impermeable (1)\nprevents more than one sperm entering / prevents polyspermy (1)\nso the zygote has the diploid number of chromosomes / is not polyploid (1)\nMax 2\nDo not accept 'the acrosome reaction stops other sperm'` },
      { q: `The garden pea, Pisum sativum, has a diploid chromosome number (2n) of 14.\n\nCalculate the number of different combinations of chromosomes that could be produced in the gametes of a pea plant as a result of independent assortment alone.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `2ⁿ where n = 7 / 2⁷ (1)\n128 (1)\nCorrect answer scores 2 marks\nDo not accept 2¹⁴ = 16 384` },
      { q: `The midpiece of a human sperm cell is packed with mitochondria wrapped in a spiral around the base of the flagellum.\n\nExplain why the midpiece contains many mitochondria.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(mitochondria are the site of) aerobic respiration producing ATP (1)\nATP provides energy for the flagellum to move / for the sperm to swim to the egg (1)\nDo not accept 'produce energy'` },
      { q: `The fruit fly Drosophila melanogaster has a diploid chromosome number (2n) of 8. Its body cells contain one pair of sex chromosomes and three pairs of autosomes.\n\nDraw a labelled diagram of an animal cell with a diploid number of 4 during anaphase of mitosis.`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `four (single) chromosomes / chromatids moving to each pole, with centromeres leading (V shapes pointing to poles) (1)\nspindle fibres drawn attached to centromeres and labelled (1)\nAccept two pairs of different sizes shown at each pole\nDo not accept chromosomes shown as X-shaped (that is metaphase)` },
      { q: `In many cancer cells, the promoter regions of tumour suppressor genes have a much higher level of DNA methylation than in normal cells.\n\nExplain how DNA methylation can prevent a gene from being expressed.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `methyl groups added to cytosine (bases) (in the promoter) (1)\nprevents (binding of) transcription factors / RNA polymerase (1)\nso the gene is not transcribed / no mRNA produced (1)\nAccept causes chromatin to become more condensed / tightly coiled, so DNA is inaccessible\nMax 2` },
      { q: `Mycoplasma pneumoniae is a bacterium that causes a form of pneumonia. Like other bacteria, it is a prokaryote.\n\nGive two differences between the ultrastructure of a prokaryotic cell and a human lung epithelial cell, other than the presence of a cell wall.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two of:\nprokaryote has no nucleus / DNA free in cytoplasm (whereas epithelial cell has a nuclear envelope) (1)\nprokaryote has circular DNA / epithelial cell DNA is linear and associated with histones (1)\nprokaryote has 70S ribosomes, epithelial cell has 80S (1)\nprokaryote has no membrane-bound organelles e.g. no mitochondria / ER / Golgi (1)\nprokaryote may have plasmids (1)\nprokaryote may have mesosomes / a capsule / pili / flagella (1)\nDifferences must be comparative or clearly implied` },
      { q: `The cell cycle of a type of meristem cell in a bean root takes 16 hours. A student counted 200 cells in a root tip squash. 6 of these cells were in metaphase.\n\nCalculate the time, in minutes, that these cells spend in metaphase. Assume that the proportion of cells in each stage is proportional to the time spent in that stage.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `6 ÷ 200 × 16 (× 60) (1)\n28.8 minutes (1)\nAccept 29 minutes\nCorrect answer scores 2 marks\n0.48 hours scores 1 mark` },
      { q: `Himalayan rabbits have a white body but black fur on the ears, nose, feet and tail. The black pigment is made by an enzyme coded for by the C gene. If a patch of white fur on the back is shaved and an ice pack is kept on the skin, the fur grows back black.\n\nSuggest an explanation for these observations.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `the enzyme is (temperature sensitive and) only active at low temperatures / is denatured at body core temperature (1)\nthe extremities / iced skin are cooler so pigment is produced there (1)\nAccept phenotype is the result of interaction between genotype and environment, if linked to temperature` },
      { q: `Pancreatic acinar cells secrete digestive enzymes. In an investigation, acinar cells were given radioactive amino acids for 3 minutes and then non-radioactive amino acids. The percentage of the radioactivity in the cell found in each structure was measured at intervals (data written for this practice question).\n\nTime 3 min: rough ER 86%, Golgi apparatus 9%, secretory vesicles 5%\nTime 20 min: rough ER 41%, Golgi apparatus 49%, secretory vesicles 10%\nTime 90 min: rough ER 15%, Golgi apparatus 17%, secretory vesicles 68%\n\nExplain these results.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `amino acids are first used / incorporated into polypeptides at ribosomes on the rough ER (so radioactivity highest there at 3 min) (1)\npolypeptides move (in vesicles) from the RER to the Golgi apparatus (so Golgi radioactivity peaks at 20 min) (1)\nproteins are modified / packaged in the Golgi and leave in secretory vesicles (so vesicle radioactivity highest at 90 min) (1)\nAccept use of data to support each point\nDo not accept 'proteins are made in the Golgi'` },
      { q: `A student investigated the effect of an extract of Allium (onion) bulb on mitosis in garlic root tips. She counted the cells in one field of view for a control root tip and a treated root tip (data written for this practice question).\n\nControl: 200 cells counted, 28 in a stage of mitosis\nTreated with extract: 200 cells counted, 12 in a stage of mitosis\n\nThe mitotic index = number of cells in mitosis ÷ total number of cells.\n\nCalculate the percentage change in the mitotic index caused by the extract.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `control mitotic index = 28 ÷ 200 = 0.14 (1)\ntreated mitotic index = 12 ÷ 200 = 0.06 (1)\n(0.06 − 0.14) ÷ 0.14 × 100 = −57.1% / a decrease of 57.1% (1)\nAccept 57% decrease\nCorrect answer scores 3 marks\nTE for third mark from incorrect indices` },
      { q: `Cystic fibrosis and sickle cell disease are both caused by recessive alleles on different chromosomes. Two parents are heterozygous for both genes. Their children may inherit new combinations of alleles on the same chromosome that were not present in either parent's chromosomes.\n\nExplain how crossing over during meiosis produces new combinations of alleles.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `homologous chromosomes pair up / form bivalents (in prophase I) (1)\nchromatids (of homologous chromosomes) break and rejoin / exchange sections at chiasmata (1)\nalleles are exchanged between non-sister chromatids, so a chromatid carries a new combination of alleles / linkage groups are broken (1)\nDo not accept 'chromosomes swap genes' without reference to chromatids or alleles\nDo not accept references to independent assortment alone` },
      { q: `Red-green colour blindness is caused by a recessive allele, Xᵇ, on the X chromosome. The dominant allele is Xᴮ. A woman with normal vision whose father was colour blind has children with a man who has normal vision.\n\nDetermine the probability that their first child will be a colour-blind boy. Use a genetic diagram to show your working.`, marks: 3, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `parental genotypes XᴮXᵇ and XᴮY (1)\ngametes Xᴮ, Xᵇ and Xᴮ, Y with offspring XᴮXᴮ, XᴮXᵇ, XᴮY, XᵇY shown (1)\nprobability 0.25 / 1 in 4 / 25% (1)\nAccept 1:3 as ratio only if probability also stated\nDo not accept 0.5 (that is the probability for a son)` },
      { q: `In the thyroid gland, the calcitonin gene produces a hormone that lowers blood calcium. In some neurones, the same gene produces a different protein, CGRP, which dilates blood vessels.\n\nExplain how one gene can code for two different proteins.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the gene is transcribed into pre-mRNA (containing introns and exons) (1)\npre-mRNA is spliced / introns removed and exons joined (1)\ndifferent exons are retained / removed in different cells, producing different mRNA (1)\nso different sequences of amino acids / polypeptides are translated (1)\nMax 3\nAccept post-transcriptional modification / alternative splicing` },
      { q: `Induced pluripotent stem cells (iPS cells) are made by adding genes for particular transcription factors to adult skin cells. Some scientists hope to use iPS cells to replace damaged heart muscle after a heart attack.\n\nExplain why iPS cells made from the patient's own skin may be preferred to embryonic stem cells for this treatment.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the cells are genetically identical to the patient / have the patient's antigens (1)\nso they will not be rejected by the immune system / no need for immunosuppressant drugs (1)\nno embryo is destroyed so there are fewer ethical objections (1)\nare available / do not require a donor embryo or IVF (1)\nMax 3\nDo not accept 'iPS cells are totipotent'` },
      { q: `Seed mass was measured in 500 seeds from a single variety of bean grown in a field (data written for this practice question). The masses ranged from 0.30 g to 0.90 g. The distribution was bell-shaped, with the mode at 0.60 g, and there were no distinct classes.\n\nExplain why seed mass shows this pattern of variation.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `continuous variation / normal distribution (1)\n(seed mass is) polygenic / controlled by many genes (1)\neach gene / allele has a small (additive) effect (1)\nenvironment also affects phenotype e.g. light / water / mineral ions / soil nitrate (1)\nMax 3` },
      { q: `A student prepared one root tip squash and counted the cells in a single field of view near the edge of the slide. She concluded that the mitotic index of the root tip was 0.08.\n\nSuggest three improvements to her method that would give a more valid estimate of the mitotic index of the root tip.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `count cells in several / at least five fields of view (1)\nchoose fields of view at random / using a grid / from the meristem region just behind the root cap (1)\nuse several root tips / repeat with more squashes and calculate a mean (1)\ncount a larger number of cells e.g. at least 100 / 200 per field (1)\nhave rules for borderline cells e.g. count cells crossing the top and left edge only (1)\nMax 3` },
      { q: `In a flowering plant, the female gametophyte inside the ovule contains a haploid egg cell and two haploid polar nuclei. The pollen tube delivers two haploid male nuclei.\n\nExplain why the cells of the embryo are diploid but the cells of the endosperm are triploid.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `double fertilisation occurs (1)\none male nucleus fuses with the (haploid) egg cell (nucleus) to form a diploid zygote, which forms the embryo (1)\nthe other male nucleus fuses with the two (haploid) polar nuclei forming a triploid nucleus that gives rise to the endosperm (1)` },
      { q: `MyoD is a protein found in stem cells that develop into muscle. When the MyoD gene was artificially switched on in cultured fibroblast (connective tissue) cells, the fibroblasts developed into muscle cells.\n\nSuggest how MyoD causes a cell to differentiate into a muscle cell.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `MyoD is a transcription factor (1)\nbinds to the promoter / DNA of muscle-specific genes (1)\n(stimulates RNA polymerase so) these genes are transcribed / switched on, producing mRNA (1)\nmuscle proteins e.g. actin / myosin produced which give the cell its specialised structure (1)\nMax 3\nAccept differential gene expression` },
      { q: `I-cell disease is a rare inherited condition. Lysosomal enzymes are made normally on the rough ER, but they are released from the cell by exocytosis instead of being sent to lysosomes. The disease is caused by a faulty enzyme in the Golgi apparatus that normally adds a phosphate-containing sugar 'tag' to lysosomal enzymes.\n\nSuggest why cells of people with I-cell disease fill up with undigested material.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `the Golgi apparatus modifies proteins / adds carbohydrate and packages them into vesicles (1)\nwithout the tag the enzymes are not packaged into lysosomes / are put into secretory vesicles (1)\nso lysosomes lack hydrolytic enzymes and cannot digest / break down old organelles / engulfed material (1)` },
      { q: `Escherichia coli normally respires glucose. When glucose is absent and lactose is present, E. coli produces β-galactosidase, an enzyme that hydrolyses lactose. The gene for β-galactosidase is part of the lac operon, which is controlled by a repressor protein.\n\nExplain how the presence of lactose leads to the production of β-galactosidase.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(without lactose) the repressor protein binds to the operator (1)\nwhich blocks RNA polymerase from binding to the promoter / transcribing the structural genes (1)\nlactose binds to the repressor, changing its shape so it can no longer bind to the operator (1)\nRNA polymerase binds to the promoter and the gene is transcribed (1)\nmRNA is translated to produce β-galactosidase (1)\nMax 4` },
      { q: `In a clinic, sperm are mixed with eggs during in vitro fertilisation (IVF). Embryologists observe that the sperm first bind to the zona pellucida, and later only one sperm nucleus is found inside each fertilised egg.\n\nDescribe the events from the binding of a sperm to the zona pellucida to the formation of a zygote.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `acrosome reaction: acrosome releases (hydrolytic / digestive) enzymes (1)\nwhich digest the zona pellucida (1)\nsperm cell membrane fuses with the egg cell membrane / sperm nucleus enters the egg (1)\ncortical reaction: cortical granules release their contents (by exocytosis), making the zona pellucida harder / impermeable (1)\nsperm nucleus fuses with egg nucleus forming a diploid zygote (1)\nMax 4\nDo not accept 'the sperm head bursts' for acrosome reaction` },
      { q: `A technician is writing instructions for students to make a stained root tip squash to observe mitosis using garlic bulbs that have grown roots in water.\n\nDescribe the method the students should use to prepare the slide.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `cut off the end 5 mm (approx.) of a root tip (1)\nplace in (warm) hydrochloric acid for a few minutes then rinse in water (1)\nplace tip on a slide and add a stain e.g. toluidine blue / acetic orcein (1)\ncover with a coverslip and squash / press firmly (with blotting paper over it) to spread cells into a single layer (1)\nview with a light microscope, first at low power then high power (1)\nwear eye protection when handling acid / stain (1)\nMax 4` },
      { q: `Female honeybee larvae are genetically very similar. Larvae fed royal jelly develop into queens; larvae fed worker jelly develop into sterile workers. Researchers found that the larvae fed royal jelly had lower activity of the enzyme DNA methyltransferase, which adds methyl groups to DNA. When this enzyme was silenced in larvae fed worker jelly, most of them developed into queens.\n\nExplain how diet can determine whether a larva develops into a queen or a worker.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `diet causes an epigenetic change / modification (1)\nroyal jelly reduces DNA methylation (1)\n(less methylation) allows transcription factors / RNA polymerase to bind, so (queen-development) genes are transcribed / switched on (1)\nmore methylation in workers silences / switches off these genes (1)\nthe DNA base sequence is not changed (1)\nthe pattern of gene expression is maintained in daughter cells by mitosis (1)\nMax 4` },
      { q: `The mass of DNA in a single cell from a mouse embryo was measured over 30 hours (data written for this practice question). The graph showed:\n0–6 h: constant at 6.6 pg\n6–14 h: rising steadily to 13.2 pg\n14–20 h: constant at 13.2 pg\nat 20 h: falling suddenly to 6.6 pg, then constant until 26 h\n26–30 h: rising again\n\nDescribe and explain the changes in the mass of DNA in this cell.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `0–6 h is G1 / interphase, DNA mass constant as cell grows / makes organelles and proteins (1)\n6–14 h DNA mass doubles from 6.6 to 13.2 pg because of DNA replication in S phase (1)\n14–20 h G2 / mitosis, mass stays at 13.2 pg (1)\nat 20 h mass halves because of cytokinesis / chromatids separated into two daughter cells (1)\nfrom 26 h DNA replication begins in the next cell cycle (1)\nMax 4\nMust use data for full marks` },
      { q: `A student measured the diameter of six cheek epithelial cells using a calibrated eyepiece graticule. Her results in µm were:\n52 48 61 55 57 49\n\nStandard deviation, s = √[ Σ(x − x̄)² ÷ (n − 1) ]\n\nCalculate the mean diameter and the standard deviation, and comment on what the standard deviation shows.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mean = 322 ÷ 6 = 53.7 µm (1)\nΣ(x − x̄)² = 123.3 (1)\ns = √(123.3 ÷ 5) = 5.0 µm (1)\nAccept 4.97 µm\ncomment: the SD is small compared with the mean / about 9% of the mean, so diameters are similar / little spread around the mean (1)\nCorrect mean and SD with no working score 3 marks` },
      { q: `In Drosophila, the allele for grey body (G) is dominant to black body (g), and normal wings (N) is dominant to vestigial wings (n). Flies heterozygous for both genes (GgNn) were crossed with black-bodied, vestigial-winged flies (ggnn). The offspring were (data written for this practice question):\n\ngrey, normal 160\nblack, vestigial 155\ngrey, vestigial 44\nblack, normal 41\n\nχ² = Σ (O − E)² ÷ E\nCritical value of χ² at p = 0.05 for 3 degrees of freedom = 7.82\n\nUse a chi-squared test to determine whether these results fit the expected 1 : 1 : 1 : 1 ratio, and explain the results.`, marks: 5, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `expected = 100 for each class (1)\nχ² = (3600 + 3025 + 3136 + 3481) ÷ 100 = 132.4 (1)\n132.4 is greater than 7.82 so the difference is significant / reject the null hypothesis / results do not fit 1:1:1:1 (1)\nthe genes are linked / on the same chromosome (1)\nthe grey vestigial and black normal flies are recombinants produced by crossing over (1)\nrecombination frequency = 85 ÷ 400 × 100 = 21.25% (1)\nMax 5\nAccept 132.42` },
      { q: `Colchicine is a chemical extracted from the autumn crocus. It binds to tubulin, the protein that makes up spindle fibres, and stops spindle fibres from forming. Onion root tips grown in colchicine solution for 4 hours were squashed and stained.\n\nExplain the effect of colchicine on the appearance of the cells and on the mitotic index of the root tip.`, marks: 5, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `chromosomes still condense / become visible (in prophase) (1)\nchromosomes cannot be attached to / lined up on the equator by spindle fibres (1)\nsister chromatids are not pulled apart / anaphase does not occur (1)\nso many cells are held in (a metaphase-like stage) with condensed chromosomes (1)\nmitotic index increases because cells enter mitosis but do not complete it (1)\ncells that eventually re-form a nucleus will have double the chromosome number / be polyploid (1)\nMax 5` },
      { q: `Wheat flowers are pollinated by wind. A pollen grain landing on the stigma of a wheat flower germinates and grows towards an ovule.\n\nDescribe the events that occur from the germination of the pollen grain to the completion of double fertilisation.`, marks: 5, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `pollen tube grows out of the pollen grain down through the style (1)\npollen tube secretes (digestive) enzymes to digest a path through the style tissue (1)\ngrowth is directed by chemicals from the ovule / chemotropism towards the micropyle (1)\ntube nucleus at the tip / leads the way; generative nucleus divides by mitosis to form two male nuclei (1)\npollen tube enters the ovule through the micropyle and the tip bursts, releasing the male nuclei into the embryo sac (1)\none male nucleus fuses with the egg nucleus to form a diploid zygote (1)\nother male nucleus fuses with the two polar nuclei to form a triploid endosperm nucleus (1)\nMax 5` },
      { q: `*Parkinson's disease is caused by the death of dopamine-producing neurones in the brain. In clinical trials, patients have received transplants of dopamine-producing neurones grown from human embryonic stem cells. The embryos used were spare embryos donated after IVF treatment. In the UK, research using human embryos needs a licence from the Human Fertilisation and Embryology Authority (HFEA), and embryos cannot be kept beyond 14 days.\n\nDiscuss the use of embryonic stem cells to treat Parkinson's disease, including the role of regulatory authorities.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 embryonic stem cells are pluripotent / can differentiate into any type of body cell, including neurones\nIC2 cells from the inner cell mass of a blastocyst can be made to differentiate by adding specific factors / growth factors\nIC3 the transplanted cells could replace dead neurones and restore dopamine production / relieve symptoms\nIC4 risks: rejection by the immune system as cells are not genetically identical / need for immunosuppressants\nIC5 risk of uncontrolled division / tumours / cells differentiating into the wrong cell type\nIC6 ethical objection: the embryo is destroyed / some people believe an embryo has a right to life from fertilisation\nIC7 counter-argument: spare IVF embryos would be destroyed anyway / potential to relieve suffering\nIC8 regulatory authorities (HFEA) license and inspect research, consider ethics, limit the age of embryos to 14 days and ensure informed consent from donors\nIC9 alternatives such as adult / iPS stem cells reduce ethical concerns\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 IC = 4 marks; 5–4 IC = 3 marks; 3–2 IC = 2 marks; 1 IC = 1 mark\nReasoning: 2 marks for a sustained line of reasoning that weighs benefits against risks and ethical issues; 1 mark for partially structured reasoning; 0 marks for no linkage` },
      { q: `*A student read that the rate of mitosis in plant root tips depends on temperature. Garlic bulbs can be grown with their roots in water in a temperature-controlled water bath.\n\nDevise an investigation to find out the effect of temperature on the mitotic index of garlic root tips.`, marks: 6, command: 'Devise', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 independent variable: grow roots at a range of at least five temperatures e.g. 10, 15, 20, 25, 30 °C using water baths\nIC2 control variables: same variety / size of garlic bulb, same time of growth, same time of day root tips are cut, same volume of water\nIC3 cut the end 5 mm of root tips and place in (warm) hydrochloric acid, then rinse\nIC4 stain with toluidine blue / acetic orcein, add coverslip and squash to a single layer\nIC5 observe with a light microscope at high power and count number of cells in mitosis and total number of cells in a field of view\nIC6 mitotic index = cells in mitosis ÷ total cells\nIC7 count several random fields of view and several root tips at each temperature; calculate mean\nIC8 plot a graph of mean mitotic index against temperature / use a statistical test e.g. Spearman's rank correlation\nIC9 risk: hydrochloric acid is an irritant / corrosive, wear eye protection and gloves; care with hot water bath and scalpel\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 IC = 4 marks; 5–4 IC = 3 marks; 3–2 IC = 2 marks; 1 IC = 1 mark\nReasoning: 2 marks for a logically ordered method that would produce valid, repeatable data; 1 mark for partially structured method; 0 marks for no linkage` },
      { q: `*Twin studies compare identical (monozygotic, MZ) twins, who have the same genotype, with non-identical (dizygotic, DZ) twins, who share about 50% of their alleles. The table shows correlation coefficients for two characteristics (data written for this practice question). A coefficient of 1.0 means the twins are always the same.\n\nAdult height: MZ twins reared together 0.94; MZ twins reared apart 0.89; DZ twins reared together 0.47\nBody mass: MZ twins reared together 0.81; MZ twins reared apart 0.61; DZ twins reared together 0.44\n\nA separate study found that older MZ twins show more differences in their patterns of DNA methylation than young MZ twins.\n\nAnalyse the data to explain the relative contributions of genes and the environment to height and body mass.`, marks: 6, command: 'Analyse', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 both characteristics show continuous variation / are polygenic, controlled by many genes each with a small effect\nIC2 MZ correlations are higher than DZ for both, showing a genetic contribution\nIC3 height: MZ reared apart (0.89) almost as similar as MZ reared together (0.94), so height is mainly determined by genes\nIC4 body mass: much lower for MZ reared apart (0.61) than together (0.81), so environment has a larger effect\nIC5 examples of environmental factors e.g. diet, exercise for body mass; nutrition / disease in childhood for height\nIC6 no correlation is 1.0, so even height is affected by the environment\nIC7 environment can cause epigenetic changes, e.g. DNA methylation / histone modification, that alter gene expression without changing base sequence\nIC8 increasing methylation differences with age explains why MZ twins become less alike over time\nIC9 limitations: twins reared apart may still share similar environments / small sample / correlation does not prove causation\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 IC = 4 marks; 5–4 IC = 3 marks; 3–2 IC = 2 marks; 1 IC = 1 mark\nReasoning: 2 marks for sustained reasoning using comparisons of the data to reach a conclusion; 1 mark for partially structured reasoning; 0 marks for no linkage` }
    ]
  },
  'S4': {
    name: 'Biodiversity and Natural Resources',
    questions: [
      { q: `A thin section of potato tuber was stained with iodine solution and viewed with a light microscope. Many small blue-black, membrane-bound organelles were seen inside each cell.\n\nName these organelles.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• amyloplasts (1)\nAccept: leucoplasts / starch grains in amyloplasts\nDo not accept: chloroplasts / vacuoles` },
      { q: `Cotton fibres are almost pure cellulose. Cellulose is a polymer of β-glucose.\n\nName the type of bond that joins adjacent β-glucose molecules in a cellulose chain.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• 1,4 glycosidic bond (1)\nAccept: β-1,4 glycosidic bond\nDo not accept: 1,6 glycosidic / glycosidic bond without 1,4 / hydrogen bond / peptide bond` },
      { q: `Methanococcus jannaschii is a single-celled organism found around deep-sea hydrothermal vents. It has no nucleus. Its ribosomal RNA base sequences and its cell membrane lipids are very different from those of Escherichia coli.\n\nName the domain to which Methanococcus jannaschii belongs.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• Archaea (1)\nAccept: Archaebacteria\nDo not accept: Bacteria / Prokaryotae / Eukarya` },
      { q: `The hazel dormouse lives in woodland in southern England. It feeds on hazelnuts, flowers and insects, is active at night and spends the winter hibernating in nests on the woodland floor.\n\nGive the term used to describe the role of a species within its habitat, including its interactions with other organisms and its environment.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• niche (1)\nDo not accept: habitat / ecosystem / community` },
      { q: `Barley seedlings were grown in a nutrient solution containing all the required mineral ions except nitrate ions. After four weeks the seedlings were small and had pale leaves.\n\nName one type of biological molecule in a plant that needs nitrate ions for its synthesis.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• any one from: amino acids / proteins / enzymes / DNA / RNA / nucleic acids / nucleotides / ATP / chlorophyll (1)\nAccept: named protein or named nucleic acid\nDo not accept: cellulose / starch / glucose / lipids` },
      { q: `A student was preparing to identify sclerenchyma fibres and xylem vessels in a transverse section of a sunflower stem. The student cut a thin section and placed it on a slide.\n\nName a stain the student could add to the section to show the lignified tissues.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• toluidine blue (O) (1)\nAccept: phloroglucinol (and hydrochloric acid)\nDo not accept: iodine solution / methylene blue / eosin` },
      { q: `The Mauritius kestrel population fell to four wild birds in 1974 before a captive breeding programme was started. Scientists genotyped 40 birds from the present-day population at five gene loci. The table shows the number of birds that were heterozygous at each locus (data written for this practice question).\n\nLocus | Number of heterozygous birds\n1 | 12\n2 | 8\n3 | 0\n4 | 15\n5 | 5\n\nThe heterozygosity index, H, for a locus is given by:\nH = number of heterozygotes ÷ number of individuals in the population\n\nCalculate the mean heterozygosity index for these five loci.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• correct H for each locus: 0.3, 0.2, 0, 0.375, 0.125 (1)\nAccept: total heterozygotes 40 ÷ total genotypes 200\n• mean H = 0.2 (1)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks\nAccept: 0.20` },
      { q: `A student viewed a transverse section of a buttercup stem with a light microscope. In a photomicrograph taken at a magnification of ×400, the lumen of one xylem vessel measured 18 mm across.\n\nCalculate the actual diameter of the lumen of this xylem vessel. Give your answer in micrometres (µm).`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• converts 18 mm to 18 000 µm or divides 18 by 400 to give 0.045 mm (1)\n• 45 (µm) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks\nDo not accept: 0.045 µm` },
      { q: `Ropes made from hemp fibres were used on sailing ships because the fibres have a high tensile strength. Hemp fibres contain a high proportion of cellulose.\n\nExplain how the structure of cellulose molecules allows them to form fibres with a high tensile strength.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• cellulose chains are straight / unbranched / every other β-glucose is inverted (1)\n• many hydrogen bonds form between (parallel) chains to form microfibrils (1)\nAccept: microfibrils grouped into macrofibrils / fibres\nIgnore: references to glycosidic bonds alone\nDo not accept: hydrogen bonds within a coiled / helical chain` },
      { q: `The Millennium Seed Bank at Kew stores seeds from thousands of wild plant species. Before storage, the seeds are dried until their water content is very low and are then kept in sealed containers at −20 °C.\n\nExplain why the seeds are dried and stored at −20 °C.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• low water content and low temperature prevent germination (1)\n• (low temperature / dry conditions) slow enzyme activity / metabolism so stored food reserves are not used up / seeds remain viable for longer (1)\nAccept: dry seeds prevent ice crystals forming and damaging cells when frozen\nAccept: prevents growth of fungi / microorganisms\nDo not accept: enzymes are denatured` },
      { q: `A fluorescent dye was injected into the cytoplasm of a single cell in a leaf of the water plant Elodea. Within a few minutes the dye was seen in the cytoplasm of neighbouring cells, even though the dye cannot cross cell surface membranes.\n\nExplain how the dye moved into the neighbouring cells.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• through plasmodesmata (1)\n• (plasmodesmata are) strands / channels of cytoplasm that pass through the cell walls / pits linking adjacent cells (1)\nAccept: symplast route\nDo not accept: by diffusion across the membrane / through the cell wall alone` },
      { q: `Tomato plants grown in soil deficient in calcium ions often have distorted young leaves, and the walls of the new cells near the shoot tip are weak.\n\nExplain why a lack of calcium ions leads to weak cell walls in new cells.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• calcium (ions) are needed to form calcium pectate (1)\n• which forms the middle lamella that holds / sticks adjacent cell walls together (1)\nAccept: calcium pectate cross-links pectin in the cell wall\nDo not accept: calcium is needed to make cellulose` },
      { q: `In 1785 William Withering published his account of treating patients with dropsy (oedema) using extracts of foxglove leaves. He tried the extract on patients, recorded the effects and gradually changed the dose he gave.\n\nGive two ways in which Withering's approach differed from the way a new drug is tested today.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• no initial testing for safety on healthy volunteers / no phase 1 (1)\n• no control group / no placebo / no comparison with an existing treatment (1)\n• not double blind / patients and doctor knew the treatment given (1)\n• small number of patients / not a large trial (1)\n• dosage was found by trial and error on ill patients (1)\n• no prior laboratory or animal testing (1)\n• the amount of active ingredient (digitalis) in the extract was not standardised (1)` },
      { q: `In a phase 3 trial of a new drug to treat heart failure, patients were randomly given either the new drug or a placebo. The trial was double blind.\n\nExplain why the trial was double blind.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• neither the patients nor the doctors know who receives the drug or the placebo (1)\n• to avoid bias / the placebo effect affecting how results are reported or recorded (1)\nAccept: doctors' expectations cannot influence how they assess patients\nIgnore: to make it a fair test` },
      { q: `Przewalski's horse became extinct in the wild in the 1960s. Captive-bred horses from zoos were reintroduced into a reserve in Mongolia. The population in the reserve was 48 in 2010 and 132 in 2020 (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the percentage change in the population between 2010 and 2020.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• (132 − 48) ÷ 48 × 100 or 84 ÷ 48 × 100 (1)\n• (+) 175 (%) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2 marks\nDo not accept: −175 %` },
      { q: `Two ponds each contained 6 species of aquatic insect. In pond A, 95% of the insects caught were of one species. In pond B, the insects were spread evenly across the six species.\n\nExplain why an index of diversity is a better measure than species richness for comparing the biodiversity of these two ponds.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• species richness only counts the number of species, so both ponds would be given the same value (1)\n• index of diversity also takes into account the number of individuals of each species / evenness, so pond B would have a higher value (1)\nAccept: pond A is dominated by one species` },
      { q: `A student investigated the antimicrobial properties of garlic. She soaked paper discs in garlic extract made with ethanol, placed them straight onto an agar plate spread with bacteria and incubated the plate. A teacher said that the discs should have been left to dry before being placed on the agar.\n\nExplain why the discs should have been left to dry first.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• so that the ethanol evaporates (1)\n• otherwise ethanol may kill / inhibit the bacteria, so any clear zone may not be due to the garlic extract (1)\nAccept: ethanol is antimicrobial / is a disinfectant\nAccept: control disc soaked in ethanol only, dried, needed for comparison` },
      { q: `Students sampled ground beetles in a woodland using pitfall traps. The table shows the number of each species caught (data written for this practice question).\n\nSpecies | Number caught (n)\nA | 12\nB | 8\nC | 5\nD | 3\nE | 2\n\nThe index of diversity, D, can be calculated using the formula:\nD = N(N − 1) ÷ Σn(n − 1)\nwhere N is the total number of organisms of all species and n is the number of organisms of each species.\n\nCalculate the index of diversity for this woodland.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• N = 30 and N(N − 1) = 870 (1)\n• Σn(n − 1) = 132 + 56 + 20 + 6 + 2 = 216 (1)\n• D = 4.03 (1)\nAccept: 4.0 / 4.028\nCorrect answer with no working scores 3 marks\nTE for D from incorrect N(N − 1) or Σn(n − 1)` },
      { q: `In a population of field mice, a pale coat colour is caused by a recessive allele. In a survey, 9% of the mice had a pale coat.\n\nThe Hardy–Weinberg equations are:\np + q = 1\np² + 2pq + q² = 1\n\nCalculate the percentage of mice in this population that are heterozygous for the coat colour gene. Assume the population is in Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• q² = 0.09 so q = 0.3 (1)\n• p = 0.7 (1)\n• 2pq = 2 × 0.7 × 0.3 = 0.42, so 42 (%) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working scores 3 marks\nTE for 2pq from an incorrect q` },
      { q: `A student tested the tensile strength of fibres. She tied one end of a fibre to a clamp stand and hung a mass carrier from the other end. She added 50 g masses until the fibre broke and recorded the total mass. She tested one fibre from a nettle stem and one fibre from a flax stem. Fibres were chosen at random from fibres extracted by soaking the stems in water for different lengths of time.\n\nSuggest three improvements to this method.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• use smaller masses (e.g. 10 g) near the breaking point / add masses gradually (1)\n• repeat with several fibres of each type and calculate a mean (1)\n• use fibres of the same length (between the clamp and the mass) (1)\n• use fibres of the same diameter / cross-sectional area or measure it and calculate breaking stress (1)\n• extract fibres in the same way / soak for the same time / at the same temperature (1)\n• keep fibres at the same moisture content / humidity (1)\n• attach the fibre in the same way each time / use same knot (1)\nIgnore: safety goggles (not an improvement to validity)` },
      { q: `Wheat grains store starch, while the cell walls of wheat straw are made mainly of cellulose.\n\nCompare the structure of amylose and cellulose.`, marks: 3, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `• both are polymers of glucose joined by 1,4 glycosidic bonds (1)\n• amylose is made of α-glucose but cellulose is made of β-glucose (1)\n• amylose forms a coiled / helical chain but cellulose forms a straight chain (1)\n• in cellulose, alternate glucose molecules are inverted (1)\n• cellulose chains are linked by hydrogen bonds to form microfibrils, amylose is not (1)\nMax 3. Comparative statements required for marks 2, 3 and 5\nAccept: both unbranched` },
      { q: `The Merriam's kangaroo rat lives in deserts in North America and rarely drinks water. It has the following features:\n• it stays in a cool, humid burrow during the day and feeds at night\n• its kidneys have very long loops of Henle\n• it obtains most of its water from the respiration of the seeds it eats\n\nExplain how each of these features is an adaptation to living in a desert.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• behavioural: staying in a cool, humid burrow reduces water loss by evaporation / sweating / reduces need to cool (1)\n• anatomical: long loops of Henle allow production of small volumes of concentrated urine, so less water is lost (1)\n• physiological: metabolic water from respiration (of carbohydrates / fats) replaces water lost, so it does not need to drink (1)\nAccept: correct explanation without classifying the adaptation` },
      { q: `Scientists compared a 1000-base-pair sequence of mitochondrial DNA from four species of horseshoe bat (P, Q, R and S). The table shows the number of base differences between each pair of species (data written for this practice question).\n\nPair | Number of base differences\nP and Q | 86\nP and R | 21\nP and S | 90\nQ and R | 84\nQ and S | 12\nR and S | 88\n\nDeduce which two pairs of species are most closely related and explain your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `• Q and S (are most closely related) (1)\n• P and R (form the other closely related pair) (1)\n• fewer base differences indicate a more recent common ancestor / less time for mutations to accumulate (1)\nAccept: Q and S have the fewest differences (12)` },
      { q: `In 1990 Carl Woese proposed that all living organisms should be classified into three domains, based on differences in the base sequences of ribosomal RNA.\n\nExplain how the scientific community would evaluate this new classification before it was accepted.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• the work is submitted to a (scientific) journal and checked by peer review (1)\n• peer reviewers are independent experts / scientists working in the same field who check that the methods are valid and conclusions are supported by the data (1)\n• results are published so that other scientists can repeat / check the findings (1)\n• results are presented at scientific conferences for discussion (1)\n• other scientists gather further (molecular) evidence, e.g. from other genes / proteins, to support or reject the classification (1)\nIgnore: tested by the government` },
      { q: `An electron micrograph of a longitudinal section through a celery leaf stalk shows long, hollow tubes with no end walls. The walls of these tubes are thickened with lignin in a spiral pattern and contain many small unthickened areas called pits.\n\nExplain how these features of xylem vessels are related to their function.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• hollow / no end walls / no cytoplasm so water (and mineral ions) can flow as a continuous column with little resistance (1)\n• lignin strengthens the walls so the vessels do not collapse under tension (1)\nAccept: lignin makes walls waterproof\n• pits allow lateral movement of water (and ions) between vessels / to surrounding cells (1)\nAccept: spiral lignin allows the stem to bend / elongate\nIgnore: references to sugars / translocation` },
      { q: `A student examined a stained transverse section of a sunflower stem. She identified three types of cell in each vascular bundle: xylem vessels, phloem sieve tubes and sclerenchyma fibres.\n\nCompare the structure of sclerenchyma fibres with the structure of xylem vessels.`, marks: 3, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• both are made of dead cells / have no cytoplasm or contents (1)\n• both have walls thickened with lignin (1)\n• sclerenchyma fibres have end walls but xylem vessels do not (1)\n• sclerenchyma fibres have a narrower lumen / thicker walls than xylem vessels (1)\n• xylem vessels have pits, sclerenchyma fibres have few / no pits (1)\n• sclerenchyma fibres are tapered / pointed at the ends, xylem vessels are continuous tubes (1)\nComparative statements required` },
      { q: `A student investigated the antimicrobial properties of a thyme extract. A paper disc of diameter 6 mm soaked in the extract was placed on an agar plate spread with bacteria. After incubation, a circular clear zone of diameter 14 mm, centred on the disc, was measured.\n\nThe area of a circle is πr².\n\nCalculate the area of the clear zone around the disc, not including the area of the disc itself. Give your answer to three significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• radii of 7 mm and 3 mm used (1)\n• π × 7² − π × 3² = 153.9 − 28.3 (1)\n• 126 (mm²) (1)\nAccept: 125.7 rounded correctly to 126\nTE for an incorrect radius used correctly` },
      { q: `An electron micrograph of a cell from the leaf of a pea plant shows a large central vacuole surrounded by a membrane, many chloroplasts, and a thick outer layer outside the cell surface membrane. Thin channels pass through this outer layer into the neighbouring cell.\n\nExplain how three of the structures described identify this as a plant cell and not an animal cell, giving the function of each structure.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any three from (structure and function both needed for each mark):\n• cellulose cell wall, which provides strength / support / prevents bursting when turgid (1)\n• large permanent vacuole bounded by a tonoplast, which stores cell sap / maintains turgor (1)\n• chloroplasts, which carry out photosynthesis (1)\n• plasmodesmata, which allow transport / communication between adjacent cells (1)\nAccept: middle lamella / pits with correct function\nDo not accept: cell surface membrane / mitochondria / nucleus (present in animal cells too)` },
      { q: `Rock pocket mice live in the deserts of Arizona. Most areas have pale, sandy rocks, but some areas are covered by dark lava flows that formed thousands of years ago. On pale rocks most mice have sandy-coloured fur, while on the lava flows most mice have dark fur. Dark fur is caused by a mutation in the Mc1r gene. Owls hunt the mice by sight.\n\nExplain how the high frequency of dark fur arose in the mice living on the lava flows.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• (random) mutation (in Mc1r gene) produced a new allele for dark fur (1)\n• on dark lava, mice with dark fur are camouflaged / less likely to be seen and eaten by owls (1)\n• these mice are more likely to survive and reproduce (1)\n• passing on the dark fur allele to their offspring (1)\n• so frequency of the allele increases over many generations (1)\nMax 4\nDo not accept: mutation occurred in order to / because the mice needed camouflage` },
      { q: `A herd of 12 000 fallow deer lives in a large park. About 1 in every 2500 deer is born with a white coat, caused by a recessive allele.\n\nThe Hardy–Weinberg equations are:\np + q = 1\np² + 2pq + q² = 1\n\nCalculate the number of deer in the herd that would be expected to carry the allele for white coat but have a normal coat, and state one assumption made in your calculation.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• q² = 1 ÷ 2500 = 0.0004 so q = 0.02 (1)\n• p = 0.98 and 2pq = 0.0392 (1)\n• 0.0392 × 12 000 = 470 (deer) (1)\nAccept: 470.4 / 471\nTE for an incorrect frequency\n• assumption, any one from: large population / random mating / no selection for or against white coat / no migration / no mutation (1)` },
      { q: `A student compared the tensile strength of flax and nettle fibres of the same length. The table shows the mass needed to break each fibre (data written for this practice question).\n\nFibre | Mass needed to break the fibre / g\nFlax | 450, 470, 460, 250, 455\nNettle | 320, 340, 310, 335, 330\n\nThe student concluded that flax fibres are stronger than nettle fibres.\n\nEvaluate whether the data support this conclusion.`, marks: 4, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `• mean for flax (excluding anomaly) = 459 g compared with nettle 327 g (1)\nAccept: flax mean 417 g including anomaly\n• 250 g is an anomalous result / should be excluded or repeated (1)\n• the ranges (excluding the anomaly) do not overlap, so the difference is likely to be real (1)\n• but diameter / cross-sectional area of fibres not known / controlled, so cannot compare strength fairly (1)\n• small number of fibres tested / no statistical test carried out (1)\n• fibres from only one plant / one source of each type (1)\nMax 4. At least one supporting and one limiting point needed for full marks` },
      { q: `The scimitar-horned oryx was declared extinct in the wild in 2000. Zoos in several countries kept captive populations, and in 2016 captive-bred oryx began to be released into a reserve in Chad.\n\nExplain how zoos can make a captive breeding and reintroduction programme for an animal such as the oryx successful.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Any four from:\n• use of studbooks / records to choose unrelated animals for mating (1)\n• to maximise genetic diversity / reduce inbreeding (1)\n• exchange of animals / sperm between zoos, or use of IVF / artificial insemination / frozen embryos (1)\n• research into behaviour / reproduction / diet / disease to improve breeding success (1)\n• prepare animals for release, e.g. soft release / acclimatisation enclosures / reduce human contact (1)\n• restore / protect habitat and work with local people before release (1)\n• monitor released animals, e.g. with radio collars (1)\n• education of the public / raising funds (1)` },
      { q: `A student plans to investigate whether extracts of garlic and mint inhibit the growth of the bacterium Bacillus subtilis using agar plates.\n\nDescribe the aseptic techniques the student should use when setting up the agar plates.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any four from:\n• disinfect the work surface before and after (1)\n• work near a lit Bunsen burner / under an updraught (1)\n• flame the neck of the culture bottle / flame the inoculating loop or flame-sterilise the spreader with ethanol (1)\n• lift the Petri dish lid only slightly / at an angle and for a minimum time (1)\n• use sterile pipettes / forceps / sterile agar and Petri dishes (1)\n• tape the lid with two short strips, do not seal fully (1)\n• label the base of the dish, not the lid (1)\n• incubate at 25 °C (not 37 °C) (1)\nIgnore: wear gloves / goggles` },
      { q: `Digoxin is a drug obtained from foxglove plants and is used to treat heart failure. Before a new formulation of digoxin can be licensed, it must pass through a series of clinical trials.\n\nDescribe the three phases of clinical trials used to test a new drug.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• phase 1: small number of healthy volunteers, to test safety / side effects (1)\n• phase 2: small number of patients with the condition, to test effectiveness / find the best dose (1)\n• phase 3: large number of patients (hundreds or thousands) (1)\n• phase 3: randomised, compared with a placebo or an existing drug, double blind (1)\nAccept: pre-clinical testing on cells / tissues / animals before phase 1 as an additional point in place of any one above` },
      { q: `A student read that extracts of some plant leaves can kill bacteria. She wanted to find out whether mint leaves or sage leaves had the greater antimicrobial effect on the bacterium Micrococcus luteus. She had access to fresh leaves, ethanol, a pestle and mortar, filter paper discs, agar plates and a culture of the bacterium.\n\nDevise an investigation to find out which plant extract has the greater antimicrobial effect.`, marks: 5, command: 'Devise', markScheme: `• grind the same mass of each type of leaf with the same volume of ethanol and leave to stand / filter (1)\n• spread the same volume of bacterial culture evenly over the agar using a sterile spreader (1)\n• soak discs of the same size in each extract for the same time and allow the ethanol to evaporate (1)\n• control disc soaked in ethanol only (and dried) (1)\n• incubate the plates at 25 °C for the same time, e.g. 24–48 hours (1)\n• measure the diameter / calculate the area of the clear zone around each disc (1)\n• repeat with several discs / plates and calculate means (1)\n• aseptic technique described, e.g. flaming loop / lid lifted at an angle (1)\nMax 5` },
      { q: `Ecologists compared the beetle species in two hedgerows. The table shows the number of each species found (data written for this practice question).\n\nSpecies | Hedgerow A (n) | Hedgerow B (n)\nW | 10 | 34\nX | 10 | 2\nY | 10 | 2\nZ | 10 | 2\n\nThe index of diversity, D, can be calculated using the formula:\nD = N(N − 1) ÷ Σn(n − 1)\n\nCalculate the index of diversity for each hedgerow and explain what your answers show about the two hedgerows.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• N = 40 for both hedgerows, so N(N − 1) = 1560 (1)\n• hedgerow A: Σn(n − 1) = 360 and D = 4.33 (1)\n• hedgerow B: Σn(n − 1) = 1122 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 1128 and D = 1.38 (1)\nTE for an incorrect Σn(n − 1)\n• both hedgerows have the same species richness (four species) (1)\n• hedgerow A is more diverse because individuals are spread evenly between species / hedgerow B is dominated by species W (1)` },
      { q: `Some supermarkets now use carrier bags made from starch-based bioplastics instead of bags made from oil-based polythene. The starch is obtained from maize grains.\n\nEvaluate the use of starch-based bioplastics in terms of sustainability.`, marks: 5, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Any five from, at least one advantage and one disadvantage for full marks:\n• maize is a renewable resource / can be regrown, whereas oil is non-renewable / finite (1)\n• bioplastics are biodegradable so produce less long-term waste / landfill / plastic pollution (1)\n• carbon dioxide released when they decompose / are burned was recently absorbed by photosynthesis, so they are closer to carbon neutral (1)\n• growing maize uses land that could be used to grow food (1)\n• growing and processing maize still uses fossil fuels for machinery / fertilisers / transport (1)\n• fertiliser use can cause eutrophication / pesticides harm wildlife / habitat loss for crops (1)\n• bioplastics may be weaker / degrade during use / break down slowly in landfill without oxygen (1)\n• biodegradation in landfill may release methane (1)` },
      { q: `*The Ascension Island spurge is a rare plant found only on one island. Fewer than 50 wild plants are known and they are threatened by introduced grazing animals and invasive species. Conservationists are considering storing seeds of this plant in a seed bank and growing plants in a botanic garden for possible reintroduction.\n\nDiscuss the role of seed banks and botanic gardens in the conservation of this species.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 seeds collected from many different plants / sites to maximise genetic diversity\nIC2 seeds are dried and stored at low temperature so they remain viable for a long time\nIC3 seeds are tested regularly for viability / samples are germinated and new seeds collected\nIC4 seed bank provides an insurance against extinction in the wild / large number of seeds stored in a small space\nIC5 plants grown in botanic gardens / from seeds can be reintroduced to the island or used to restore habitat\nIC6 research into germination conditions / growth requirements / plant breeding, and education of the public\nIC7 limitations: stored seeds do not evolve / adapt to changing conditions in the wild\nIC8 limitations: some seeds cannot be dried or frozen (recalcitrant) / small number of parent plants means low genetic diversity / inbreeding\nIC9 reintroduction fails unless threats such as grazing animals and invasive species are removed first\n\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 or more IC points = 4 marks; 5–4 = 3 marks; 3–2 = 2 marks; 1 = 1 mark; 0 = 0 marks\nLinkages and sustained reasoning: sustained reasoning throughout, with benefits weighed against limitations = 2 marks; some linked reasoning = 1 mark; none = 0 marks` },
      { q: `*Hemp has been grown for thousands of years to make rope, sacking and textiles. The stem contains long sclerenchyma fibres and xylem vessels. The cellulose microfibrils in the cell walls of these cells are laid down in layers at different angles, and the walls contain lignin.\n\nExplain how the structure of the cells in a hemp stem, and the arrangement of cellulose in their walls, make hemp stems a useful source of strong fibres.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 cellulose is a straight chain of β-glucose linked by 1,4 glycosidic bonds\nIC2 hydrogen bonds between parallel cellulose chains form microfibrils with high tensile strength\nIC3 microfibrils are embedded in a matrix (of hemicellulose / pectin) forming a composite material\nIC4 microfibrils in different layers at different angles give strength in more than one direction / resist bending and twisting\nIC5 lignin in secondary thickening adds strength / rigidity / prevents collapse\nIC6 sclerenchyma fibres are long cells with thick walls and narrow lumen, overlapping at tapered ends\nIC7 xylem vessels are long, continuous lignified tubes\nIC8 sclerenchyma and xylem cells are dead, so fibres can be extracted (by retting) without the contents decaying the fibre\nIC9 fibres run along the length of the stem, giving long fibres suitable for spinning into rope\n\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 or more IC points = 4 marks; 5–4 = 3 marks; 3–2 = 2 marks; 1 = 1 mark; 0 = 0 marks\nLinkages and sustained reasoning: sustained reasoning linking molecular structure to cell structure to use = 2 marks; some linked reasoning = 1 mark; none = 0 marks` },
      { q: `*A clothing company wants to know whether fibres extracted from banana plant stems or from pineapple leaves are stronger. A student has samples of both plants, a clamp stand, string, a mass carrier, a set of 10 g and 50 g masses and a micrometer.\n\nDevise an investigation to compare the tensile strength of fibres from these two plants.`, marks: 6, command: 'Devise', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 extract fibres from each plant in the same way, e.g. soak (ret) in water for the same time then pull fibres from the tissue\nIC2 use fibres of the same length between the clamp and the mass carrier\nIC3 measure the diameter with a micrometer and select fibres of the same diameter / calculate cross-sectional area\nIC4 attach one end to the clamp and the other to the mass carrier, both tied in the same way\nIC5 add masses one at a time / 10 g masses near the breaking point, until the fibre breaks\nIC6 record the total mass (or weight in N) at which the fibre breaks\nIC7 repeat with at least five fibres of each type and calculate a mean\nIC8 keep fibres at the same moisture content / humidity / temperature\nIC9 use a statistical test (e.g. t-test) to see if the difference between means is significant\nIC10 safety: goggles / place a tray or soft surface under the masses / keep feet clear\n\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 or more IC points = 4 marks; 5–4 = 3 marks; 3–2 = 2 marks; 1 = 1 mark; 0 = 0 marks\nLinkages and sustained reasoning: a logically sequenced method with reasons for controls = 2 marks; some linked reasoning = 1 mark; none = 0 marks` }
    ]
  },
  'S5': {
    name: 'On the Wild Side',
    questions: [
      { q: `An electron micrograph of a chloroplast from a leaf palisade cell shows stacks of flattened membrane sacs surrounded by a fluid-filled matrix that also contains starch grains and lipid droplets.\n\nName the part of the chloroplast where the Calvin cycle takes place.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `stroma (1)\nDo not accept: thylakoid / granum / grana / stoma` },
      { q: `In the light-independent reactions of photosynthesis, carbon dioxide from the air combines with a five-carbon compound. The product immediately splits into two molecules of a three-carbon compound.\n\nName the enzyme that catalyses this reaction.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `RUBISCO / ribulose bisphosphate carboxylase(-oxygenase) (1)\nIgnore: incorrect capitalisation` },
      { q: `A student separated the pigments from a nettle leaf extract by thin-layer chromatography. The solvent front moved 6.0 cm from the origin. A yellow spot moved 4.2 cm from the origin.\n\nCalculate the Rf value of the yellow pigment.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `0.70 / 0.7 (1)\nDo not accept: a value with units, or 1.43 (inverted ratio)` },
      { q: `In 1963 a new volcanic island, Surtsey, formed off the south coast of Iceland. Scientists have recorded the plant and animal community there ever since. After many decades, a community may develop that remains stable over time unless it is disturbed.\n\nGive the term used for this stable final community.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `climax community (1)\nAccept: climax\nDo not accept: pioneer community / sere / ecosystem` },
      { q: `On a rocky shore, oystercatchers feed on mussels and limpets. Mussels and limpets feed on algae growing on the rocks. Gulls sometimes eat young oystercatchers.\n\nState the trophic level occupied by the oystercatcher in the food chain algae → limpet → oystercatcher → gull.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `secondary consumer / third trophic level / trophic level 3 (1)\nAccept: carnivore at the third trophic level\nDo not accept: primary consumer / tertiary consumer` },
      { q: `Scientists drilled a core 3 km deep into the Antarctic ice sheet. The ice contains small bubbles of air that were trapped when the snow was compressed into ice thousands of years ago.\n\nGive one measurement that can be made on the air in these bubbles that is used as evidence of past climate.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `(concentration of) carbon dioxide / methane (in the trapped air) (1)\nAccept: ratio of oxygen isotopes / ¹⁸O : ¹⁶O ratio / deuterium ratio (as an indicator of past temperature)\nDo not accept: temperature of the bubbles / amount of air` },
      { q: `The absorption spectrum of chlorophyll a shows two peaks, at about 430 nm (blue) and 662 nm (red). Absorption between 500 nm and 600 nm is very low.\n\nExplain why the leaves of most plants appear green.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `chlorophyll (pigments) absorb (mainly) red and blue light (1)\ngreen light is (not absorbed so is) reflected / transmitted (to the eye) (1)\nDo not accept: chlorophyll absorbs green light` },
      { q: `Ecologists measured energy flow in a grassland in Wales (data written for this practice question).\nGross primary productivity (GPP) = 21 000 kJ m⁻² year⁻¹\nEnergy lost by the plants in respiration = 12 600 kJ m⁻² year⁻¹\n\nCalculate the net primary productivity (NPP) of this grassland. Give your answer with units.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `21 000 − 12 600 / NPP = GPP − R (1)\n8400 kJ m⁻² year⁻¹ (1)\nCorrect answer with units gains 2 marks; 8400 without units gains 1 mark` },
      { q: `Brine shrimp, Artemia salina, live in salt lakes. A student measured the rate of hatching of brine shrimp eggs at different temperatures (data written for this practice question).\nMean rate of hatching at 25 °C = 1.8 eggs hour⁻¹\nMean rate of hatching at 35 °C = 4.1 eggs hour⁻¹\nThe temperature coefficient, Q₁₀ = rate at (T + 10) °C ÷ rate at T °C\n\nCalculate the Q₁₀ for the hatching of brine shrimp eggs between 25 °C and 35 °C.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `4.1 ÷ 1.8 (1)\n2.3 / 2.28 (1)\nCorrect answer with no working gains 2 marks\nDo not accept: 0.44 (inverted ratio) for the second mark` },
      { q: `In the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis, water molecules are split inside the thylakoid space.\n\nDescribe what happens to the products of this splitting of water.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any two from:\nelectrons replace those lost from (chlorophyll in) photosystem II (1)\nprotons / hydrogen ions (accumulate in thylakoid space and) are used to reduce NADP / produce reduced NADP (1)\noxygen is released as a waste product / diffuses out of the leaf / is used in respiration (1)\nAccept: H⁺ build up a proton gradient used for ATP synthesis, as an alternative to the NADP point\nDo not accept: hydrogen atoms reduce NADP without reference to electrons and protons combining` },
      { q: `Dendrochronologists measured the width of the annual growth rings in a cross-section of an oak tree felled in 2019. The rings formed between 1975 and 1985 were narrower than those formed between 1995 and 2005.\n\nExplain how the width of the growth rings can be used as evidence of past temperature.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `warmer conditions increase (the rate of) photosynthesis / growth (1)\nso wider rings form in warmer years / narrow rings indicate cooler years (1)\nAccept: each ring is one year's growth, so the ring can be dated to a known year\nIgnore: references to rainfall unless linked to growth` },
      { q: `The dog whelk, Nucella lapillus, is a carnivorous snail found on rocky shores in Britain. Its abundance changes from the low-tide mark to the high-tide mark.\n\nSuggest two abiotic factors that could affect the distribution of dog whelks on the shore.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any two from:\nexposure to air / time out of water / desiccation (1)\ntemperature (1)\nwave action / degree of exposure to waves (1)\nsalinity (1)\noxygen concentration (of rock pools) (1)\ntype of substrate / rock surface (1)\nDo not accept: biotic factors such as prey, predators or competition` },
      { q: `Pollen grains from different plant species have distinctive shapes. Pollen found deep in peat bogs can be several thousand years old but is still well preserved.\n\nExplain why pollen grains are preserved in peat bogs.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `peat bogs are anaerobic / waterlogged / acidic (1)\nso decomposers / bacteria / fungi cannot (easily) survive / activity of decomposers is reduced (1)\nAccept: pollen has a tough outer wall that resists decay, for one mark only if conditions are not described` },
      { q: `Two populations of a fish species, Gasterosteus aculeatus (three-spined stickleback), live in the same lake in Canada. One population feeds near the lake bottom, the other feeds in open water. Fish from the two populations rarely mate with each other in the wild.\n\nSuggest how biologists could confirm that the two populations have become separate species.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `breed fish from the two populations together (in captivity) (1)\nif they do not produce (viable) fertile offspring they are separate species (1)\nAccept: compare DNA base sequences / protein amino acid sequences and find large differences, for one mark` },
      { q: `A group of students wanted to estimate the percentage cover of daisies in a school playing field. They decided to place 20 quadrats at random positions using a pair of tape measures laid at right angles and a random number generator.\n\nExplain why the quadrats should be placed at random positions.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `to avoid (sampling) bias / students choosing areas with more daisies (1)\nso that the sample is representative (of the whole field) / so that results are valid (1)\nDo not accept: to make it fair / to make it accurate` },
      { q: `A student investigated the effect of temperature on the hatching of brine shrimp eggs. She placed 0.1 g of eggs into beakers of salt water kept in water baths at 15 °C, 20 °C, 25 °C, 30 °C and 35 °C, and counted the number of hatched larvae after 48 hours.\n\nGive two variables, other than mass of eggs, that she should control.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two from:\nconcentration of salt / salinity (1)\nvolume of water (1)\nlight intensity / exposure to light (1)\npH of the water (1)\naeration / oxygen concentration (1)\nsource / age / batch of eggs (1)\nDo not accept: temperature / time (time already stated) / amount of water without "volume"` },
      { q: `Large areas of rainforest in Indonesia are cleared by burning to make space for oil palm plantations.\n\nExplain how this deforestation leads to an increase in the concentration of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `combustion (of trees) releases carbon dioxide (1)\nfewer trees so less carbon dioxide is removed (from the atmosphere) by photosynthesis (1)\nAccept: microorganisms decompose remaining plant material, releasing carbon dioxide by respiration\nAccept: loss of carbon sink` },
      { q: `In a lake ecosystem the food chain is phytoplankton → zooplankton → perch → pike. Ecologists found that only about 10% of the energy in the zooplankton is transferred to the biomass of the perch.\n\nExplain why so little of the energy in the zooplankton becomes part of the biomass of the perch.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any three from:\nnot all of the zooplankton is eaten (by perch) (1)\nsome parts are not digested / are lost as faeces (1)\nenergy is lost in excretion / urea / ammonia (1)\nenergy is used in respiration / for movement / to maintain body processes (1)\n(respiration) releases energy as heat (1)\nDo not accept: energy is used up / energy is made into heat by movement without respiration` },
      { q: `Chlorella is a single-celled alga. Scientists supplied Chlorella cells with carbon dioxide containing radioactive carbon, ¹⁴C, and then measured the radioactivity in different compounds over time. Radioactivity appeared first in glycerate 3-phosphate (GP) and then in glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate (GALP).\n\nDescribe how GP is converted to GALP and how ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP) is regenerated in the Calvin cycle.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `GP is reduced to GALP (1)\nusing reduced NADP (from the light-dependent reactions) (1)\nusing ATP (for both reduction and regeneration of RuBP) (1)\n(most) GALP is used to regenerate RuBP / five out of every six GALP molecules regenerate RuBP (1)\nmax 3\nAccept: NADPH / NADPH₂ for reduced NADP\nDo not accept: GP is oxidised` },
      { q: `A student measured the rate of oxygen production by a piece of Cabomba (pondweed) at different light intensities, at two carbon dioxide concentrations (data written for this practice question).\nAt 0.01% CO₂ the rate increased from 0 to 12 bubbles min⁻¹ as light intensity increased from 0 to 30 arbitrary units, then stayed at 12 bubbles min⁻¹ up to 100 arbitrary units.\nAt 0.04% CO₂ the rate increased to 28 bubbles min⁻¹ at 60 arbitrary units, then stayed at 28 bubbles min⁻¹ up to 100 arbitrary units.\n\nExplain these results.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `at low light intensities light (intensity) is the limiting factor (1)\nat 0.01% CO₂ the rate levels off above 30 units because carbon dioxide (concentration) becomes the limiting factor (1)\nincreasing CO₂ concentration increases the rate of the Calvin cycle / carbon fixation by RUBISCO (so more GP / GALP formed) (1)\nabove 60 units at 0.04% CO₂ another factor such as temperature / carbon dioxide is limiting (1)\nmax 3\nDo not accept: light is no longer needed` },
      { q: `A student wanted to separate the photosynthetic pigments from spinach leaves using thin-layer chromatography (TLC).\n\nDescribe how she should prepare and run the chromatogram.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any three from:\ngrind leaves with (a small volume of) propanone / solvent (and sand) to extract pigments (1)\ndraw a pencil origin line (about 1–2 cm from the bottom of the TLC plate) (1)\nspot extract onto the origin repeatedly, drying between applications, to give a small concentrated spot (1)\nplace plate in a (closed) container with solvent below the origin line (1)\nremove when solvent nearly reaches the top and mark the solvent front (1)\nDo not accept: using pen to draw the origin line` },
      { q: `A newspaper article claimed that a temperature record from a single weather station in central London showed that global warming is happening, because the mean annual temperature there rose by 1.5 °C between 1960 and 2020.\n\nExplain why this record alone is not reliable evidence for global warming.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `any three from:\nit is only one location so may not be representative of the whole Earth (1)\nurban areas are warmer due to buildings / traffic / heat island effect (1)\nthe area around the station may have changed (e.g. more building) over time (1)\nmeasuring equipment / methods / site may have changed over time (1)\n60 years is a short period compared with natural climate variation (1)\nneed many records from around the world / other evidence such as ice cores, to see if a global trend exists (1)` },
      { q: `Analysis of an Antarctic ice core showed that, over the past 400 000 years, the concentration of carbon dioxide in the trapped air rose and fell at the same times as the temperature estimated from isotope ratios in the ice.\n\nComment on whether these data show that increasing carbon dioxide concentration causes increasing global temperature.`, marks: 3, command: 'Comment on', markScheme: `there is a (positive) correlation between CO₂ concentration and temperature (1)\ncorrelation does not prove causation / a causal link (1)\nother factors could be involved such as changes in solar output / Earth's orbit / methane concentration (1)\nincreased temperature could cause the increase in CO₂ / both could be caused by another factor (1)\nhowever, there is a known mechanism: CO₂ is a greenhouse gas that absorbs infrared radiation (1)\nmax 3` },
      { q: `Brine shrimp eggs contain enzymes that break down stored glycogen to provide energy for hatching. Researchers found that the percentage of eggs hatching increased up to 30 °C, but fell sharply at 35 °C.\n\nExplain why the percentage of eggs hatching fell at 35 °C.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(high temperature causes) bonds / hydrogen bonds in the enzyme to break (1)\ntertiary structure / shape of active site changes / enzyme is denatured (1)\nfewer enzyme–substrate complexes form / substrate no longer fits (1)\nso less energy (from respiration) available for development / hatching (1)\nmax 3\nDo not accept: enzymes are killed` },
      { q: `In the Netherlands, the date at which oak leaves open (bud burst) has become earlier as spring temperatures have risen. Winter moth caterpillars feed on young oak leaves. Their eggs hatch in response to temperature, but the date of hatching has not changed as much as the date of bud burst. Pied flycatchers feed their nestlings on these caterpillars.\n\nSuggest how these changes could affect the population of pied flycatchers.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `caterpillars may hatch after the young leaves have become tough / unpalatable so fewer caterpillars survive (1)\npeak caterpillar numbers may no longer coincide with when flycatcher nestlings need feeding (1)\nso less food for nestlings / fewer nestlings survive / lower breeding success (1)\nso flycatcher population decreases (unless the birds breed earlier) (1)\nmax 3\nAccept: flycatchers with earlier breeding may be selected for, for one mark` },
      { q: `ATP synthase molecules are found in the thylakoid membranes of chloroplasts.\n\nExplain how ATP is produced in the thylakoid during the light-dependent reactions.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(as electrons pass along the electron transport chain) energy is used to pump protons / H⁺ into the thylakoid space (1)\ncreating a proton / H⁺ concentration gradient (1)\nprotons diffuse back through ATP synthase into the stroma (1)\n(energy released) is used to join ADP and Pi / inorganic phosphate (1)\nmax 3\nAccept: chemiosmosis for the gradient point only if described\nDo not accept: ATP is made from energy, without reference to ADP and Pi` },
      { q: `About 3 million years ago, the Isthmus of Panama formed and separated the sea into the Caribbean and the Pacific. Populations of snapping shrimp, Alpheus, on either side of the isthmus are now separate species.\n\nExplain how the formation of the isthmus could have led to the formation of new species of Alpheus.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `populations were geographically isolated / no gene flow between them (1)\ndifferent selection pressures / mutations on each side (1)\ndifferent alleles selected / allele frequencies change in each population (1)\nover time populations become reproductively isolated / can no longer interbreed to produce fertile offspring (1)\nmax 3\nAccept: allopatric speciation as a named process, but it does not score alone` },
      { q: `The first organisms to colonise the bare lava of Surtsey were lichens and mosses. After 50 years, the island had grasses, small flowering plants and colonies of nesting seabirds.\n\nDescribe how succession leads to the development of a community on bare rock such as this.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any three from:\npioneer species (lichens / mosses) colonise first (1)\nthey break down rock / when they die they decompose and form (thin) soil / humus (1)\nconditions change, allowing other species to colonise / which may outcompete the pioneers (1)\nincreasing biodiversity / larger plants / more complex community (1)\neventually a climax community forms (1)\nDo not accept: pioneer species decide to leave` },
      { q: `A student investigated whether the number of rushes, Juncus effusus, was correlated with distance from a stream. He placed a 1 m² quadrat at 2 m intervals along a transect (data written for this practice question).\n\nDistance from stream / m: 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14\nNumber of rushes: 34, 29, 31, 18, 20, 11, 7, 9\n\nThe Spearman rank correlation coefficient is given by rs = 1 − (6Σd²) ÷ (n(n² − 1))\nThe critical value for n = 8 at p = 0.05 is 0.738.\n\nCalculate rs and use your answer to state a conclusion.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `correct ranking of both variables (1)\nΣd² = 162 (1)\nrs = −0.93 / −0.929 (1)\n|rs| / 0.93 is greater than the critical value of 0.738, so there is a significant (negative) correlation (between distance from the stream and number of rushes) / reject null hypothesis (1)\nTE: allow conclusion consistent with their calculated value\nAccept: ranks in either direction provided consistent (rs = +0.93 if one variable ranked in reverse, with correctly worded conclusion)\nDo not accept: distance causes the decrease` },
      { q: `A student investigated the effect of light intensity on the rate of photosynthesis in leaf discs from a spinach leaf. Leaf discs float when oxygen accumulates in the air spaces.\n\nDescribe a method she could use to carry out this investigation.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `any four from:\nremove air from discs (using a syringe) in sodium hydrogencarbonate solution so that they sink (1)\nplace discs in the same volume / concentration of sodium hydrogencarbonate solution (1)\nvary light intensity by changing distance of lamp from discs (1)\nrecord time taken for (a number of) discs to float / number floating after a fixed time (1)\ncontrol temperature using a water bath / heat filter / LED lamp (1)\nrepeat at each distance / at least five distances and calculate a mean (1)\nAccept: 1 ÷ time as a measure of rate\nDo not accept: control the amount of light` },
      { q: `The table shows the percentage of brine shrimp eggs that hatched after 48 hours at different temperatures. 200 eggs were used at each temperature (data written for this practice question).\n\nTemperature / °C: 15, 20, 25, 30, 35\nNumber of eggs hatched: 24, 92, 158, 170, 46\n\nAnalyse the data to describe and explain the effect of temperature on the hatching of brine shrimp eggs.`, marks: 4, command: 'Analyse', markScheme: `percentage hatching calculated: 12%, 46%, 79%, 85%, 23% (at least two correct) (1)\nhatching increases from 15 °C to 30 °C / optimum at about 30 °C (in this range) (1)\nbecause molecules have more kinetic energy so more enzyme–substrate collisions / faster metabolism (1)\nhatching decreases above 30 °C / sharp fall at 35 °C (1)\nbecause enzymes denature (at high temperature) (1)\nmax 4\nAccept: the true optimum may lie between 25 °C and 35 °C because intervals are 5 °C` },
      { q: `An ecologist wanted to compare the distribution of heather, Calluna vulgaris, from the edge of a pine forest into an open moorland.\n\nDevise a sampling method she could use to collect valid data.`, marks: 4, command: 'Devise', markScheme: `any four from:\nlay a (line / belt) transect (tape) from the forest into the moorland (1)\nplace quadrats at regular intervals along the transect / e.g. every 2 m (1)\nrecord percentage cover / frequency / ACFOR abundance of heather in each quadrat (1)\nuse the same size of quadrat throughout (1)\nrepeat with several transects (placed at random positions along the forest edge) (1)\nmeasure abiotic factors such as light intensity / soil pH / soil moisture at each quadrat (1)\nDo not accept: random quadrats only, without a transect, for the first point` },
      { q: `Some governments encourage the use of biofuels, such as bioethanol made from sugar cane, as a replacement for petrol. Supporters say these fuels are carbon neutral.\n\nDiscuss whether using bioethanol from sugar cane reduces the concentration of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.`, marks: 4, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `for: CO₂ released on burning was (recently) absorbed by photosynthesis in the sugar cane (1)\nfor: replaces fossil fuels so less carbon released from long-term stores (1)\nagainst: fossil fuels used in growing / fertilisers / harvesting / transport / processing release CO₂ (1)\nagainst: forests may be cleared to plant sugar cane, releasing CO₂ and removing a carbon sink (1)\nagainst: fermentation releases CO₂ / net reduction depends on the whole life cycle (1)\nmax 4, must include at least one point for and one point against for full marks` },
      { q: `Ecologists measured energy flow in a temperate grassland (data written for this practice question).\nLight energy reaching the grass = 1 700 000 kJ m⁻² year⁻¹\nGross primary productivity (GPP) = 20 400 kJ m⁻² year⁻¹\nEnergy used by grass in respiration = 11 900 kJ m⁻² year⁻¹\nEnergy in the biomass of primary consumers = 850 kJ m⁻² year⁻¹\n\nCalculate the efficiency of energy transfer from the grass (NPP) to the primary consumers and the percentage of light energy that becomes GPP. Show your working.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `NPP = 20 400 − 11 900 = 8500 (kJ m⁻² year⁻¹) (1)\n850 ÷ 8500 × 100 (1)\n= 10% (1)\n20 400 ÷ 1 700 000 × 100 = 1.2% (1)\nTE: allow efficiency calculated using their NPP\nDo not accept: 4.2% (850 ÷ 20 400 × 100) for the efficiency marks` },
      { q: `Mutant plants of Arabidopsis thaliana lack a functional photosystem II but still contain photosystem I. These plants can make some ATP in the light but cannot produce reduced NADP or release oxygen.\n\nDescribe the light-dependent reactions in normal plants and use your description to explain the observations on these mutant plants.`, marks: 5, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `light energy excites electrons in chlorophyll in photosystem II (1)\nelectrons pass along an electron carrier chain to photosystem I, releasing energy used to make ATP (1)\nlight excites electrons in photosystem I, which (with H⁺) reduce NADP (1)\nphotolysis of water (at PSII) replaces electrons and releases oxygen (1)\nmutants: no photolysis so no oxygen release / no electrons supplied to PSI (from water) so no reduced NADP (1)\nmutants: ATP made by cyclic photophosphorylation, where electrons from PSI return to it via the carrier chain (1)\nmax 5` },
      { q: `A student set up an investigation into the effect of temperature on the hatching of brine shrimp eggs. She used one beaker at each temperature. She put a "pinch" of eggs into each beaker of tap water mixed with a spoonful of sea salt. She placed the beakers on windowsills in different rooms of the school with temperatures of 16 °C, 19 °C and 24 °C. After 24 hours she counted the larvae she could see by eye, and concluded that 24 °C was the optimum temperature for hatching.\n\nEvaluate her method and conclusion.`, marks: 5, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `any five from:\nmass / number of eggs not controlled – weigh a fixed mass (e.g. 0.1 g) (1)\nsalt concentration / volume of water not controlled – use measured mass of salt and volume of water (1)\ntemperature not controlled / rooms fluctuate – use thermostatically controlled water baths (1)\nlight intensity / other conditions vary between rooms (1)\nno repeats – repeat at least three times at each temperature and calculate a mean (1)\ncounting by eye is inaccurate – use a hand lens / sample a known volume of water (1)\nonly three temperatures / narrow range – cannot conclude an optimum because the highest temperature gave the highest hatching (1)\n24 hours may be too short for hatching to be complete (1)\nmax 5` },
      { q: `Scientists took a core from a peat bog in northern England and identified the pollen grains in layers of different ages (data written for this practice question).\n\nAge of layer / years before present: 10 500, 9000, 7000, 5000, 3000\nBirch pollen / % of total: 62, 38, 15, 10, 12\nOak pollen / % of total: 2, 18, 41, 44, 28\nGrass pollen / % of total: 25, 14, 8, 9, 35\n\nBirch tolerates cold conditions. Oak requires warmer summers.\n\nAnalyse the data to deduce what happened to the climate and vegetation near the bog over this period.`, marks: 5, command: 'Analyse', markScheme: `birch pollen decreases from 62% to 10% / 12% (between 10 500 and 5000 years ago) (1)\noak pollen increases from 2% to 44% (by 5000 years ago) (1)\nsuggests the climate became warmer (allowing oak to replace birch) (1)\nhigh grass pollen 3000 years ago and fall in oak suggest woodland was cleared (by humans / for farming) / climate cooled (1)\ncaution: pollen can be carried long distances by wind / some species produce more pollen than others, so percentages may not reflect abundance (1)\nidea that peat layers are dated (e.g. by radiocarbon dating) (1)\nmax 5\nAccept: quoted data must include figures for credit on the first two points` },
      { q: `*Plants in a tropical forest capture about 1–2% of the light energy reaching their leaves and store it in the chemical energy of glucose and other organic molecules.\n\nExplain how light energy is converted into chemical energy in glucose in a chloroplast.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 light absorbed by (chlorophyll in) photosystems in thylakoid membranes\nIC2 electrons excited / raised to a higher energy level and leave chlorophyll\nIC3 electrons pass along electron transport chain, energy used to pump H⁺ into thylakoid space, ATP made by ATP synthase (photophosphorylation)\nIC4 photolysis of water provides electrons and H⁺, releasing oxygen\nIC5 NADP reduced (at photosystem I) by electrons and H⁺\nIC6 in the stroma, CO₂ combines with RuBP, catalysed by RUBISCO, forming GP\nIC7 GP reduced to GALP using ATP and reduced NADP; GALP used to make glucose and to regenerate RuBP\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 IC → 4; 5–4 IC → 3; 3–2 IC → 2; 1 IC → 1\nReasoning: 2 = a sustained line linking the products of the light-dependent reactions (ATP and reduced NADP) to their use in the Calvin cycle; 1 = some linkage; 0 = isolated points` },
      { q: `*A report on climate change presented three sources of evidence (data written for this practice question):\n• Ice cores: atmospheric CO₂ concentration was between 180 and 280 ppm for 800 000 years, then rose to over 400 ppm after 1850.\n• Temperature records: global mean surface temperature has risen by about 1.1 °C since 1880, recorded at thousands of land and sea stations.\n• Dendrochronology: ring widths in bristlecone pines show variation linked to temperature over 4000 years.\n\nEvaluate the extent to which these data support the conclusion that human activity is causing global warming.`, marks: 6, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 rise in CO₂ after 1850 coincides with industrialisation / burning of fossil fuels / deforestation\nIC2 current CO₂ level is above natural range shown in the ice core record\nIC3 CO₂ is a greenhouse gas: absorbs / re-emits infrared radiation, so a mechanism exists\nIC4 temperature records from many locations give a global, reliable trend\nIC5 correlation between CO₂ and temperature does not prove causation\nIC6 early temperature records less reliable / fewer stations / methods changed; ice core and tree ring data are proxy measures\nIC7 tree rings are affected by other factors such as rainfall, so are less direct evidence of temperature\nIC8 natural factors (solar activity, volcanic activity, orbital variation) also affect climate\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 IC → 4; 5–4 IC → 3; 3–2 IC → 2; 1 IC → 1\nReasoning: 2 = a balanced judgement weighing strengths and limitations of the evidence; 1 = some linkage; 0 = isolated points` },
      { q: `*A government is deciding how to spend a fixed budget to reduce carbon dioxide emissions. Proposal A: restore a 2000-hectare drained peat bog in Scotland by blocking drainage ditches, which would stop the release of stored carbon and provide habitat for rare species such as the golden plover. Proposal B: plant 2000 hectares of fast-growing non-native Sitka spruce on farmland for timber and biofuel. Reports on the proposals were written by a conservation charity and by a forestry company.\n\nDiscuss the scientific and ethical issues the government should consider when deciding between these proposals.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 drained peat oxidises / decomposes aerobically, releasing CO₂; rewetting makes it anaerobic so decomposition slows and carbon is stored\nIC2 young spruce trees take up CO₂ rapidly by photosynthesis while growing (carbon sink)\nIC3 carbon in timber stored long term but biofuel carbon is released again on burning; harvesting / processing releases CO₂\nIC4 peat bog restoration conserves biodiversity / rare species; spruce monoculture has low biodiversity\nIC5 farmland is lost for food production; effect on farmers' livelihoods / local economy\nIC6 evidence may be biased depending on who reports it (charity vs company) / funding source / need peer-reviewed data\nIC7 ethical frameworks: duty to protect species and future generations vs benefit to most people (utilitarian) / economic benefit of timber\nIC8 timescale: benefits of each proposal occur over different periods; uncertainty in predictions\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 IC → 4; 5–4 IC → 3; 3–2 IC → 2; 1 IC → 1\nReasoning: 2 = a sustained discussion weighing scientific evidence against ethical viewpoints, reaching a reasoned judgement; 1 = some linkage; 0 = isolated points` }
    ]
  },
  'S6': {
    name: 'Immunity, Infection and Forensics',
    questions: [
      { q: `A forensic scientist has only a tiny sample of skin cells from a crime scene. She uses the polymerase chain reaction (PCR) to make many copies of the DNA. In each cycle the mixture is heated to 95 °C, cooled to 55 °C and then held at 72 °C.\n\nName the enzyme that joins nucleotides together at 72 °C.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `(Taq) DNA polymerase (1)\nAccept thermostable / heat-stable DNA polymerase\nDo not accept RNA polymerase / DNA ligase / polymerase alone` },
      { q: `In gel electrophoresis, DNA fragments are loaded into wells at one end of an agarose gel and a potential difference is applied across the gel. The fragments move towards the positive electrode.\n\nGive the reason why DNA fragments move towards the positive electrode.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `DNA is negatively charged because of its phosphate groups (1)\nAccept phosphates (in the backbone) carry a negative charge\nIgnore DNA is attracted to the anode without reference to charge` },
      { q: `Mycobacterium tuberculosis is inhaled in droplets and reaches the alveoli. It is engulfed by phagocytes but is not destroyed, and can remain dormant inside these cells for many years.\n\nName the type of phagocyte in which Mycobacterium tuberculosis can survive.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `macrophage (1)\nAccept alveolar macrophage\nDo not accept neutrophil / white blood cell alone / lymphocyte` },
      { q: `HIV is a retrovirus. Its genetic material is RNA, but after it enters a T helper cell a DNA copy of its genome is inserted into the host cell's DNA.\n\nName the enzyme that HIV uses to make DNA from its RNA.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `reverse transcriptase (1)\nDo not accept integrase / DNA polymerase / RNA polymerase` },
      { q: `A student investigated the effect of different antibiotics on the growth of a non-pathogenic strain of bacteria grown on nutrient agar plates. After adding the antibiotic discs, the student taped the lid on, labelled the plate and incubated it at 25 °C rather than at 37 °C.\n\nGive the reason why the plate was incubated at 25 °C rather than at 37 °C.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `to reduce the risk of growing (human) pathogens (1)\nAccept 37 °C is body temperature so pathogens / harmful bacteria would grow\nIgnore "for safety" alone / "bacteria would die at 37 °C"` },
      { q: `Tears contain the enzyme lysozyme. People with a disorder that reduces tear production have more frequent bacterial eye infections.\n\nState how lysozyme protects the eye against bacteria.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `it breaks down / hydrolyses bacterial cell walls (causing lysis) (1)\nAccept digests peptidoglycan / murein (cell wall)\nAccept breaks glycosidic bonds in bacterial cell walls\nDo not accept breaks down cell membranes / kills viruses` },
      { q: `A short tandem repeat (STR) locus used in DNA profiling has a 4-base-pair repeat unit. The primers used in PCR bind so that the amplified fragment always contains 100 base pairs of flanking DNA in addition to the repeats. A suspect's DNA profile shows two bands at this locus, at 148 bp and 172 bp.\n\nCalculate the number of repeats in each of the suspect's two alleles at this locus.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(148 − 100) ÷ 4 = 12 repeats (1)\n(172 − 100) ÷ 4 = 18 repeats (1)\nAward 1 mark for correct method (subtracting 100 and dividing by 4) if both answers incorrect` },
      { q: `In 2003 a new disease, SARS, was found to be caused by a coronavirus. Some doctors initially thought the disease was caused by a bacterium, Chlamydia, which was also found in some patients.\n\nGive two structural features that would distinguish a coronavirus particle from a bacterial cell.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\nvirus has no cell (surface) membrane / no cytoplasm / is acellular (1)\nvirus has no ribosomes / bacteria have (70S) ribosomes (1)\nvirus has a capsid / protein coat; bacteria do not (1)\nvirus has no cell wall / bacteria have a peptidoglycan cell wall (1)\nvirus genome is RNA / bacteria have DNA (in circular chromosome and plasmids) (1)\nAccept coronavirus has a lipid envelope derived from the host\nIgnore size alone\nAccept answers written from the bacterium's point of view` },
      { q: `A body was found in a cold room. The pathologist recorded that the muscles of the jaw and limbs were stiff and could not be moved.\n\nExplain why muscles become stiff in the hours after death.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `respiration stops so no ATP is produced (1)\nmyosin heads remain attached to actin / cross-bridges cannot be broken (so muscle stays contracted) (1)\nAccept ATP is needed to detach myosin from actin\nAccept calcium ions are released (from sarcoplasmic reticulum) and cannot be removed without ATP, so binding sites on actin stay exposed\nDo not accept "muscles contract" alone` },
      { q: `When a cell in the lining of the airways is infected by influenza virus, it releases the protein interferon before it dies.\n\nExplain how the release of interferon reduces the spread of the virus in the airways.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `interferon acts on neighbouring / uninfected cells (1)\ninhibiting viral protein synthesis / preventing viral replication in those cells (1)\nAccept stops viruses multiplying in other cells\nAccept stimulates other cells to make antiviral proteins\nDo not accept interferon kills / destroys the virus` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nA culture of bacteria was divided into three flasks. Antibiotic P was added to one flask, antibiotic Q to another and nothing to the third. The number of viable bacteria per cm³ was measured every 2 hours.\n\nTime / h | No antibiotic | Antibiotic P | Antibiotic Q\n0 | 1.0 × 10⁶ | 1.0 × 10⁶ | 1.0 × 10⁶\n2 | 4.0 × 10⁶ | 1.0 × 10⁶ | 2.0 × 10⁵\n4 | 1.6 × 10⁷ | 1.1 × 10⁶ | 3.0 × 10⁴\n6 | 6.4 × 10⁷ | 1.0 × 10⁶ | 1.0 × 10³\n\nDeduce which antibiotic is bacteriostatic. Give a reason for your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `antibiotic P (1)\nnumber of viable bacteria stays (about) constant / bacteria stop multiplying but are not killed, whereas with Q the number falls (1)\nAccept Q is bactericidal because the number of viable bacteria decreases\nThe reason mark is dependent on P being chosen` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nA new respiratory virus has a basic reproduction number, R₀, of 6. This means each infected person passes the virus to 6 others in a population with no immunity. The proportion of the population that must be immune to give herd immunity can be estimated using:\n\nherd immunity threshold = 1 − (1 ÷ R₀)\n\nCalculate the percentage of the population that must be immune to give herd immunity against this virus.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 − (1 ÷ 6) = 0.833 (1)\n83(.3)% (1)\nAccept 83%\nAward 1 mark for 0.83 not converted to a percentage` },
      { q: `A transmission electron micrograph shows a single rod-shaped bacterium. On the micrograph the bacterium is 30 mm long. The actual length of the bacterium is 2 µm.\n\nCalculate the magnification of the micrograph.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `conversion to the same units, e.g. 30 mm = 30 000 µm (1)\n× 15 000 (1)\nAccept 15 000 / 1.5 × 10⁴\nCorrect answer with no working scores 2` },
      { q: `A student placed paper discs soaked in three different antibiotics on a nutrient agar plate spread with bacteria. She also placed on the plate a disc soaked in sterile distilled water.\n\nExplain why the disc soaked in sterile distilled water was included.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `it is a control (1)\nto show that any zone of inhibition is due to the antibiotic and not the disc / solvent / handling (1)\nAccept to show the paper disc itself does not inhibit bacterial growth\nAccept for comparison with the antibiotic discs\nIgnore "to make it a fair test" alone` },
      { q: `Clostridium difficile is a bacterium that causes hospital-acquired infections. It forms spores that are not killed by alcohol hand gels. Infection is most common in patients who have been treated with broad-spectrum antibiotics.\n\nSuggest two measures that a hospital code of practice could include to reduce the spread of Clostridium difficile.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from:\nstaff and visitors wash hands with soap and water (rather than alcohol gel) (1)\nisolate / barrier-nurse infected patients (1)\nclean wards / surfaces / equipment with chlorine-based disinfectant / bleach (1)\nwear disposable gloves and aprons (1)\nreduce prescription of (broad-spectrum) antibiotics / only prescribe when necessary (1)\nscreen patients on admission (1)\nIgnore "clean the hospital" without detail\nDo not accept use of alcohol gel` },
      { q: `A baby who is breast-fed receives antibodies against several pathogens in its mother's milk. These antibodies are absorbed from the baby's gut into its blood.\n\nExplain why this protection lasts only a few weeks after breast-feeding stops.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `this is (natural) passive immunity / the baby's immune system has not made the antibodies (1)\nno memory cells / plasma cells are made, so the antibodies are broken down and not replaced (1)\nAccept the antibodies are not produced by the baby's B cells\nAccept antibodies are hydrolysed / removed from the blood` },
      { q: `Bodies buried in wet, airless peat bogs may be very well preserved for thousands of years. Bodies left on the surface of well-drained soil in summer decompose within weeks.\n\nExplain how the activity of microorganisms in decomposition returns carbon from a body to the atmosphere.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `microorganisms secrete enzymes that break down / digest organic molecules (in the body) (1)\nthey absorb the products and use them in respiration, releasing carbon dioxide (1)\nAccept saprotrophic / extracellular digestion\nAccept methane released by anaerobic microorganisms\nDo not accept "microorganisms eat the body" without reference to enzymes` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nA body was found at 04:00. The rectal temperature at 04:00 was 29.5 °C. A pathologist used the rule that, in these conditions, body temperature falls by 1.5 °C per hour from a normal body temperature of 37.0 °C.\n\nCalculate the estimated time of death.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `37.0 − 29.5 = 7.5 °C (1)\n7.5 ÷ 1.5 = 5 hours (1)\n23:00 (the previous evening) (1)\nAccept 11 pm\nTE for time of death from an incorrect number of hours` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nBlowfly larvae found on a body were at a stage that takes 2400 accumulated degree hours (ADH) to reach after eggs are laid. ADH is calculated using the temperature above a base temperature of 10 °C. The site where the body was found had a constant temperature of 20 °C.\n\nCalculate the minimum number of days since the eggs were laid.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `20 − 10 = 10 degrees above the base temperature (1)\n2400 ÷ 10 = 240 hours (1)\n240 ÷ 24 = 10 days (1)\nTE from an incorrect number of hours\nAward 1 mark only for 2400 ÷ 20 = 120 hours = 5 days` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nA patient's wound became infected with a bacterium that divides once every 20 minutes. There were 50 bacteria in the wound at first.\n\nCalculate the number of bacteria after 4 hours, assuming that all the bacteria survive and divide. Give your answer in standard form.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `number of generations = 240 ÷ 20 = 12 (1)\n50 × 2¹² = 204 800 (1)\n2.0 × 10⁵ / 2.048 × 10⁵ (1)\nAccept 2.05 × 10⁵\nCorrect answer not in standard form scores 2` },
      { q: `When a macrophage engulfs a bacterium in the lungs, it becomes an antigen-presenting cell.\n\nExplain how the macrophage leads to the activation of T helper cells.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `macrophage digests / hydrolyses the bacterium (using enzymes from lysosomes) (1)\n(bacterial) antigens are displayed on the cell surface membrane combined with MHC (proteins) (1)\nT helper cell with a complementary (CD4) receptor binds to the antigen–MHC complex (1)\nAccept T helper cell then divides by mitosis / produces cytokines (as alternative to third point only if binding implied)\nDo not accept antibodies on T helper cells` },
      { q: `A gardener cuts her hand on a thorn. Within a few hours the area around the cut becomes red, warm and swollen.\n\nExplain how inflammation helps to protect the body against infection at the site of the cut.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `histamine is released (by mast cells / damaged cells) (1)\ncausing vasodilation / arterioles dilate, increasing blood flow (1)\ncapillaries become more permeable (1)\nso more phagocytes / plasma proteins / antibodies leave the blood and reach the infected tissue (1)\nMax 3\nAccept higher temperature reduces pathogen reproduction` },
      { q: `Neutrophils are phagocytes that arrive at a site of bacterial infection within minutes. They are attracted by chemicals released by the bacteria.\n\nDescribe how a neutrophil destroys a bacterium.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `bacterium is engulfed / surrounded by the cell surface membrane (forming a phagosome / vacuole) (1)\nlysosomes fuse with the phagosome (1)\nlysosomal enzymes / lysozymes hydrolyse / digest the bacterium (1)\nAccept by endocytosis\nIgnore "eats the bacterium"` },
      { q: `There is still no effective vaccine against HIV. The virus has a very high mutation rate and the antigens on its surface change frequently.\n\nExplain why the high mutation rate of HIV makes it difficult to develop an effective vaccine.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `mutations change the shape / structure of the antigens (1)\nantibodies / memory cells produced in response to the vaccine are no longer complementary to the new antigens (1)\nso there is no secondary response / the virus is not recognised and the person is infected (1)\nAccept many different strains exist at the same time\nAccept antigenic variability\nDo not accept the virus becomes resistant to the vaccine` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nIn one hospital, the proportion of Staphylococcus aureus infections that were caused by methicillin-resistant S. aureus (MRSA) rose from 2% to 40% over ten years, during which methicillin was widely prescribed.\n\nExplain how the proportion of MRSA increased.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `a mutation (in a gene) gave some bacteria resistance to methicillin (1)\nwhen methicillin was used, non-resistant bacteria were killed while resistant bacteria survived (1)\nresistant bacteria reproduced, passing on the allele / gene for resistance (so the frequency of the resistance allele increased) (1)\nAccept resistance gene passed on by horizontal gene transfer / on plasmids by conjugation (for the third point)\nDo not accept the bacteria become immune / the bacteria develop resistance in response to the antibiotic` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nIn an investigation into the effect of different antibiotics on bacteria, a student measured the diameter of the clear zone around each disc. The mean diameter around antibiotic X was 23 mm and around antibiotic Y was 15 mm. The area of a circle is πr².\n\nCalculate the percentage by which the area of the clear zone around X is larger than the area around Y.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `area X = π × 11.5² = 415(.5) mm² (1)\narea Y = π × 7.5² = 176(.7) mm² (1)\n(415.5 − 176.7) ÷ 176.7 × 100 = 135% (1)\nAccept 135.1%\nAward 1 mark for 53% (percentage difference in diameter) only if no areas calculated\nTE from incorrect areas` },
      { q: `A student is to spread a culture of bacteria onto a nutrient agar plate before adding antibiotic discs.\n\nDescribe the aseptic techniques the student should use when transferring and spreading the bacterial culture.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any three from:\ndisinfect the work surface (before and after) (1)\nwork close to a (lit) Bunsen burner (to create an updraft) (1)\nflame the neck of the culture bottle after removing / before replacing the lid (1)\nsterilise the spreader / loop by flaming / dipping in ethanol and flaming (and allow to cool) (1)\nlift the Petri dish lid only slightly / for as short a time as possible (1)\nuse a sterile pipette to transfer the culture (1)\nwash hands before and after (1)\nIgnore wear gloves / goggles / lab coat` },
      { q: `A forensic scientist separates DNA fragments from a profile using gel electrophoresis.\n\nExplain how gel electrophoresis separates the DNA fragments.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `DNA is negatively charged so moves towards the positive electrode / anode (when a current is applied) (1)\nthe gel acts as a mesh / sieve, with pores that resist movement of fragments (1)\nsmaller / shorter fragments move faster / further through the gel (than longer fragments) (1)\nAccept fragments are separated according to length / size\nDo not accept smaller fragments are lighter as the only explanation` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nA paternity case was investigated using DNA profiles at two STR loci. The table gives the sizes of the bands (in base pairs) for a child, its mother and three men.\n\nPerson | Locus 1 / bp | Locus 2 / bp\nChild | 148, 172 | 210, 226\nMother | 148, 160 | 210, 218\nMan A | 160, 184 | 226, 234\nMan B | 136, 172 | 202, 226\nMan C | 148, 184 | 218, 226\n\nDeduce which man is most likely to be the child's father. Explain your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `Man B (1)\nthe child inherits one allele / band at each locus from each parent (1)\nchild's 148 (locus 1) and 210 (locus 2) bands come from the mother, so the father must provide 172 and 226 (1)\nonly man B has both 172 and 226 / men A and C have 226 but not 172 (1)\nThe first mark may be awarded within the explanation\nAccept bands that are not shared with the mother must come from the father` },
      { q: `A sample of DNA was extracted from a hair root found at the scene of a burglary. It contained too little DNA to produce a profile.\n\nDescribe how the polymerase chain reaction (PCR) is used to produce enough DNA for profiling.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `DNA is mixed with primers, (free DNA) nucleotides and (thermostable / Taq) DNA polymerase (in buffer) (1)\nheated to about 90–95 °C to separate the strands / break hydrogen bonds (1)\ncooled to about 50–60 °C so primers bind / anneal to the single strands (1)\nheated to about 70–75 °C so DNA polymerase joins nucleotides to form complementary strands (1)\ncycle repeated many times, doubling the DNA each cycle (1)\nMax 4\nAccept temperature ranges within those given\nDo not accept DNA polymerase breaks hydrogen bonds` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nA child received two doses of a vaccine, eight weeks apart. After the first dose, the concentration of antibody in the blood rose slowly to a peak of 12 arbitrary units after 14 days and then fell. After the second dose, the concentration rose within 2 days and reached a peak of 150 arbitrary units after 5 days.\n\nExplain the differences between the responses to the two doses.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the second response is faster and produces a higher concentration of antibody (12 vs 150 units / 14 vs 5 days) (1)\nfirst dose: B cells with complementary receptors must be selected and divide (clonal selection / expansion), which takes time (1)\nmemory (B) cells were produced after the first dose (1)\nafter the second dose, memory cells divide rapidly / differentiate into plasma cells (1)\nso more plasma cells secrete antibody sooner (secondary response) (1)\nMax 4\nAccept this is artificial active immunity\nDo not accept memory cells produce antibodies` },
      { q: `A body was found in woodland. Several species of insect were found on it, including blowfly larvae, beetles that feed on dried tissue, and parasitic wasps that lay eggs in fly pupae.\n\nExplain how forensic entomologists use insects to estimate the time since death, and give one factor that can make this estimate less reliable.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `different species of insect colonise the body in a predictable sequence / succession (1)\nas the body decomposes, conditions change, making it suitable for different species (1)\nthe stage of the life cycle / size of larvae of blowflies gives time since eggs were laid (as development rates are known) (1)\nfactor: temperature / humidity / burial / clothing / time of year / location affects colonisation or development rate (1)\nAccept presence of drugs / toxins in the body affecting larval development (for factor)\nAccept blowflies arrive first, followed by beetles, then wasps` },
      { q: `Tuberculosis (TB) is caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis. People with active pulmonary TB develop a persistent cough, may cough up blood, lose weight and have a fever.\n\nExplain the causes of these symptoms of active TB.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `bacteria multiply in / destroy lung tissue (1)\ndamage to blood vessels / capillaries in the lungs causes blood in the sputum (1)\nlung tissue damage / formation of tubercles / granulomas reduces gas exchange (surface area) (1)\nfever because the immune response / pathogen releases chemicals that raise the body's temperature set point (1)\nweight loss because the bacteria use the body's resources / tissue is broken down (1)\nMax 4\nAccept coughing is a reflex to remove mucus / damaged tissue from airways` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nA student measured the diameter of the zones of inhibition around discs containing antibiotic X and antibiotic Y on five plates of the same bacterium.\n\nPlate | X / mm | Y / mm\n1 | 22 | 14\n2 | 24 | 16\n3 | 21 | 15\n4 | 25 | 13\n5 | 23 | 17\n\nThe student concluded that antibiotic X is better at killing this bacterium than antibiotic Y.\n\nEvaluate this conclusion.`, marks: 4, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `mean zone for X (23 mm) is larger than for Y (15 mm) (1)\nranges do not overlap (X 21–25 mm, Y 13–17 mm) so the difference is likely to be significant / supports the conclusion (1)\na clear zone shows inhibition of growth, not necessarily killing / X could be bacteriostatic (1)\nthe antibiotics may diffuse through agar at different rates / may be at different concentrations (so zone size may not reflect effectiveness) (1)\nonly one species / strain tested / no statistical test carried out (1)\nMax 4\nAt least one supporting and one limiting point needed for full marks` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nThe CD4 T helper cell count of an untreated person infected with HIV was measured over several years. A count below 200 cells per mm³ is used to diagnose AIDS.\n\nYears after infection | CD4 count / cells per mm³ | Viral load\n1 | 820 | low\n4 | 500 | low\n8 | 190 | very high\n\nAnalyse the data to explain why this person is likely to develop opportunistic infections such as TB after 8 years. Include a calculation of the percentage decrease in CD4 count between year 1 and year 8.`, marks: 5, command: 'Analyse', markScheme: `(820 − 190) ÷ 820 × 100 (1)\n= 76.8% (1)\nHIV infects and destroys T helper cells / viral replication rises as the CD4 count falls (1)\nfewer T helper cells to activate B cells / T killer cells (1)\nso fewer antibodies / plasma cells / T killer cells produced, so the specific immune response fails (1)\nAccept fewer cytokines released\nAccept count is below 200 so the person has AIDS\nMax 5\nAccept 77%` },
      { q: `A pharmaceutical company has developed three new antibiotics, A, B and C. A technician has been asked to compare their effects on the growth of a non-pathogenic strain of Escherichia coli using a lawn of bacteria on agar plates.\n\nDevise a method the technician could use to compare the effects of the three antibiotics.`, marks: 5, command: 'Devise', markScheme: `spread a known volume of bacterial culture evenly over (sterile) nutrient agar plates (1)\nplace discs soaked in the same concentration / volume of each antibiotic on the plate (using sterile forceps) (1)\ncontrol: disc soaked in sterile water / solvent (1)\nincubate at 25 °C for 24–48 hours / a stated time (1)\nmeasure the diameter of the clear zone and calculate area / πr² (1)\nrepeat with several plates and calculate a mean (1)\nuse aseptic technique, e.g. flame the neck of the culture bottle / sterilise spreader (1)\ncontrol variables: same depth / volume of agar, same disc size, same incubation time (1)\nMax 5\nDo not accept incubation at 37 °C` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nA body was found in a house where the heating had been switched off. The pathologist found that:\n• the rectal temperature was the same as the room temperature\n• rigor mortis was present in the large limb muscles but had started to disappear from the small muscles of the face\n• there was no green discoloration of the skin of the abdomen\n• first-stage blowfly larvae were present around the eyes and mouth.\n\nEvaluate the usefulness of each of these observations in estimating the time since death.`, marks: 5, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `body temperature: body has reached room temperature, so this only gives a minimum time / it can no longer be used to estimate time accurately (1)\nrigor mortis: is passing off (in small muscles first), suggesting roughly 24–36 hours since death (1)\nrigor mortis timing is affected by temperature / muscle mass / activity before death (1)\ndecomposition: no discoloration suggests early stage, consistent with less than 2–3 days / is affected by temperature (1)\nentomology: first-stage larvae show eggs were laid recently / gives a minimum time since death (1)\nusing several methods together gives a more reliable estimate / they are consistent with about 1–2 days (1)\nMax 5\nAccept reasonable time ranges consistent with the observations` },
      { q: `*Tuberculosis is treated with a combination of several antibiotics taken for at least six months. Mycobacterium tuberculosis is a slow-growing bacterium with a thick, waxy cell wall that can survive inside macrophages, where it may be walled off in a tubercle (granuloma) for many years.\n\nExplain why Mycobacterium tuberculosis is difficult to eliminate from the body and why it is treated in this way.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 bacteria survive inside macrophages, so are not destroyed by lysosomal enzymes\nIC2 the thick waxy cell wall resists digestion / reduces entry of antibiotics\nIC3 bacteria inside cells / tubercles are hidden from antibodies and the immune system\nIC4 the bacteria may suppress antigen presentation / T cell activation\nIC5 slow growth / dormant bacteria are less affected by antibiotics that act on growing / dividing cells, so treatment must be long\nIC6 several antibiotics reduce the chance of a bacterium being resistant to all of them\nIC7 stopping the course early leaves survivors, which may be resistant, allowing (multi-drug) resistant strains to be selected\nIC8 long treatment needed to reach bacteria walled off in tubercles\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 IC → 4; 5–4 IC → 3; 3–2 IC → 2; 1 IC → 1\nReasoning: 2 = sustained, linked explanation of both difficulty and treatment; 1 = some linkage; 0 = isolated points` },
      { q: `*A person is infected for the first time with a virus that infects cells lining the airways.\n\nExplain how the specific immune response leads to the destruction of infected cells and the removal of virus particles from the body.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 macrophages engulf viruses and present viral antigens with MHC proteins (antigen-presenting cells)\nIC2 T helper cells with complementary receptors bind and are activated, then divide by mitosis (clonal selection / expansion)\nIC3 T helper cells release cytokines that activate T killer cells and B cells\nIC4 T killer cells bind to infected cells displaying viral antigen–MHC and destroy them (e.g. by releasing perforin / creating pores)\nIC5 activated B cells divide and differentiate into plasma cells (B effector cells)\nIC6 plasma cells secrete antibodies complementary to the viral antigens\nIC7 antibodies cause agglutination / neutralise viruses / act as opsonins so phagocytes engulf them\nIC8 memory T and B cells are produced, giving a faster secondary response\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 IC → 4; 5–4 IC → 3; 3–2 IC → 2; 1 IC → 1\nReasoning: 2 = sustained sequence from antigen presentation to both cellular and humoral responses; 1 = some linkage; 0 = isolated points\nDo not accept antibodies kill viruses` },
      { q: `*Blood found on broken glass at a crime scene was used to produce a DNA profile. The profile was compared with profiles from three suspects and with a national DNA database.\n\nExplain how a DNA profile is produced from the blood and how it is used to identify the person who left the blood.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 DNA is extracted from white blood cells (red blood cells have no nucleus)\nIC2 DNA is amplified using PCR (with primers for STR regions)\nIC3 DNA is cut with restriction enzymes / STR regions are amplified using primers that bind to flanking regions\nIC4 STRs (short tandem repeats) vary in the number of repeats between individuals\nIC5 fragments are separated by gel electrophoresis according to length\nIC6 fragments are made visible using fluorescent tags / radioactive probes and UV light / autoradiography\nIC7 bands from the crime scene are compared with each suspect's profile / a match at all loci identifies the person\nIC8 many loci are used so the probability of a match by chance is very small (except identical twins)\nIC9 samples must be handled without contamination / a database match is evidence of presence, not of guilt\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 IC → 4; 5–4 IC → 3; 3–2 IC → 2; 1 IC → 1\nReasoning: 2 = logical sequence from extraction to comparison with justification of reliability; 1 = some linkage; 0 = isolated points` }
    ]
  },
  'S7': {
    name: 'Run for Your Life',
    questions: [
      { q: `A 34-year-old marathon runner feels a sudden pain at the back of her ankle. A physiotherapist diagnoses a partial tear of the Achilles tendon, which attaches the calf muscle (gastrocnemius) to the heel bone.\n\nState the function of a tendon.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `joins / attaches muscle to bone (so that the force of contraction moves the bone) (1)\nDo not accept: joins bone to bone (this is a ligament)` },
      { q: `During a 100 m sprint, most of the ATP used by the leg muscles is regenerated from creatine phosphate and from glycolysis. In glycolysis, each glucose molecule is phosphorylated and then split into two triose phosphate molecules, which are oxidised.\n\nName the 3-carbon compound produced at the end of glycolysis.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `pyruvate / pyruvic acid (1)\nDo not accept: lactate / triose phosphate / glycerate 3-phosphate` },
      { q: `A cardiologist is examining the electrocardiogram (ECG) of a patient who complains of feeling faint during exercise. The ECG shows a P wave, a QRS complex and a T wave for every heartbeat.\n\nState which part of the ECG represents depolarisation of the ventricles.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `QRS complex (1)\nAccept: QRS / the R wave region\nDo not accept: T wave / P wave` },
      { q: `Core body temperature is controlled by negative feedback. Thermoreceptors in the brain detect changes in the temperature of the blood flowing through it, and the information is used to coordinate responses such as sweating and shivering.\n\nName the part of the brain that contains the thermoregulatory centre.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `hypothalamus (1)\nDo not accept: medulla / pituitary / cerebellum` },
      { q: `A student set up a respirometer to measure the rate of oxygen uptake by woodlice. The woodlice were placed on a gauze platform above a layer of soda lime, and a drop of coloured liquid was introduced into a capillary tube attached to the tube.\n\nGive the reason for including soda lime in the respirometer.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `to absorb carbon dioxide (so that movement of the liquid is due only to oxygen uptake) (1)\nAccept: removes CO₂ produced in respiration\nIgnore: absorbs water` },
      { q: `Cyanide is a poison that binds to and inhibits the last enzyme complex of the electron transport chain in mitochondria. In a cell poisoned by cyanide, electrons can no longer be passed to their final acceptor.\n\nName the final electron acceptor in aerobic respiration.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `oxygen (1)\nAccept: O₂\nDo not accept: water / hydrogen / NAD` },
      { q: `An ECG was recorded from a cyclist during a hill climb. The ECG paper moved at 25 mm s⁻¹. The distance between two successive R peaks on the trace was 12 mm.\n\nCalculate the heart rate of the cyclist in beats per minute.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `time for one beat = 12 ÷ 25 = 0.48 s (1)\nheart rate = 60 ÷ 0.48 = 125 (beats per minute) (1)\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `In a healthy heart the wave of excitation from the sinoatrial node (SAN) spreads across the atria but is delayed by about 0.1 s at the atrioventricular node (AVN) before passing down the bundle of His.\n\nExplain why this delay is important for the heart to pump blood efficiently.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `allows the atria to finish contracting / complete atrial systole before the ventricles contract (1)\nso that the ventricles fill completely with blood (before ventricular systole) (1)\nAccept: so atria and ventricles do not contract at the same time for mp1` },
      { q: `The elbow is a hinge joint. The biceps muscle is attached to the radius on the front of the arm and the triceps muscle is attached to the ulna at the back of the arm. A rower straightens (extends) her arms at the end of each stroke.\n\nDescribe how the biceps and triceps act as an antagonistic pair to extend the arm at the elbow.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `triceps contracts / acts as the extensor and pulls on the ulna (1)\nbiceps relaxes (and is stretched / lengthened by the triceps) (1)\nAccept: triceps is the agonist, biceps the antagonist\nDo not accept: muscles push the bone` },
      { q: `A researcher measured sarcomeres in a frog muscle fibre using an electron microscope (data written for this practice question).\n\nRelaxed muscle: sarcomere length 2.4 µm, A band length 1.6 µm\nContracted muscle: sarcomere length 1.9 µm, A band length 1.6 µm\n\nExplain why the length of the A band did not change when the muscle contracted.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the A band is the length of the myosin (thick) filaments (1)\nfilaments do not shorten / actin filaments slide past (between) the myosin filaments (1)\nAccept: only the I band and H zone shorten as the filaments overlap more` },
      { q: `A spirometer trace was recorded from a student sitting at rest. The trace showed 5 complete breaths in 20 s. The difference between each peak and the following trough was 0.5 dm³.\n\nCalculate the student's minute ventilation. Give your answer in dm³ min⁻¹.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `breathing rate = 5 × 3 = 15 breaths min⁻¹ (1)\nminute ventilation = 0.5 × 15 = 7.5 (dm³ min⁻¹) (1)\nAward 2 marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `A former professional footballer has severe osteoarthritis of the knee. The articular cartilage on the ends of the femur and tibia has been worn away. He is offered a total knee replacement in which the joint surfaces are replaced with a titanium alloy component and a polyethylene (plastic) spacer.\n\nSuggest two properties that the materials used in this prosthetic joint should have.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `any two of:\nlow friction / smooth (so the surfaces slide past each other as cartilage did) (1)\nhard-wearing / resistant to wear / strong / durable (1)\nnot rejected by the immune system / biocompatible / non-toxic (1)\ndo not corrode in body fluids (1)\nlightweight (1)\nIgnore: cheap` },
      { q: `Some sprinters take creatine supplements. Creatine is stored in muscle as creatine phosphate. During a sprint, creatine phosphate donates a phosphate group to ADP.\n\nSuggest how creatine supplements might improve performance in a 100 m sprint.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `more creatine phosphate stored in the muscle (1)\nso ATP can be regenerated more rapidly / for longer (without relying on anaerobic respiration) (1)\nAccept: delays fatigue / delays lactate build up for mp2\nIgnore: more energy` },
      { q: `A student used a respirometer to compare the rate of respiration of germinating mung beans at 15 °C and at 25 °C. The movement of a drop of coloured liquid along a capillary tube was recorded for 10 minutes.\n\nGive two variables, other than temperature, that the student should control in this investigation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two of:\nmass / number of beans (1)\nage / stage of germination / species of bean (1)\nvolume / mass of soda lime (1)\ntime allowed for equilibration before readings (1)\ndiameter / bore of capillary tube (1)\nvolume of air in the respirometer (1)\nIgnore: amount of beans (unqualified)` },
      { q: `During a 400 m race, much of the ATP in the leg muscles comes from anaerobic respiration. Pyruvate produced in glycolysis is converted to lactate.\n\nExplain why the conversion of pyruvate to lactate allows glycolysis to continue.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `reduced NAD is oxidised / NAD is regenerated (when pyruvate is reduced to lactate) (1)\nNAD is needed to accept hydrogen in glycolysis / for the oxidation of triose phosphate (1)\nDo not accept: lactate is used for glycolysis` },
      { q: `A rugby player tore the cartilage (meniscus) in his knee. The surgeon repaired it by arthroscopy (keyhole surgery), in which a camera and instruments are passed into the joint through small incisions, rather than by open surgery.\n\nGive two advantages of keyhole surgery compared with open surgery.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `any two of:\nless blood loss (1)\nsmaller scars / less tissue damage (1)\nlower risk of infection (1)\nfaster recovery / shorter stay in hospital / returns to sport sooner (1)\nless pain after the operation (1)\nAccept: can be done under local anaesthetic` },
      { q: `A survey of runners found that those who completed a marathon were about six times more likely to report symptoms of an upper respiratory tract infection in the two weeks after the race than runners of similar ability who did not compete (data written for this practice question).\n\nSuggest an explanation for this observation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `very strenuous / prolonged exercise suppresses the immune system (1)\nfewer / less active natural killer cells / phagocytes / lymphocytes (1)\nlower levels of antibodies / IgA in the mucus of airways (1)\nmore exposure to pathogens from crowds / increased ventilation draws in more pathogens (1)\nmax 2` },
      { q: `A sports scientist measured the stroke volume and heart rate of a cyclist (data written for this practice question).\n\nAt rest: stroke volume 70 cm³, heart rate 68 beats min⁻¹\nDuring maximum effort: stroke volume 120 cm³, heart rate 175 beats min⁻¹\n\nCardiac output = stroke volume × heart rate\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in cardiac output from rest to maximum effort.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `cardiac output at rest = 70 × 68 = 4760 cm³ min⁻¹ (1)\ncardiac output at maximum effort = 120 × 175 = 21 000 cm³ min⁻¹ (1)\npercentage increase = (21 000 − 4760) ÷ 4760 × 100 = 341 % (1)\nAccept: 341.2 %\nAward 3 marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `A student used a respirometer to measure oxygen uptake by 1.6 g of blowfly larvae at 20 °C. A drop of liquid in the capillary tube moved 36 mm in 15 minutes. The internal radius of the capillary tube was 0.5 mm.\n\nVolume of a cylinder = πr²l\n\nCalculate the rate of oxygen uptake by the larvae in mm³ g⁻¹ min⁻¹.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `volume = π × 0.5² × 36 = 28.3 mm³ (1)\n÷ 15 = 1.88 mm³ min⁻¹ (1)\n÷ 1.6 = 1.18 (mm³ g⁻¹ min⁻¹) (1)\nAccept: 1.2\nTE for an error in an earlier step\nAward 3 marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `Dantrolene is a drug used to treat malignant hyperthermia, a condition in which skeletal muscles contract uncontrollably. Dantrolene blocks the release of calcium ions from the sarcoplasmic reticulum in muscle fibres.\n\nExplain why dantrolene causes skeletal muscles to relax.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(fewer) calcium ions do not bind to troponin (1)\ntropomyosin is not moved / continues to cover the myosin-binding sites on actin (1)\nso myosin heads cannot bind to actin / no cross-bridges (actomyosin bridges) form (1)\n(so actin filaments are not pulled past myosin / no sliding)\nDo not accept: calcium binds to tropomyosin` },
      { q: `2,4-dinitrophenol (DNP) was once sold as a slimming drug. DNP carries hydrogen ions (protons) across the inner mitochondrial membrane, back into the matrix, without them passing through ATP synthase.\n\nExplain how ATP is normally produced by ATP synthase during oxidative phosphorylation.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `energy released by electrons passing along the electron transport chain / electron carriers (1)\nis used to pump H⁺ from the matrix into the intermembrane space (1)\nH⁺ diffuse back into the matrix through ATP synthase down their concentration / electrochemical gradient (1)\n(this) provides energy to join ADP and Pi / phosphorylate ADP (1)\nmax 3\nAccept: chemiosmosis for mp3 if described` },
      { q: `Some athletes have misused anabolic steroids such as testosterone to increase muscle mass. Testosterone is a lipid-soluble hormone that acts as a transcription factor once inside a muscle cell.\n\nExplain how testosterone can increase the production of muscle proteins.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `testosterone diffuses through the cell surface membrane / phospholipid bilayer (1)\nbinds to a receptor (in the cytoplasm) and the hormone–receptor complex enters the nucleus (1)\nbinds to the promoter region of DNA / attracts or allows RNA polymerase to bind (1)\nstimulating transcription of genes for muscle proteins / more mRNA made (1)\nmax 3\nDo not accept: testosterone enters by facilitated diffusion / via a channel protein` },
      { q: `A muscle biopsy showed that the slow twitch fibres in the leg of a long-distance runner contained many more mitochondria and a much higher concentration of myoglobin than the fast twitch fibres.\n\nExplain how these features suit slow twitch fibres to their role.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `slow twitch fibres are used for sustained / endurance activity / contract for long periods without fatigue (1)\nmany mitochondria allow a high rate of aerobic respiration / Krebs cycle and oxidative phosphorylation (1)\nmyoglobin stores oxygen / has a high affinity for oxygen and releases it when oxygen concentration is low (1)\nso (more) ATP is produced without lactate build up (1)\nmax 3` },
      { q: `Muscle biopsies were taken from the thigh muscles of trained sprinters and trained marathon runners (data written for this practice question).\n\nSprinters: slow twitch fibres 24 %, capillaries per fibre 1.8, mitochondria (% of fibre volume) 3.2\nMarathon runners: slow twitch fibres 79 %, capillaries per fibre 3.5, mitochondria (% of fibre volume) 7.1\n\nCompare the muscles of the two groups of athletes.`, marks: 3, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `marathon runners have a higher percentage of slow twitch fibres / sprinters have more fast twitch fibres (76 % compared with 21 %) (1)\nmarathon runners have about twice as many capillaries per fibre (1)\nmarathon runners have about twice the volume of mitochondria (1)\nAccept: manipulated figures for any point\nThe comparison must be made for each mark; two separate lists of figures score max 1` },
      { q: `A student breathed from a spirometer filled with oxygen, with a canister of soda lime in the circuit. The level of the troughs on the trace fell from 3.60 dm³ to 3.10 dm³ over 1 minute at rest. Immediately after 2 minutes of step-ups, the troughs fell from 3.10 dm³ to 1.85 dm³ over 1 minute.\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in the rate of oxygen consumption after exercise.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `rate at rest = 0.50 dm³ min⁻¹ (1)\nrate after exercise = 1.25 dm³ min⁻¹ (1)\npercentage increase = (1.25 − 0.50) ÷ 0.50 × 100 = 150 % (1)\nAward 3 marks for correct answer with no working` },
      { q: `An elderly patient's ECG shows no clear P waves. The QRS complexes are of normal shape but occur at irregular intervals, varying between 0.4 s and 1.1 s apart. The patient reports breathlessness when climbing stairs.\n\nSuggest an explanation for this ECG and for the patient's breathlessness.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `atrial fibrillation / atria are not contracting in a coordinated way (1)\nSAN not acting as the pacemaker / impulses are produced randomly across the atria (1)\nAVN passes on impulses irregularly so the ventricles beat irregularly (1)\nventricles do not fill fully / cardiac output reduced, so less oxygen delivered to muscles (causing breathlessness) (1)\nmax 3` },
      { q: `The haematocrit (percentage of blood volume made of red blood cells) of a professional cyclist was recorded during a season (data written for this practice question). A cycling federation suspends riders whose haematocrit exceeds 50 % (invented threshold for this question).\n\nJanuary 43 %; March 44 %; June (during a major race) 52 %; August 45 %\n\nDeduce, with reasons, whether these data prove that the cyclist used EPO.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `not proof / data suggest but do not prove EPO use (1)\nhaematocrit rose above the limit / by 8 percentage points during the race, consistent with EPO stimulating red blood cell production (1)\nmay be due to altitude training / dehydration (reduces plasma volume) / natural variation (1)\na direct test for (recombinant) EPO in blood or urine would be needed (1)\nmax 3` },
      { q: `The complete oxidation of 1 mol of glucose releases 2870 kJ. Hydrolysis of 1 mol of ATP releases 30.5 kJ. Assume that aerobic respiration yields 30 ATP per glucose and that anaerobic respiration in muscle yields 2 ATP per glucose.\n\nCalculate the percentage efficiency of energy transfer to ATP for aerobic and for anaerobic respiration. Give your answers to one decimal place.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `30 × 30.5 = 915 kJ (1)\naerobic: 915 ÷ 2870 × 100 = 31.9 % (1)\nanaerobic: 2 × 30.5 ÷ 2870 × 100 = 2.1 % (1)\nAward full marks for correct answers with no working` },
      { q: `A few hours after death, skeletal muscles become stiff and cannot be stretched, a condition called rigor mortis. After death, aerobic respiration stops and the ATP in muscle fibres is soon used up. Calcium ions also leak out of the sarcoplasmic reticulum.\n\nExplain why rigor mortis occurs.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `calcium ions bind to troponin, moving tropomyosin (1)\nexposing myosin-binding sites on actin so myosin heads bind / cross-bridges form (1)\nATP is needed to detach the myosin head from actin (1)\nwithout ATP the cross-bridges cannot break / actin and myosin stay locked (1)\nATP is needed to actively transport Ca²⁺ back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum so Ca²⁺ remains in the sarcoplasm (1)\nmax 4` },
      { q: `A student wants to use a spirometer to investigate the effect of exercise on tidal volume and breathing rate.\n\nDescribe how the student could carry out this investigation safely.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `subject wears a nose clip and breathes through the mouthpiece, recording a trace at rest (1)\nthen carries out a set exercise (e.g. step-ups for 2 minutes at a set rate) and the trace is recorded again immediately (1)\ntidal volume measured as the vertical distance between a peak and the next trough; breathing rate as number of peaks per minute (1)\nrepeat with several subjects / repeat readings and calculate a mean (1)\nsafety: disinfect / sterilise mouthpiece; use medical-grade oxygen; soda lime in place to absorb CO₂; stop if dizzy; subject without asthma / heart conditions (1)\nmax 4; must include a safety point for full marks` },
      { q: `A student set up a respirometer containing germinating peas and measured how far a drop of liquid moved in 5 minutes. She used a single tube with no control tube, took one reading at room temperature, and recorded the result to the nearest 5 mm.\n\nSuggest how the student could improve the validity and reliability of her results.`, marks: 4, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `use a control / compensation tube with glass beads (of the same volume as the peas) to account for changes in temperature / pressure (1)\nplace the respirometer in a thermostatically controlled water bath (1)\nleave to equilibrate before taking readings (1)\nmeasure distance to the nearest mm / use a scale with smaller divisions / use a syringe to reset the drop (1)\nrepeat at least three times and calculate a mean / identify anomalies (1)\nmeasure for a longer time / calculate the rate per gram of peas (1)\nmax 4` },
      { q: `On a hot day, the core temperature of a marathon runner rises from 37.0 °C to 38.5 °C during a race.\n\nExplain how the body responds to reduce core temperature.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `thermoreceptors in the hypothalamus detect the rise in blood temperature (1)\nimpulses sent (via the autonomic nervous system) to effectors (1)\nsweat glands secrete more sweat; evaporation of water uses heat energy from the skin (1)\nvasodilation of arterioles in the skin, so more blood flows near the surface and more heat is lost by radiation (1)\nhair erector muscles relax so hairs lie flat (1)\nthis is negative feedback (1)\nmax 4\nDo not accept: capillaries dilate / blood vessels move to the surface` },
      { q: `Blood lactate concentration was measured in an athlete before and after a 400 m race (data written for this practice question).\n\nAt rest: 1.2 mmol dm⁻³\nImmediately after the race: 14.8 mmol dm⁻³\n30 minutes after the race: 3.1 mmol dm⁻³\n\nAnalyse these data to explain the changes in blood lactate concentration.`, marks: 4, command: 'Analyse', markScheme: `lactate rises by 13.6 mmol dm⁻³ / about twelve-fold / 1133 % during the race (1)\noxygen supply insufficient for demand so anaerobic respiration / pyruvate converted to lactate (1)\nlactate falls by 79 % in recovery but does not return to resting level after 30 minutes (1)\nlactate is oxidised back to pyruvate (and enters Krebs cycle) or converted to glucose / glycogen in the liver (1)\nthis requires extra oxygen / oxygen debt / excess post-exercise oxygen consumption (1)\nmax 4` },
      { q: `In a healthy heart, the ventricles contract starting from the apex (lowest point) of the heart and moving upwards. A child is born with a defect in the bundle of His, which prevents impulses reaching the ventricles.\n\nExplain how the electrical activity of a normal heart causes the ventricles to contract from the apex upwards.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `SAN generates a wave of excitation / depolarisation (heart muscle is myogenic) (1)\nspreads across the atria causing atrial systole, but a layer of non-conducting tissue stops it passing directly to the ventricles (1)\nAVN delays the impulse and then passes it down the bundle of His (in the septum) (1)\nto the apex, then up the Purkyne fibres in the ventricle walls (1)\nso ventricles contract from the apex upwards, pushing blood up into the arteries (1)\nmax 4` },
      { q: `A student measured the vital capacity of ten rowers and ten students who did not train (data written for this practice question).\n\nRowers: mean 5.8 dm³, standard deviation 0.6 dm³, n = 10\nNon-rowers: mean 4.9 dm³, standard deviation 0.7 dm³, n = 10\n\nt = (x̄₁ − x̄₂) ÷ √(s₁²/n₁ + s₂²/n₂)\nCritical values of t at p = 0.05: 18 degrees of freedom 2.10; 20 degrees of freedom 2.09\n\nState a null hypothesis, calculate t and use it to determine whether there is a significant difference in vital capacity.`, marks: 5, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `null hypothesis: there is no (significant) difference between the mean vital capacity of rowers and non-rowers (1)\n√(0.036 + 0.049) = 0.292 (1)\nt = 0.9 ÷ 0.292 = 3.09 (1)\ndegrees of freedom = 18 so critical value is 2.10 (1)\n3.09 is greater than 2.10 so reject the null hypothesis; the difference is significant (at p = 0.05) / less than 5 % probability that it is due to chance (1)\nTE for conclusion based on calculated t` },
      { q: `DNP carries hydrogen ions across the inner mitochondrial membrane back into the matrix without them passing through ATP synthase. People who took DNP as a slimming drug lost body mass rapidly, but many developed dangerously high body temperatures.\n\nExplain why taking DNP leads to loss of body mass and a rise in body temperature.`, marks: 5, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the H⁺ (proton) gradient across the inner membrane is reduced (1)\nfewer H⁺ diffuse through ATP synthase so less ATP is produced by oxidative phosphorylation / chemiosmosis (1)\nenergy from the electron transport chain is released as heat instead of being used to make ATP (1)\nto meet ATP demand, the rate of respiration increases (1)\nso more respiratory substrate / glycogen / fat is broken down, reducing body mass (1)\nheat production exceeds heat loss so core temperature rises / thermoregulation cannot compensate (1)\nmax 5` },
      { q: `A spirometer trace was recorded from a student at rest and during exercise on a cycle ergometer.\n\nAt rest: 12 peaks in 60 s; peak-to-trough height 0.50 dm³\nDuring exercise: 24 peaks in 60 s; peak-to-trough height 1.75 dm³\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in minute ventilation, and explain how the increase in ventilation is brought about.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `minute ventilation at rest = 0.50 × 12 = 6.0 dm³ min⁻¹ (1)\nduring exercise = 1.75 × 24 = 42 dm³ min⁻¹ (1)\npercentage increase = (42 − 6) ÷ 6 × 100 = 600 % (1)\nincreased respiration raises blood CO₂ concentration / lowers blood pH, detected by chemoreceptors (in the medulla / carotid and aortic bodies) (1)\nventilation centre in the medulla sends more frequent impulses to the diaphragm and intercostal muscles, increasing rate and depth (1)\nAccept: stretch receptors in muscles / joints stimulate ventilation centre for mp4` },
      { q: `*In 1988 a gold-medal sprinter was disqualified after testing positive for an anabolic steroid. Some people argue that all performance-enhancing drugs should be allowed in sport, while international sports bodies ban substances such as anabolic steroids and EPO but allow creatine.\n\nDiscuss the arguments for and against the use of performance-enhancing drugs by athletes.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 anabolic steroids / testosterone increase muscle mass and strength by increasing transcription of muscle protein genes\nIC2 EPO increases red blood cell count, increasing oxygen delivery for aerobic respiration / endurance\nIC3 against: unfair advantage / not a level playing field / cheating\nIC4 against: health risks, e.g. EPO thickens blood causing thrombosis / stroke / heart attack; steroids cause liver damage, infertility, aggression, heart disease\nIC5 against: athletes may feel pressured / coerced to take drugs to compete; poor role models\nIC6 for: individual autonomy / athletes are informed and should be free to choose\nIC7 for: allowing drugs could be safer if medically supervised; testing is expensive and some natural advantages (e.g. altitude training, genetics) are already unequal\nIC8 creatine allowed because it is a natural dietary substance / difficult to detect / few health risks\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 IC → 4; 5–4 IC → 3; 3–2 IC → 2; 1 IC → 1\nReasoning: 2 = sustained, balanced argument with both sides and links to biology; 1 = some linkage; 0 = list of unlinked points\nTo reach 6 marks, both arguments for and against must be given` },
      { q: `*A long-distance runner's slow twitch muscle fibres respire aerobically for several hours during a race, using glucose from glycogen stores.\n\nExplain how ATP is produced from glucose in aerobic respiration in these muscle fibres.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 glycolysis in the cytoplasm: glucose phosphorylated using 2 ATP and split into two triose phosphate\nIC2 triose phosphate oxidised to pyruvate, producing reduced NAD and a net gain of 2 ATP (substrate-level phosphorylation)\nIC3 link reaction in the mitochondrial matrix: pyruvate decarboxylated and dehydrogenated, combined with coenzyme A to form acetyl CoA; CO₂ and reduced NAD produced\nIC4 Krebs cycle: acetyl (2C) combines with 4C to form 6C compound, regenerated through decarboxylation and dehydrogenation\nIC5 Krebs cycle produces reduced NAD, reduced FAD and ATP (by substrate-level phosphorylation)\nIC6 reduced NAD / FAD release electrons to the electron transport chain on the cristae / inner membrane\nIC7 energy from electrons used to pump H⁺ into the intermembrane space; H⁺ flow back through ATP synthase producing ATP (chemiosmosis)\nIC8 oxygen is the final electron acceptor, combining with electrons and H⁺ to form water\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 IC → 4; 5–4 IC → 3; 3–2 IC → 2; 1 IC → 1\nReasoning: 2 = stages in sequence with correct locations and links between products and later stages; 1 = some sequence or linkage; 0 = unlinked points` },
      { q: `*A fitness tracker showed that a cyclist's heart rate rose from 62 beats min⁻¹ to 160 beats min⁻¹ during a race and returned to 70 beats min⁻¹ within ten minutes of finishing.\n\nExplain how the heart rate is increased during exercise and returned to resting level after exercise.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 increased respiration increases CO₂ / lactate in the blood, lowering pH\nIC2 detected by chemoreceptors in the carotid / aortic bodies (and medulla)\nIC3 stretch receptors in muscles / joints detect movement\nIC4 impulses sent to the cardiovascular control centre in the medulla (oblongata)\nIC5 more impulses along the sympathetic nerve (accelerator nerve) to the SAN, increasing the frequency of excitation\nIC6 adrenaline released (in anticipation / during exercise) also increases SAN firing rate and stroke volume\nIC7 after exercise CO₂ falls / pH rises; baroreceptors detect raised blood pressure\nIC8 more impulses along the parasympathetic (vagus) nerve to the SAN, decreasing heart rate: negative feedback\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 IC → 4; 5–4 IC → 3; 3–2 IC → 2; 1 IC → 1\nReasoning: 2 = clear sequence from stimulus to receptor to control centre to effector for both increase and decrease; 1 = partial sequence; 0 = unlinked points` }
    ]
  },
  'S8': {
    name: 'Grey Matter',
    questions: [
      { q: `Lettuce seeds will only germinate after they have been exposed to light. Germination is stimulated by red light (660 nm) and inhibited if the red light is followed immediately by far-red light (730 nm). Phytochrome in the seeds exists in two interconvertible forms, Pr and Pfr.\n\nName the form of phytochrome that is produced when Pr absorbs red light.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `Pfr / P730 / far-red-absorbing phytochrome (1)\nAccept: phytochrome far red\nDo not accept: Pr / P660` },
      { q: `Owls hunt at night. The retina of an owl contains a very high proportion of rod cells compared with cone cells.\n\nName the light-sensitive pigment found in rod cells.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `rhodopsin (1)\nAccept: visual purple\nDo not accept: iodopsin / retinal alone / opsin alone` },
      { q: `A 68-year-old woman has a stroke. After the stroke she can understand speech and her memory is unaffected, but she walks unsteadily, cannot touch her nose accurately with her eyes closed and has difficulty with fine hand movements.\n\nIdentify the region of the brain most likely to have been damaged.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `cerebellum (1)\nDo not accept: cerebrum / medulla / hypothalamus` },
      { q: `Parkinson's disease is caused by the death of neurones in a part of the brain called the substantia nigra. Symptoms include muscle tremors, stiffness and slow movement.\n\nName the neurotransmitter whose secretion is reduced in people with Parkinson's disease.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `dopamine (1)\nDo not accept: L-dopa / serotonin / acetylcholine` },
      { q: `A student investigated habituation in garden snails. She touched each snail on the head with a cotton bud at 1-minute intervals and used a stopwatch to time how long it took for the snail's tentacles to fully re-emerge after each touch.\n\nState the dependent variable in this investigation.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `time taken for the (stalked) tentacles to (fully) re-emerge / extend after the touch (1)\nAccept: withdrawal time / recovery time of tentacles\nDo not accept: number of touches / the touch` },
      { q: `Researchers wanted to identify which regions of the brain become active when healthy volunteers look at photographs of faces. They wanted a technique that would not expose the volunteers to ionising radiation or a radioactive tracer.\n\nName the brain imaging technique that the researchers should use.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `fMRI / functional magnetic resonance imaging (1)\nDo not accept: MRI alone / CT / PET` },
      { q: `When a hare sees a fox, it immediately runs away. At the same time, its adrenal glands secrete adrenaline, which increases its heart rate for several minutes.\n\nGive two differences between nervous coordination and hormonal coordination, using this example.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• nervous response (running away) is faster / immediate, whereas hormonal response (adrenaline) is slower (1)\n• nervous response is short-lived, whereas the hormonal effect lasts longer / several minutes (1)\n• nervous impulses travel along neurones whereas hormones are carried in the blood (1)\n• nervous response is localised / to specific muscles, whereas hormones can have widespread effects / act on many target organs (1)\n• nervous transmission is electrical (and chemical at synapses), hormonal is only chemical (1)\nComparative statements needed for each mark` },
      { q: `A scientist placed two recording electrodes 45 mm apart on a myelinated axon from a frog's sciatic nerve. An action potential was recorded at the second electrode 0.60 ms after it was recorded at the first electrode.\n\nCalculate the conduction velocity of the impulse in m s⁻¹.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `conversion: 45 mm = 0.045 m and 0.60 ms = 0.0006 s / velocity = distance ÷ time (1)\n75 (m s⁻¹) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks\nAccept: 75 mm ms⁻¹ for 1 mark only if units not converted` },
      { q: `A photomicrograph of a transverse section through a spinal nerve was printed. The diameter of one myelinated axon, including its myelin sheath, measured 18 mm in the image. The actual diameter of the axon was 12 µm.\n\nCalculate the magnification of the image.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `18 mm = 18 000 µm / magnification = image size ÷ actual size (1)\n× 1500 (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks\nDo not accept: × 1.5 (units not converted)` },
      { q: `In a mammalian motor neurone, the minimum time between the start of one action potential and the start of the next (the refractory period) was measured as 2.5 ms (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the maximum frequency of action potentials that this neurone could transmit, in Hz.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 ÷ 0.0025 s / 1000 ÷ 2.5 (1)\n400 (Hz) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks` },
      { q: `Oat coleoptiles were placed in unilateral light. Group A coleoptiles were left intact. Group B coleoptiles had their tips removed. After 3 hours, group A had bent towards the light and group B had not bent.\n\nExplain why the coleoptiles in group B did not bend.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the tip is the site of IAA / auxin production / tip contains the photoreceptor that detects (direction of) light (1)\nno IAA moved to the shaded side / no IAA moved down, so no (unequal) cell elongation (1)\nAccept: auxin for IAA\nIgnore: references to growth hormone without naming IAA/auxin for the first mark only if second marking point given` },
      { q: `An optician shines a bright light into a patient's left eye. The pupil of the left eye becomes smaller.\n\nDescribe the role of the iris muscles in causing this response.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `circular muscles (of the iris) contract (1)\nradial muscles (of the iris) relax (1)\nAccept: sphincter muscles for circular, dilator muscles for radial\nIgnore: references to the autonomic nervous system` },
      { q: `In the human retina, several rod cells synapse with a single bipolar neurone. In the fovea, each cone cell usually synapses with its own bipolar neurone.\n\nExplain why vision using rod cells has low visual acuity.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `many rods connect to one bipolar neurone / retinal convergence (1)\nso the brain cannot distinguish which rod was stimulated / light falling on separate rods gives only one impulse / one image (1)\nAccept: two points of light close together are seen as one` },
      { q: `A student plans to investigate whether garden snails habituate to being touched. She will touch each snail on the head ten times and time how long the tentacles take to re-emerge after each touch.\n\nGive two variables, other than the species of snail, that she should control in this investigation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• the time interval between touches (1)\n• the force / pressure of the touch / same cotton bud / same object (1)\n• the position on the snail's head that is touched (1)\n• temperature (1)\n• light intensity (1)\n• time snails are left to settle / acclimatise before starting (1)\n• humidity / moisture of the surface (1)\n• size / age of snail (1)\nIgnore: amount / volume of snail` },
      { q: `Alpha-1-antitrypsin (AAT) is a human protein used to treat people with an inherited form of emphysema. Transgenic sheep have been produced that secrete human AAT in their milk.\n\nGive two ethical objections to using transgenic sheep to produce this drug.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from:\n• possible harm / suffering to the sheep (e.g. from the procedures / health problems in transgenic animals) (1)\n• animals are being used as a commodity / animal rights / not respecting the animal's interests (1)\n• it is unnatural / interfering with nature / 'playing God' / crossing species boundaries (1)\n• many embryos / animals die or are wasted in producing one transgenic animal (1)\n• religious objection to mixing human and animal genes (1)\nIgnore: cost\nDo not accept: risk of drug being unsafe for patients (this is a safety, not ethical, objection)` },
      { q: `The enzyme CYP2D6 converts the painkiller codeine into morphine in the liver. Different alleles of the CYP2D6 gene produce enzymes with different activities. Some people are 'ultra-rapid metabolisers' and others are 'poor metabolisers'.\n\nExplain how information from the Human Genome Project could be used to improve the treatment of pain with codeine.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `patients could be screened / genotyped for CYP2D6 alleles before prescribing / personalised medicine (1)\nso the dose could be adjusted / an alternative drug given, avoiding overdose / toxic effects in ultra-rapid metabolisers or lack of pain relief in poor metabolisers (1)` },
      { q: `A baby is born with a cataract that makes the lens of her right eye cloudy. Doctors advise that the cataract should be removed in the first few weeks of life rather than waiting until she is older.\n\nSuggest why the cataract should be removed as early as possible.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `there is a critical window / period for visual development (early in life) (1)\nvisual cortex neurones / synapses (for the right eye) only develop / are retained if they receive stimulation (during this time) / otherwise the right eye's connections are lost and the left eye takes over the visual cortex (1)\nAccept: permanent loss of vision in right eye if delayed` },
      { q: `The resting potential across the membrane of a squid giant axon is about −70 mV.\n\nExplain how this resting potential is maintained.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `sodium–potassium pump / Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase actively transports 3 Na⁺ out for every 2 K⁺ in (1)\nmembrane is more permeable to K⁺ (than Na⁺) / more K⁺ channels open / Na⁺ channels closed (1)\nso K⁺ diffuses out (down its concentration gradient), making the inside negative relative to the outside (1)\nAccept: negatively charged proteins / anions inside the axon for third mark\nDo not accept: Na⁺ pumped out and K⁺ pumped in without reference to ratio or active transport for MP1` },
      { q: `Multiple sclerosis is a disease in which the myelin sheath around axons in the central nervous system is gradually destroyed. People with multiple sclerosis may experience muscle weakness and delayed responses.\n\nExplain why destruction of the myelin sheath slows the transmission of impulses.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `myelin is an (electrical) insulator / prevents ion movement across the membrane (1)\nin myelinated axons depolarisation occurs only at the nodes of Ranvier / saltatory conduction (1)\nwithout myelin, depolarisation must occur along the whole length of the axon / local circuits are shorter / impulse cannot jump from node to node (1)\nAccept: more of the membrane must be depolarised` },
      { q: `Organophosphate insecticides inhibit the enzyme acetylcholinesterase at cholinergic synapses. Farm workers who are accidentally exposed to organophosphates may suffer muscle spasms.\n\nExplain why exposure to organophosphates causes muscle spasms.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `acetylcholine is not broken down / hydrolysed (in the synaptic cleft) (1)\nacetylcholine remains bound to receptors on the postsynaptic membrane / Na⁺ channels stay open (1)\nso the postsynaptic membrane / muscle fibre is repeatedly / continuously depolarised / action potentials continue to be generated (1)\nAccept: muscle cannot relax` },
      { q: `MDMA ('ecstasy') is a recreational drug. It binds to the serotonin transporter proteins in the presynaptic membrane and can cause these transporters to work in reverse. Users report raised mood.\n\nExplain how MDMA increases the concentration of serotonin in the synaptic cleft and how this leads to its effect.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `MDMA inhibits / blocks the reuptake of serotonin into the presynaptic neurone (1)\nMDMA causes (more) serotonin to be released / transported out of the presynaptic neurone (1)\nso more serotonin binds to receptors on the postsynaptic membrane / postsynaptic neurones are (repeatedly) depolarised / more action potentials in neurones of the brain associated with mood (1)\nAccept: 5-HT for serotonin` },
      { q: `Parkinson's disease is treated with L-dopa, a precursor molecule that is converted into dopamine by an enzyme in neurones. Dopamine itself cannot cross the blood–brain barrier, but L-dopa can.\n\nExplain why L-dopa, rather than dopamine, is given to patients and how it reduces the symptoms of Parkinson's disease.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `dopamine given as a drug would not reach the brain / cannot cross the blood–brain barrier (1)\nL-dopa enters the brain and is converted into dopamine (by surviving neurones) / increases dopamine concentration in the brain (1)\nso more dopamine binds to receptors (on postsynaptic neurones) / more impulses in the motor pathways, so improving control of movement / reducing tremor (1)` },
      { q: `When the siphon of the sea slug Aplysia is gently touched, the gill is withdrawn. If the siphon is touched repeatedly, the gill withdrawal reflex becomes weaker. This is habituation.\n\nExplain how habituation occurs at the synapse between the sensory neurone and the motor neurone.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(with repeated stimulation) calcium ion channels in the presynaptic membrane become less responsive / fewer open (1)\nso less Ca²⁺ enters the presynaptic neurone / knob (1)\nfewer vesicles fuse with the presynaptic membrane / less neurotransmitter released, so fewer action potentials / less depolarisation in the motor neurone (1)\nDo not accept: neurotransmitter runs out / is used up` },
      { q: `A student investigated habituation in garden snails. The table shows the mean time taken for the tentacles of 10 snails to re-emerge after the first touch and after the tenth touch (data written for this practice question).\n\nTouch number | Mean time for tentacles to re-emerge / s\n1 | 14.2\n10 | 4.6\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in mean time between touch 1 and touch 10, and suggest why this response is advantageous to the snail.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(14.2 − 4.6) ÷ 14.2 × 100 (1)\n67.6 (%) (1)\nAccept: 68 / 67.61\nSuggestion: the snail does not waste time / energy responding to a harmless / non-threatening (repeated) stimulus / can continue feeding / moving (1)` },
      { q: `Spinach is a long-day plant. It flowers only when the length of continuous darkness is shorter than a critical value. In summer, nights are short.\n\nExplain how phytochrome allows spinach plants to flower in summer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Pr is converted to Pfr in daylight / red light (1)\nPfr is (slowly) converted back to Pr in darkness (1)\nshort nights mean Pfr accumulates / is not all converted back to Pr, and (high concentration of) Pfr activates / promotes flowering (genes) (1)\nAccept: Pfr acts as / activates a transcription factor` },
      { q: `A patient has symptoms that suggest a brain tumour. A doctor must decide whether to request a CT scan or an MRI scan.\n\nCompare the use of CT and MRI to investigate this patient's brain.`, marks: 3, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `Any three from:\n• CT uses (thin beams of) X-rays, MRI uses magnetic fields and radio waves (1)\n• CT exposes the patient to ionising radiation / X-rays, MRI does not (1)\n• MRI gives better resolution / distinguishes between soft tissues more clearly / shows smaller tumours (1)\n• both produce cross-sectional / 3D images of brain structure (1)\n• CT is quicker / cheaper / can be used for patients with metal implants or pacemakers, MRI cannot (1)\nComparative statements needed` },
      { q: `Researchers studied the reading ability of pairs of twins raised together. The correlation coefficient between the reading test scores of the two twins in each pair was calculated (data written for this practice question).\n\nType of twins | Correlation coefficient for reading scores\nIdentical (monozygotic) | 0.78\nNon-identical (dizygotic) | 0.45\n\nDeduce what these results suggest about the roles of nature and nurture in reading ability.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `identical twins share (all / 100%) their genes, non-identical share (about) 50% (1)\nboth types of twin raised together share a similar environment (1)\nhigher correlation in identical twins suggests genes / nature have a significant role, but correlation less than 1 suggests the environment / nurture also has a role (1)` },
      { q: `A motor neurone in the spinal cord receives synapses from many presynaptic neurones. Some of these release excitatory neurotransmitters and others release inhibitory neurotransmitters that cause chloride ions to enter the postsynaptic neurone.\n\nExplain how these synapses allow the motor neurone to integrate information.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `inhibitory synapses cause hyperpolarisation / make the inside more negative / IPSP (1)\neffects of excitatory and inhibitory synapses are summed (spatial summation) / several presynaptic neurones together needed to reach threshold (1)\naction potential only generated if threshold is reached at the axon hillock / temporal summation of repeated impulses from one neurone can also reach threshold (1)` },
      { q: `Acetylcholine is the neurotransmitter at the synapses between motor neurones in the spinal cord and many other neurones.\n\nDescribe the events that occur from the arrival of an action potential at the presynaptic knob to the generation of an action potential in the postsynaptic neurone.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `depolarisation of presynaptic membrane causes (voltage-gated) calcium ion channels to open and Ca²⁺ enters (1)\nvesicles (containing acetylcholine) move to and fuse with the presynaptic membrane / exocytosis (1)\nacetylcholine diffuses across the synaptic cleft and binds to receptors on the postsynaptic membrane (1)\n(ligand-gated) sodium ion channels open, Na⁺ enters, postsynaptic membrane depolarised, threshold reached (1)` },
      { q: `The membrane potential of a neurone was recorded during an action potential. A description of the trace is given (data written for this practice question).\n\n• 0 ms: −70 mV\n• 0 to 1.0 ms: rises rapidly to +40 mV\n• 1.0 to 2.0 ms: falls rapidly to −80 mV\n• 2.0 to 4.0 ms: rises slowly back to −70 mV\n\nExplain the changes in membrane potential between 0 and 4.0 ms.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `0–1.0 ms: (voltage-gated) sodium ion channels open, Na⁺ diffuses in, depolarisation (1)\n1.0–2.0 ms: sodium channels close and (voltage-gated) potassium ion channels open (1)\nK⁺ diffuses out, repolarisation (1)\nfall to −80 mV / hyperpolarisation because K⁺ channels are slow to close / too many K⁺ leave; return to −70 mV as K⁺ channels close and Na⁺/K⁺ pump restores resting potential (1)` },
      { q: `In darkness, rod cells are depolarised and continuously release glutamate, which inhibits the bipolar neurones they synapse with.\n\nExplain how light falling on a rod cell leads to an action potential in a ganglion cell.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `light causes rhodopsin to break down into retinal and opsin / bleaching / retinal changes from cis to trans (1)\nopsin / this causes sodium ion channels (in the rod) to close, so the rod becomes hyperpolarised (1)\nrod stops releasing glutamate / less glutamate released, so bipolar neurone is no longer inhibited and becomes depolarised (1)\nbipolar neurone releases neurotransmitter which depolarises the ganglion cell, threshold reached, action potential travels along optic nerve (1)` },
      { q: `Hubel and Wiesel recorded which eye stimulated individual neurones in the visual cortex of cats. The table shows results similar to theirs for 200 neurones in each group (data written for this practice question).\n\nGroup | Respond to right eye only | Respond to left eye only | Respond to both eyes\nNormal kittens | 34 | 38 | 128\nKittens with right eye closed from birth to 3 months | 0 | 182 | 18\nAdult cats with right eye closed for 3 months | 36 | 40 | 124\n\nCalculate the percentage of neurones that responded to the left eye only in kittens with the right eye closed, and explain the differences between the three groups.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `91 (%) (1)\nin kittens with the right eye closed, no neurones respond to the right eye only / far more respond to the left eye only than in normal kittens (1)\nduring the critical window / early in life, axons from the active (left) eye take over / form synapses with the cortex neurones of the deprived eye / unstimulated synapses are lost (1)\nadult cats show no (significant) change from normal because the critical window has passed / connections already established (1)` },
      { q: `Taliglucerase alfa is an enzyme used to treat Gaucher disease. It is produced commercially in genetically modified carrot cells grown in culture. Some transgenic crop plants have been produced using the bacterium Agrobacterium tumefaciens.\n\nDescribe how a gene for a human protein could be inserted into plant cells using Agrobacterium tumefaciens.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Ti plasmid is removed from Agrobacterium and cut using a restriction enzyme (1)\nhuman gene is cut using the same restriction enzyme / has complementary sticky ends (1)\ngene inserted into plasmid / joined using DNA ligase to form recombinant plasmid (1)\nplasmid returned to Agrobacterium which infects plant cells / inserts the gene into plant DNA (1)\nAccept: use of a marker gene (e.g. antibiotic resistance) to select transformed cells as an alternative to any one point` },
      { q: `HLA-B*5701 is an allele associated with a hypersensitivity reaction to abacavir, a drug used to treat HIV. In a study of 1200 patients treated with abacavir, 66 carried HLA-B*5701. Of these, 38 had a hypersensitivity reaction. Of the patients who did not carry the allele, 9 had a hypersensitivity reaction (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate how many times more likely a carrier of HLA-B*5701 was to have a hypersensitivity reaction than a non-carrier, and comment on the implications for personalised medicine.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `non-carriers = 1200 − 66 = 1134 and risk 9 ÷ 1134 = 0.0079 / 0.79% and carriers 38 ÷ 66 = 0.58 / 57.6% (1)\n72.5 / 73 times (1)\nAccept: 72.6 / 72\nComment, any two from:\n• patients could be genotyped / screened for HLA-B*5701 before prescribing (1)\n• carriers given an alternative drug, avoiding adverse reactions (1)\n• some non-carriers still react, so screening does not remove all risk / other factors involved (1)\n• ethical concerns: storage / confidentiality of genetic data / cost of testing (1)` },
      { q: `A student wants to investigate whether giant African land snails habituate to a repeated touch stimulus, and whether the rate of habituation is affected by temperature.\n\nDevise an investigation the student could carry out to test whether snails habituate to being touched.`, marks: 5, command: 'Devise', markScheme: `Any five from:\n• leave snail to acclimatise / settle on a clean, damp surface for a set time (e.g. 2 minutes) until tentacles fully extended (1)\n• touch the snail on the head / between the tentacles with a cotton bud (1)\n• use a stopwatch to time how long the tentacles take to fully re-emerge (1)\n• repeat the touch at fixed intervals (e.g. every 1 minute / immediately after re-emergence) for a set number of touches (e.g. 10) (1)\n• control: same pressure / same cotton bud / same position touched / temperature / light intensity (at least two needed) (1)\n• use several snails / repeat with at least 5 snails and calculate a mean for each touch number (1)\n• plot mean time against touch number; habituation shown by decreasing time (1)\n• ethics: handle snails gently / return to habitat / minimise stress / wash hands (1)` },
      { q: `Tips cut from oat coleoptiles were placed on agar blocks and exposed to light from one side for 2 hours. A thin sheet of mica divided each block into a lit half and a shaded half. Each half-block was then placed on one side of a decapitated coleoptile kept in the dark. The angle of curvature was measured after 2 hours (data written for this practice question).\n\nAgar block | Mean angle of curvature / °\nShaded half | 16.2\nLit half | 9.8\nPlain agar (no tip) | 0.4\n\nCalculate the mean rate of curvature produced by the shaded-half blocks, in degrees per hour, and explain the results.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `8.1 (° h⁻¹) (1)\nIAA / auxin diffuses from the tip into the agar (1)\nmore IAA moved to the shaded side of the tip / IAA redistributed (laterally) away from the light, so more in the shaded half-block (1)\nIAA causes cell elongation in the cells beneath the block, so the coleoptile bends away from the side with the block; greater curvature means more IAA (1)\nplain agar is a control showing that agar alone does not cause curvature (1)\nAccept: IAA causes H⁺ to be pumped into cell walls, loosening them, for elongation` },
      { q: `A student compared habituation in two groups of 10 garden snails, one kept in bright light and one in dim light. She recorded the time for tentacles to re-emerge after the tenth touch (data written for this practice question).\n\nGroup | Mean time / s | Standard deviation / s\nBright light | 6.8 | 1.9\nDim light | 4.9 | 1.6\n\nt = (x̄₁ − x̄₂) ÷ √(s₁²/n₁ + s₂²/n₂)\nDegrees of freedom = n₁ + n₂ − 2\nCritical values of t at 18 degrees of freedom: p = 0.05, 2.10; p = 0.01, 2.88\n\nCalculate the value of t and state what it shows about the null hypothesis that there is no difference in the mean times.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `s²/n values: 3.61 ÷ 10 = 0.361 and 2.56 ÷ 10 = 0.256 (1)\n√0.617 = 0.785 (1)\nt = 1.9 ÷ 0.785 = 2.42 (1)\nAccept: 2.4\n18 degrees of freedom; t is greater than the critical value of 2.10 (at p = 0.05) but less than 2.88 (1)\nso reject the null hypothesis (at p = 0.05) / the difference is significant / less than 5% probability the difference is due to chance (1)\nTE from incorrect t value for last two marks` },
      { q: `*Mammals have both myelinated and unmyelinated neurones. Impulses in myelinated motor neurones travel at up to 120 m s⁻¹, whereas impulses in unmyelinated neurones of the same diameter travel at about 2 m s⁻¹.\n\nExplain how an impulse is propagated along a myelinated axon and why it travels faster than along an unmyelinated axon.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 depolarisation at one region: Na⁺ channels open and Na⁺ enters the axon\nIC2 local circuits: Na⁺ ions diffuse sideways / along the inside of the axon to the adjacent region\nIC3 this depolarises the next region to threshold, opening voltage-gated Na⁺ channels\nIC4 the region behind is refractory, so the impulse travels in one direction only\nIC5 myelin (Schwann cells) insulates the axon; depolarisation only at nodes of Ranvier\nIC6 saltatory conduction: impulse jumps node to node, local circuits longer, fewer depolarisations needed, so faster than continuous depolarisation in unmyelinated axon\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 IC → 4; 5–4 IC → 3; 3–2 IC → 2; 1 IC → 1\nReasoning: 2 = sustained, fully linked explanation linking ion movement to propagation and myelin to speed; 1 = some linkage; 0 = isolated points` },
      { q: `*Drugs such as insulin, human growth hormone and alpha-1-antitrypsin are now produced by genetically modified bacteria, plants and animals. Some people support this technology; others are concerned about it.\n\nDiscuss the benefits and risks of using genetically modified organisms to produce drugs.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 benefit: large quantities produced / cheaper / reliable supply (e.g. insulin from bacteria)\nIC2 benefit: human protein, so fewer immune / allergic reactions than animal-derived drugs\nIC3 benefit: avoids risk of transmitting disease from donor tissue / ethical issues of extracting from humans or animals\nIC4 risk: GM organisms / genes could escape into the environment / horizontal gene transfer (e.g. antibiotic resistance marker genes to other bacteria)\nIC5 risk: welfare of transgenic animals / unnatural; GM crop pollen could cross-pollinate wild relatives or contaminate food crops\nIC6 risk: long-term effects unknown / drug may be contaminated / patents and cost limit access in poorer countries\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 IC → 4; 5–4 IC → 3; 3–2 IC → 2; 1 IC → 1\nReasoning: 2 = balanced discussion of both benefits and risks, each linked to a reason; 1 = some linkage or one-sided; 0 = isolated points` },
      { q: `*Scientists use several types of evidence to investigate whether brain development is determined by genes (nature) or by the environment (nurture). These include studies of newborn babies, twin studies, cross-cultural studies, studies of people with brain damage and experiments on animals such as kittens.\n\nDiscuss how these types of evidence help scientists to decide on the relative importance of nature and nurture in brain development.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIC1 newborn studies: abilities present at birth (little environmental influence) suggest nature, e.g. recognising faces\nIC2 twin studies: identical twins share all genes; identical twins raised apart with similar traits suggest nature; differences suggest nurture\nIC3 cross-cultural studies: differences between cultures suggest nurture; similarities across cultures suggest nature, e.g. perception of visual illusions\nIC4 brain-damaged individuals: recovery of functions shows brain plasticity / nurture; loss of a function shows region is required\nIC5 animal experiments, e.g. Hubel and Wiesel: visual cortex needs stimulation in a critical window, so environment needed for development of genetically programmed structure\nIC6 limitations: small samples / difficult to separate genes and environment / animal results may not apply to humans / ethical issues; conclusion that both interact\nMarking: up to 4 marks for indicative content and up to 2 for linkages and sustained lines of reasoning\nIndicative content: 6 IC → 4; 5–4 IC → 3; 3–2 IC → 2; 1 IC → 1\nReasoning: 2 = sustained reasoning linking each type of evidence to nature or nurture with limitations; 1 = some linkage; 0 = isolated points` }
    ]
  }
};

if (typeof module !== 'undefined') module.exports = { BIOLOGY_EDEXCEL_A_PRACTICE };
